; TYPED SYMBOLIC CODE
; language specification, working draft
;
; Sections 0 to 2 say how this file and the language are written down.
; Sections 3 to 9 specify the language. Section 10 holds complete example
; programs, and section 11 lists everything that is still undecided.

;;;; 0 how to read this file

;;; 0.1 headers and numbering
; 1. A line starting with four semicolons starts a section, three start an
;    entry, and two start a sub-entry. In VS Code, each header folds up to
;    the next header of the same or a higher level.
; 2. Rules are numbered inside the innermost header that holds them: rule 3
;    of entry 5.5 is 5.5.3, and rule 1 of sub-entry 4.4.2 is 4.4.2.1.
; 3. An entry that has sub-entries keeps all of its rules in them.
; 4. A number in parentheses, such as (2.4.2), points at an entry or a rule.
; 5. (todo) after a header marks an entry that is not designed yet.

;;; 0.2 grammar notation
; General forms mix real syntax with placeholders.
; 1. <Name> is a syntactic category (2.5). Some categories take a type:
;    <Value T> is a value of type T. A category written without a type, such
;    as <Value>, allows any type.
; 2. Inside <...>, single capitals stand for types: T, U and E for any type,
;    F for a function type. They are placeholders in this file, not type
;    parameters of the language like the T in [Option T].
; 3. X... means zero or more X. X X... means one or more.
; 4. X? means X may be left out.
; 5. Everything else is literal syntax, including [ ], | | and ->.
; 6. -> T written after a complete form gives the type of the whole form. It
;    is not part of the syntax: (do) -> Unit means (do) is a Unit value.

;;; 0.3 example annotations
; 1. Examples are fragments. A define, struct, enum, trait or impl example
;    sits at the top level. Any other example reads as part of the body of
;    main (9.1).
;    Earlier examples in the same entry are in scope.
;    The examples of an entry that are not top level read as items of one
;    do, which is main's body. A bare let example is therefore a do item,
;    not a top-level let (4.3.3).
;    Warnings that come only from an example being a fragment are not
;    shown: a binding that no later example reads (4.1.10), or a
;    declaration that ends the fragment (2.5.1.3).
;    A define, struct, enum or trait example has no doc unless the entry is
;    about docs, and the warning for a missing doc (4.6.10.1) is not shown
;    either. A doc that is there must still be complete.
; 2. A trailing comment states the outcome:
;      returns X     the example evaluates to X
;      type: T       the example has type T
;      name : T      the example binds name with type T
;      prints: X     the example writes X to the console
;      warning: ...  the example compiles, with this warning
;      error: ...    the example does not compile
;      panics: ...   the example compiles, then stops at run time (8.9)
; 3. A tag such as [5.5.3] at the end of the comment names the rule the
;    example exercises.
; 4. A comment starting with hypothetical: marks an example that relies on
;    something this file does not define yet. Each one is listed in 11.9.
; 5. The operators and the other built-in functions of section 8 are methods
;    of traits (7.5) that will be defined in the prelude (8.1.3, 11.8.5).
;    An example that only calls them is not marked. An example that names
;    a prelude trait, such as Add in a bound or an impl, is marked
;    hypothetical.
; 6. In an example, ... inside an impl or a fn stands for entries or a body
;    that the example leaves out, where only the header or the signature
;    matters.

;;; 0.4 reasons
; 1. A line starting with why: gives a reason the author stated.
; 2. A line starting with why?: gives a reason added during the rewrite and
;    not yet confirmed. Confirm it by deleting the ?, or delete the line.

;;;; 1 lexical rules

;;; 1.1 comments
; 1. A comment starts with ; and runs to the end of the line. The compiler
;    ignores it, unless it is a doc comment (rule 3) or a lint command
;    (rule 4).
; 2. The number of semicolons at the start of a comment sets its level.
;    Levels are for readers and editors, for headers and folding. They never
;    change meaning.
; 3. A comment starting with exactly ;: is a doc comment. One starting with
;    ;;: is an ordinary comment. What a doc comment attaches to, and what it
;    must say, is in 4.6.
; 4. A comment starting with exactly ;! is a lint command for the item
;    below it, and one starting with exactly ;!! is a lint command for the
;    whole module. One starting with ;;! is an ordinary comment. What a
;    lint command attaches to, and what it may say, is in 4.7.

(+ 1 2)  ; a comment after code  [1.1.1]
;: The answer to everything.
(define ANSWER : u32 = 42)  ; a doc comment, attached to the define below it  [1.1.3]
;;: an ordinary comment, not a doc  [1.1.3]
;: Half of the answer.
;! allow naming
(define half_answer : u32 = 21)  ; a lint command, after the doc and above the define  [1.1.4]
;;! an ordinary comment, not a lint command  [1.1.4]
; this is a level-one comment; at the start of a line, ;; begins a level-two
; comment, which this file uses as a sub-entry header  [1.1.2]

;;; 1.2 tokens and whitespace
; 1. Whitespace separates tokens. It is required only where leaving it out
;    would change the tokens (rule 9). Everywhere else it is optional, and
;    how much to use is a matter of layout (1.5).
; 2. -> is always a token of its own, even between letters.
; 3. A token that starts with - followed by a digit is a number literal. A
;    literal's type suffix (1.3.1.6) is part of the same token.
; 4. A line break is whitespace, except that it ends a comment.
; 5. ^ is always a token of its own. It is written directly before the name
;    it pins (6.8), with no whitespace between them.
;    why?: ^name then reads as one unit, like -5
; 6. - is never part of an identifier. Outside a number literal (rule 3)
;    and -> (rule 2), it is always a token of its own, even between letters.
;    why?: x-1 then has one reading, x followed by the literal -1
; 7. The lexer always takes the longest token it can (maximal munch). The
;    symbol tokens are a fixed set:
;      ->  =>  ==  <=  >=  ::  ..  .  :  =  @  ^  _  +  -  *  /  %  <  >
;    together with the delimiters (2.4). Any other run of symbols is read as
;    several of these tokens, or is an error. A . belongs to a number only
;    with a digit after it (1.3.1.3), so 1..3 is 1, .. and 3.
;    why?: whitespace can then be left out almost everywhere, and since
;    every token is known in advance, an error can name the token that was
;    probably meant
;    Macros (11.8.4), when they come, will have to work within this set.
;    They cannot add symbol tokens of their own.
; 8. For spacing, each token falls into one of four categories. These are
;    separate from the token classes of 2.1.
;      brackets    ( ) [ ] { } | |
;      words       keywords, special forms, identifiers, literals, _, and
;                  the operators + - * / % == < > <= >=
;      separators  -> = => @ :, and .. in a pattern (6.5)
;      joiners     . :: ^, and .. before a name (4.4.4)
;    Layout (1.5) sets the spacing between the categories.
; 9. Whitespace is required between two tokens when writing them together
;    would lex as different tokens by rule 7. With the fixed set, this
;    happens:
;    a. between two words: (+ x y), (list _ _ ..)
;    b. between = and a following ==, > or >=: = > written together reads
;       as =>
;    c. between < or > and a following ==: < == written together reads as
;       <= =
;    d. between - and a following > or >=: - > written together reads as ->
;    (- 1) and (-1) differ by rule 3: the first calls - with one argument,
;    which is an error (8.2.2), and the second is a literal. That is a
;    different meaning, not a missing space.
; 10. Whitespace around . is an error, as it is after ^ (rule 5). Whitespace
;     around :: or after a .. before a name is a warning (lint spacing,
;     4.7.3).
;     why?: otherwise (f a. b) would quietly read a field instead of passing
;     two values

(+ x y)              ; same as ( + x y )                                  [1.2.1]
|x: u32|             ; same as |x : u32|; warning: layout (1.5)           [1.2.1]
first_or[i32]        ; same as first_or [i32] (7.4); warning: layout (1.5)  [1.2.1]
string->u32          ; three tokens: string -> u32; warning: layout (1.5)  [1.2.2]
-5                   ; one token, a literal                               [1.2.3]
-5_i8                ; one token, a literal with a suffix                 [1.2.3]
(- 5)                ; a call of - on 5; error: - takes two arguments (8.2.2)  [1.2.3]
(+ 1
    2)               ; the line break is just whitespace; returns 3       [1.2.4]
^LUCKY               ; two tokens: ^ and LUCKY                            [1.2.5]
^ LUCKY              ; error: ^ is written directly before the name       [1.2.5]
x-1                  ; two tokens: x and the literal -1; warning: layout (1.5)  [1.2.6]
first-name           ; three tokens: first, - and name; warning: layout (1.5)  [1.2.6]
(let x=-1)           ; same as (let x = -1); warning: layout (1.5)        [1.2.7]
(let add:[i32 i32 -> i32]=+)
                     ; same as (let add : [i32 i32 -> i32] = +); warning: layout (1.5)  [1.2.7]
(let numbers : [List i32] = (list 1 2 3))
(match numbers
    l@(list 1..3)=>l.0
    _=>0)            ; same as l @ (list 1 .. 3) => l.0 and _ => 0; warning: layout (1.5)  [1.2.7]
(let gt : [i32 i32 -> bool] =>)
                     ; error: => is one token; write = >                  [1.2.9]
; with jane : Employee, and (mod geometry::shapes::circle)
jane . dept          ; error: no whitespace around .                      [1.2.10]
circle :: Shape      ; warning: no whitespace around ::                   [1.2.10]
(Employee .. jane)   ; same as (Employee ..jane); warning: no whitespace after ..  [1.2.10]

;;; 1.3 literals

;; 1.3.1 numbers
; 1. A decimal integer literal is one or more digits, optionally preceded
;    by -.
; 2. A radix integer literal is 0x followed by hex digits (0 to 9, a to f,
;    A to F), 0o followed by octal digits (0 to 7), or 0b followed by binary
;    digits (0 and 1), optionally preceded by -. The prefix is lowercase. A
;    radix literal is always an integer literal.
; 3. A float literal is digits, a ., and more digits, optionally preceded by
;    -. A . needs digits on both sides, so 5. and .5 are errors.
;    why?: . already joins names and reads list elements (2.3), so a . with
;    a digit on one side only would have two readings
; 4. A decimal literal may end in an exponent: e, an optional + or -, and
;    one or more digits. A literal with an exponent is a float literal, even
;    without a .: 1e10 is a float. A radix literal has no exponent, since e
;    is a hex digit.
; 5. A single _ between two digits separates them and is ignored: 1_000.
;    This holds in every run of digits, including radix digits, the part
;    after the . and the exponent. A _ that is not between two digits is an
;    error, except before a type suffix (rule 6).
; 6. A number literal may end in a type suffix: _ followed by the name of a
;    numerical type (3.1.2), with no space: 42_u8, 2.5_f32. The _ is
;    required. The suffix gives the literal its type. Without a suffix, the
;    type comes from where the literal is used (5.1.1).
; 7. An integer suffix (u8 to u256, ubig, usize, i8 to i256, ibig, isize)
;    only follows an integer literal. A float suffix (f32 to f256) follows
;    any number literal, and makes an integer literal a float: 0_f32 is 0.0
;    as an f32.
; 8. When a radix literal ends in _ and a suffix name, the suffix wins over
;    the hex digits: 0x1_f32 is 1 as an f32, not 0x1F32. Write 0x1F32 or
;    0x1F_32 for the hex number.
; 9. A literal whose value does not fit its type is an error, whether the
;    type comes from a suffix or from where the literal is used (5.1.1). A
;    radix literal is a value, not a bit pattern: 0xFF_i8 is 255, which does
;    not fit in an i8. A float literal, or an integer literal with a float
;    suffix, is rounded to the nearest value of its type, and is an error
;    only when it is outside the type's range. It is rounded once, from its
;    exact decimal value: 0.1_f128 is the f128 nearest to one tenth, not the
;    f64 0.1 widened.
;    why?: a literal never silently means another number; wrap (8.6.4)
;    wraps on purpose
; 10. A decimal integer, or the part of a float before the ., that starts
;     with 0 and has more digits after it is a warning (lint leading_zero,
;     4.7.3): 007. 0, 0.5, 0x0F and the exponent in 1e05 are not.
;     why: in C and several languages that copy it, 010 is octal for 8; the
;     warning catches numbers written with that habit. Octal is written
;     0o10.
; Refining these rules is open (11.5.12).

42           ; an integer literal                                  [1.3.1.1]
-7           ; an integer literal                                  [1.3.1.1]
0xFF         ; returns 255                                         [1.3.1.2]
0o17         ; returns 15                                          [1.3.1.2]
0b1010       ; returns 10                                          [1.3.1.2]
0XFF         ; error: the prefix is lowercase                      [1.3.1.2]
3.14         ; a float literal                                     [1.3.1.3]
5.           ; error: a . needs digits on both sides               [1.3.1.3]
.5           ; error: a . needs digits on both sides; write 0.5    [1.3.1.3]
1e10         ; type: f64; an exponent makes a float                [1.3.1.4]
2.5e-3       ; returns 0.0025                                      [1.3.1.4]
1e+3         ; returns 1000.0                                      [1.3.1.4]
0x1E10       ; returns 7696; E is a hex digit, not an exponent     [1.3.1.4]
1_000_000    ; returns 1000000                                     [1.3.1.5]
0xFF_FF      ; returns 65535                                       [1.3.1.5]
1__000       ; error: a _ must sit between two digits              [1.3.1.5]
0x_FF        ; error: a _ must sit between two digits              [1.3.1.5]
42_u8        ; type: u8                                            [1.3.1.6]
-5_i8        ; type: i8                                            [1.3.1.6]
2.5_f32      ; type: f32                                           [1.3.1.6]
1_000_u32    ; type: u32                                           [1.3.1.5, 1.3.1.6]
42u8         ; error: a suffix starts with _; write 42_u8          [1.3.1.6]
0_f32        ; type: f32; returns 0.0                              [1.3.1.7]
0xFF_u8      ; type: u8; returns 255                               [1.3.1.7]
1e10_f32     ; type: f32                                           [1.3.1.7]
2.5_u8       ; error: an integer suffix on a float literal         [1.3.1.7]
1e10_u32     ; error: 1e10 is a float literal                      [1.3.1.7]
42_u128      ; type: u128                                          [1.3.1.7]
2.5_f256     ; type: f256                                          [1.3.1.7]
2.5_ibig     ; error: an integer suffix on a float literal         [1.3.1.7]
42_usize     ; type: usize                                         [1.3.1.7]
0x1_f32      ; type: f32; returns 1.0                              [1.3.1.8]
0x1_f128     ; type: f128; returns 1.0, not 0x1F128                [1.3.1.8]
0x1F_32      ; returns 7986, which is 0x1F32                     [1.3.1.8]
0xFF_i8      ; error: 255 does not fit in an i8                    [1.3.1.9]
-0x80_i8     ; returns -128, an i8                                 [1.3.1.9]
300_u8       ; error: 300 does not fit in a u8                     [1.3.1.9]
-1_u8        ; error: a u8 cannot be negative                      [1.3.1.9]
-1_ubig      ; error: a ubig cannot be negative                    [1.3.1.9]
-1_usize     ; error: a usize cannot be negative                   [1.3.1.9]
(let x : u8 = 300)  ; error: 300 does not fit in a u8              [1.3.1.9]
0.1_f128     ; the f128 nearest to 0.1, not the f64 0.1 widened    [1.3.1.9]
007          ; warning: a leading 0; write 7, or 0o7 for octal     [1.3.1.10]
1e05         ; returns 100000.0; no warning in an exponent         [1.3.1.10]

;; 1.3.2 booleans and unit
; 1. true and false are the two bool literals.
; 2. () is the literal for the single value of Unit (3.2).

true  ; type: bool  [1.3.2.1]
()    ; type: Unit  [1.3.2.2]

;; 1.3.3 characters
; 1. A char literal is a single character between single quotes: 'c'.
; 2. Single quotes never delimit strings.
; 3. A char is one Unicode scalar value: a code point from U+0000 to
;    U+10FFFF, other than the surrogates U+D800 to U+DFFF. What looks like
;    one character can be several scalar values, such as e followed by a
;    combining accent. A char literal holding more than one is an error.
; 4. char is not a numerical type (3.1.2). (as [u32] c) gives the code
;    point of c, and (try_as [char] n) turns a u32 back into a char (8.6).
;    Whether chars can be compared with < is open (11.3.3).

'c'          ; type: char                                     [1.3.3.1]
'hello'      ; error: ' delimits a single char                [1.3.3.2]
'é'          ; type: char; one scalar value, U+00E9           [1.3.3.3]
'日'         ; type: char; one scalar value, U+65E5           [1.3.3.3]
'e\u{301}'   ; error: two scalar values, e and an accent      [1.3.3.3]
(+ 'a' 'b')  ; error: char is not numerical, so + has no impl for it (8.2)  [1.3.3.4]

;; 1.3.4 strings
; 1. A simple string is written between double quotes: "hello world!".
; 2. A fancy string is written between backticks: `hello ${name}`. It may
;    contain ' and " without escapes.
; 3. Inside a fancy string, ${<Identifier>} marks a parameter.
; 4. A fancy string is not a string. It is a function value (5.1.2).
; 5. A string is a sequence of chars (1.3.3.3), stored as UTF-8, and is
;    always valid UTF-8. Source files are UTF-8. Whether len and get count
;    bytes or chars is open (11.7.5).

"hello world!"        ; type: string                   [1.3.4.1]
`say "hi" and 'bye'`  ; ' and " need no escapes        [1.3.4.2]
`hello ${name}`       ; name is a parameter            [1.3.4.3]
`hello world!`        ; type: [-> string], not string  [1.3.4.4]
"日本"                ; type: string; two chars        [1.3.4.5]

;; 1.3.5 escapes
; 1. A backslash starts an escape. Simple strings, fancy strings and char
;    literals support the same escapes:
;      \n  newline         \t  tab           \r  carriage return
;      \0  null, U+0000    \\  backslash     \`  backtick
;      \$  dollar sign     \"  double quote  \'  single quote
;      \u{H}   the char with code point H, in hex (rule 2)
;      \xHH    the char with code point HH, in hex (rule 3)
; 2. \u{H} takes 1 to 6 hex digits between the braces. H must be a Unicode
;    scalar value (1.3.3.3).
; 3. \xHH takes exactly 2 hex digits, from 00 to 7F.
;    why?: a higher value is not a char by itself in UTF-8, only one byte
;    of a longer char (1.3.4.5); \u{H} writes those chars
; 4. \0 is never the start of a longer escape: "\01" is a null char
;    followed by 1.
; 5. Any other escape is an error.
;    why?: a mistyped escape is caught, and a new escape can be added later
;    without changing what existing code means
; 6. Every escape works in every kind of literal, even where it is not
;    needed, such as \$ in a simple string.
;    why: this keeps learning strings simple

`\nthis is a newline, and this is a backtick: \`!`  ; \n and \` in a fancy string  [1.3.5.1]
"she said \"hi\"\n"  ; \" and \n in a simple string                                [1.3.5.1]
'\''                 ; a char holding a single quote                               [1.3.5.1]
`costs \${price}`    ; \$ stops ${ from marking a parameter; type: [-> string]     [1.3.5.1]
"line\r\n"           ; \r\n is a Windows line break                                [1.3.5.1]
'\0'                 ; a null char                                                 [1.3.5.1]
'\u{E9}'             ; returns 'é'                                                 [1.3.5.2]
"\u{1F600}"          ; a string holding one char, an emoji                         [1.3.5.2]
'\u{D800}'           ; error: a surrogate is not a Unicode scalar value            [1.3.5.2]
'\u{}'               ; error: \u{} needs 1 to 6 hex digits                         [1.3.5.2]
"\x41"               ; returns "A"                                                 [1.3.5.3]
"\x80"               ; error: \x goes up to 7F; write \u{80}                       [1.3.5.3]
"\01"                ; two chars: a null and 1                                     [1.3.5.4]
"\q"                 ; error: \q is not an escape                                  [1.3.5.5]
"costs \$5"          ; returns "costs $5"; \$ works though it is not needed        [1.3.5.6]

;;; 1.4 naming conventions
; 1. PascalCase names types, enum variants, constructors and traits (7.5):
;    Employee, Weapon.Bow, Shape.
; 2. snake_case names values and functions: add_one, person_1.
; 3. SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE names constants. A constant is a define whose type
;    is a primitive type (3.1): PI, SOME_NUMBER.
; 4. Breaking 1 to 3 is a warning (lint naming, 4.7.3), not an error.

(struct Employee |name : string|)  ; a type                                           [1.4.1]
(define add_one = {fn |x : u32 -> u32| (+ x 1_u32)})  ; a function                    [1.4.2]
(define PI : f64 = 3.14)           ; an f64 constant                                  [1.4.3]
(define GREETING : string = "hi")  ; string is primitive                              [1.4.3]
(define pi : f64 = 3.14)           ; warning: constants are SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE      [1.4.4]
(struct employee |name : string|)  ; warning: types are PascalCase                    [1.4.4]
(define AddOne = {fn |x : u32 -> u32| (+ x 1_u32)})  ; warning: functions are snake_case  [1.4.4]

;;; 1.5 layout
; The recommended layout. Breaking it is a warning (lint layout, 4.7.3), and
; an editor can offer to fix it. Whitespace that changes the tokens is
; covered by 1.2.9.
; 1. Spacing between the categories of 1.2.8:
;      bracket, bracket      none between two openers or two closers: ((
;                            and )); one space between a closer and an
;                            opener: ) (
;      word, bracket         none inside the brackets: (+ and x); one space
;                            outside them: first_or [i32]
;      separator, bracket    none inside the brackets, one space outside
;                            them: [-> u32], -> [u32], |-> Unit|
;      word, separator       one space: x : u32, a = 1, l @ (list ..)
;      word, word            one space
;      joiner                no space on either side: jane.dept,
;                            circle::Shape, ^LUCKY, ..jane
; 2. Each level of nesting indents 4 spaces.
; 3. A form too long for one line keeps its head, and the items that fit,
;    on its first line. Each remaining item goes on a line of its own,
;    indented 4 more than the line the form opens on.
; 4. A form that opens a body stays at the end of its line, with the body
;    below it: {fn |...|, (do, (match subject, (loop |...|.
; 5. A form spread over lines puts one item on each line: each item of a
;    do, each arm of a match, each variant of an enum, each binding of a
;    constructor or a recur, each entry of a trait or an impl (7.5).
; 6. Closers stack at the end of the last line. A closer never starts a
;    line.
; 7. An arm too long for one line breaks after =>. Its value goes on the
;    next line, indented 4 more than the arm.
; 8. A trailing comment has at least two spaces before its ;. Trailing
;    comments on consecutive lines start in the same column.
; 9. Exceptions, which win over rule 1:
;    a. In a match spread over lines, patterns are padded so that every
;       arm's => sits in the same column.
;    b. In an enum spread over lines, variant names are padded so that each
;       variant's opening | sits in the same column.
;    c. Two spaces may separate the parameters of a signature:
;       |x : u32  f : [u32 -> u32]|.
;    d. In a struct's field list spread over lines, field names may be
;       padded so that every field's : sits in the same column. Either
;       every field has one space before its :, or every : lines up.
; 10. Every line of one doc (4.6.1) starts its ;: in the same column. For a
;     doc that trails a field or variant, the lines below it are indented
;     to the column of the first ;:. The text of an example (4.6.2.9) is
;     indented two spaces past the text of its example: line.

(enum Shape
    Square |side : i32|
    Rect   |width : i32 height : i32|)  ; each variant's | in one column  [1.5.9]
(struct Account |
    owner   : string
    balance : i64|)  ; every field's : in one column  [1.5.9]
(define area = {fn |shape : Shape -> i32|
    (match shape
        (Shape.Square side = s)           => (* s s)
        (Shape.Rect width = w height = h) => (* w h))})
    ; 4 spaces per level, => in one column, closers stacked  [1.5.2, 1.5.6, 1.5.9]
(define describe = {fn |shape : Shape -> string|
    (match shape
        (Shape.Square side = s) =>
            (`a square of side ${side}` (to_string s))
        (Shape.Rect ..)         => "a rectangle")})  ; the long arm breaks after =>  [1.5.7]
(+ x y )                   ; warning: no space before a closer      [1.5.1]
{fn |->Unit| ()}           ; warning: write |-> Unit|               [1.5.1]
(let xs:[List i32] = (list 1 2))  ; warning: write xs : [List i32]  [1.5.1]
(do
    (print "a")
)                          ; warning: a closer never starts a line  [1.5.6]

;;;; 2 grammar

;;; 2.1 token classes
Keyword      ; a reserved word (2.2)
Identifier   ; a name: x, first_or, Employee
Literal      ; a literal value (1.3)
Punctuation  ; a punctuation token (2.3)
Delimiter    ; ( ) { } [ ] or | | (2.4)
; 1. Every token belongs to exactly one of these classes.
; 2. A qualified name such as Weapon.Bow is two or more identifiers joined
;    by the punctuation token . (2.3.1).

(define PI : f64 = 3.14)  ; each token has one class; see 2.6.1  [2.1.1]
Weapon.Bow                ; Identifier, Punctuation, Identifier  [2.1.2]

;;; 2.2 keywords
do      ; a block (5.3)
fn      ; a function value (5.4)
forall  ; the type parameters of a generic function or impl (7.3, 7.5.3)
with    ; starts the bounds of a forall list (7.5.4), or a trait's supertraits (7.5.5)
define  ; a global binding (4.2)
let     ; a local binding (4.3)
if      ; a two-way choice (5.5), and the start of a guard (5.7.1)
when    ; a Unit body run if a condition holds (5.6)
unless  ; a Unit body run unless a condition holds (5.6)
struct  ; a struct type (4.4)
enum    ; an enum type (4.5)
trait   ; a trait: methods that types implement (7.5.1)
impl    ; an implementation of a trait for some types (7.5.3)
match   ; pattern matching (5.7)
next    ; an arm that shares the following arm's value (5.7.3)
loop    ; a loop (5.8)
recur   ; a new pass of a loop or fn (5.9)
for     ; a counting loop (5.10)
mod     ; imports a module (9.4)
export  ; exports a define, enum, struct or trait (9.6)
; 1. Keywords are reserved and are not values. A keyword cannot be bound,
;    passed or returned; it can only be the head of its own form.
; 2. The built-ins print, panic, assert and debug are not values either.
;    They are special forms (8.1.2).

(define choose = if)  ; error: if is a keyword, not a value  [2.2.1]
(let do : u32 = 1)    ; error: do is reserved                [2.2.1]
(define say = print)  ; error: print is a special form       [2.2.2]
(let with : u32 = 1)  ; error: with is reserved              [2.2.1]

;;; 2.3 punctuation
.   ; qualified names, field access and literal indexing
:   ; type annotation
::  ; module paths and access (9.4)
->  ; separates inputs from the output in signatures and function types
=>  ; separates a match arm's pattern from its value (5.7.1)
_   ; the wildcard pattern (6.1), a let or parameter that binds nothing (4.1.10),
    ; and the mark of an optional parameter in a trait (7.5.2.8)
@   ; binds a whole value in a pattern (6.7)
^   ; pins a name in a pattern, so it compares instead of binding (6.8)
..  ; skips items in a pattern, or copies fields in construction (4.4.4)
=   ; binds: define, let, defaults, bindings, field patterns
;   ; starts a comment (1.1)
; 1. A . between two identifiers forms a qualified name: Weapon.Bow,
;    Option.Some, Employee.dept. Qualified names chain, and are read left to
;    right: a.b.c is (a.b).c.
; 2. name.field, where name is bound to a struct, reads that field (4.4.3);
;    where name is bound to an enum, reads a field shared by all its
;    variants (4.5.3).
; 3. name.N, where name is bound to a list and N is an integer literal, reads
;    element N (3.4.2). Digits after a . are always an index, never part of
;    a float literal: grid.0.1 is (grid.0).1.
; 4. The left side of . must be an identifier or a qualified name. For any
;    other value, call an accessor function (4.4.3) or get (8.7.2).
; 5. :: binds tighter than ., so it is read first. An alias followed by an
;    item, such as shapes::circle::Shape, counts as an identifier for rule 4.
; 6. Where whitespace may go around these tokens is set by 1.2.9, 1.2.10
;    and 1.5.

; with jane : Employee, numbers : [List i32], grid : [List [List i32]],
; make_employee : [-> Employee], and team : Team, where Team has a field
; lead : Employee
Weapon.Bow                       ; a variant name                                [2.3.1]
Employee.dept                    ; an accessor name; type: [Employee -> string]  [2.3.1]
team.lead.dept                   ; (team.lead).dept: the dept of team's lead     [2.3.1]
jane.dept                        ; reads jane's dept field                       [2.3.2]
numbers.0                        ; reads element 0 of numbers                    [2.3.3]
grid.0.1                         ; (grid.0).1: element 1 of grid's first list    [2.3.3]
(make_employee).dept             ; error: . must follow an identifier            [2.3.4]
(Employee.dept (make_employee))  ; reads the field of any Employee value         [2.3.4]
; with (mod geometry::shapes::circle) and an exported define origin : Point
circle::Shape.Round              ; (circle::Shape).Round, a variant              [2.3.5]
circle::origin.x                 ; reads field x of circle::origin               [2.3.5]

;;; 2.4 delimiters

;; 2.4.1 what each delimiter wraps
()  ; an expression whose value is not a function (2.4.2)
{}  ; an expression whose value is a function (2.4.2)
[]  ; a type, a type header (7.2), a forall list (7.3), a type argument (7.4),
    ; or a trait header, bound or impl header (7.5)
||  ; a signature or a field list (2.5.2)
; 1. [ ] never wrap an expression, and ( ) and { } never wrap a type.
; 2. Bars pair up within one level of brackets. A | | pair inside a nested
;    { } belongs to the form inside those braces, which is how a fn can
;    appear inside a signature (2.6.3).

(let xs : [List u32] = (list [u32] 1 2))  ; types in [ ], a value in ( )        [2.4.1.1]
(let xs : (List u32) = (list [u32] 1 2))  ; error: a type must be in [ ]          [2.4.1.1]
[list 1 2]                                ; error: [ ] cannot wrap an expression  [2.4.1.1]
{fn |f : [u32 -> u32] = {fn |y : u32 -> u32| y} -> u32| (f 1)}
                                    ; two bar pairs, kept apart by { }  [2.4.1.2]

;; 2.4.2 ( ) versus { }
; 1. A form is written in { } when its value is a function, and in ( )
;    otherwise. This applies to calls and to every keyword form.
; 2. What counts is the type known at that point in the code. A value whose
;    type is a type parameter counts as not a function, even if that
;    parameter could stand for a function type (7.3.4).
; 3. Using the wrong bracket is a compile error.
; 4. {f} calls f with no arguments and expects a function back. It does not
;    wrap f. A function value on its own is written bare: f.
; 5. fn always produces a function, so it is always written {fn ...}.
;    why: a reader can tell from the bracket alone whether an expression
;    produces a function

(define make_adder = {fn |x : u32 -> [u32 -> u32]| {fn |y : u32 -> u32| (+ x y)}})
{make_adder 1}           ; type: [u32 -> u32]                                            [2.4.2.1]
({make_adder 1} 2)       ; returns 3; the callee can be any function value               [2.4.2.1]
{if true {make_adder 1} {make_adder 2}}  ; type: [u32 -> u32]; if follows the same rule  [2.4.2.1]
(make_adder 1)           ; error: the call returns a function; write {make_adder 1}      [2.4.2.3]
{+ 1 2}                  ; error: the call returns an i32; write (+ 1 2)                 [2.4.2.3]
make_adder               ; type: [u32 -> [u32 -> u32]], written bare                     [2.4.2.4]
{make_adder}             ; error: make_adder needs its argument x                        [2.4.2.4]
(define greet = `hello`)
(greet)                  ; returns "hello"; a call with no arguments                     [2.4.2.4]
(fn |x : u32 -> u32| x)  ; error: fn makes a function; write {fn ...}                    [2.4.2.5]

(define identity = {fn [forall T] |x : T -> T| x})
(identity [i32] 5)  ; returns 5; T is i32  [2.4.2.2]
{identity [u32 -> u32] {make_adder 1}}
    ; type: [u32 -> u32]; at this call T is known to be a function  [2.4.2.2]
(identity [u32 -> u32] {make_adder 1})
    ; error: here T is [u32 -> u32], so the call returns a function  [2.4.2.2]

;;; 2.5 syntactic categories
; These categories appear in general forms throughout the file.

;; 2.5.1 values and expressions
<Special T>      ; a form whose head is a keyword (2.2) or a built-in special form (8.1.2)
<Declaration>    ; a <Special Unit> that binds a name: define, let, struct, enum, trait
<Callee T>       ; a <Value F> whose return type is T: the function being called
<Application T>  ; (<Callee T> <Value>...), or {<Callee T> <Value>...} when T is a function type
<Expression T>   ; a <Special T> or an <Application T>
<Value T>        ; an <Expression T>, a literal of type T, an identifier bound to a T, or an
                 ; identifier and its <Type Argument>s (7.4)
; 1. Every <Declaration> returns Unit, so it is an <Expression Unit> and a
;    <Value Unit>.
; 2. Both function values and other values are <Value T>s. An identifier
;    bound to a function is a <Value F>.
; 3. A <Declaration>'s () is meant to be discarded. Using it as a value
;    warns (lint declaration_value, 4.7.3): as the last item of a do, a
;    branch of an if, the body of a when, unless, fn or loop, an arm value,
;    or an argument.
;    why?: only a do item that is not last can be followed by something
;    that reads the binding (4.1.3), so a used () marks a binding nothing
;    can read

(let x : u32 = 1)  ; a <Declaration>; returns ()                                     [2.5.1.1]
(+ 1 2)            ; an <Application i32>, so an <Expression i32> and a <Value i32>  [2.5.1.2]
+                  ; an identifier bound to a function: a <Value F>                  [2.5.1.2]
+ [u32]            ; + with a type argument: a <Value [u32 u32 -> u32]> (7.4)        [2.5.1.2]
(when true (let y : u32 = 1))
    ; warning: the let's () is used; warning: y is never used  [2.5.1.3, 4.1.10]

;; 2.5.2 types, parameters and fields
<Type>           ; a type identifier such as u32, or a bracketed type such as [List u32]
<Annotated T>    ; <Identifier> : <Type>
<Parameter T>    ; <Annotated T>, or <Annotated T> = <Value T> with a default
<Fields>         ; |<Parameter>...|: parameters with no output
<Signature>      ; |<Parameter>... -> <Type>|: parameters and an output
<Definition>     ; <Identifier> <Fields>?: a struct, or an enum variant
<Binding>        ; <Identifier> = <Value>: a value given to a field or parameter by name
<Constructor T>  ; (<Callee T> <Binding>...): builds a struct or variant value of type T
<Type Header>    ; [<Identifier> <Identifier> <Identifier>...]: a generic type and its parameters
<Forall>         ; [forall <Identifier> <Identifier>...], or
                 ; [forall <Identifier> <Identifier>... with <Bound> <Bound>...]: the type
                 ; parameters of a generic function or impl, and their bounds (7.5.4)
<Bound>          ; [<Identifier> <Type> <Type>...]: a trait applied to types (7.5.4)
<Type Argument>  ; [<Type>]: one type given to a generic name (7.4)
<Trait Header>   ; [<Identifier> <Identifier> <Identifier>...]: a trait and its parameters (7.5.1)
<Method Type>    ; a function type whose last parameters may be written <Type> = _ (7.5.2.8)
<Trait Entry>    ; <Identifier> : <Method Type>, a required method, or
                 ; <Identifier> = <Value F>, an auto-implemented one (7.5.1)
<Impl Entry>     ; <Identifier> = <Value F>: a method's implementation (7.5.3)
; 1. A <Signature> always has an output type after ->. A bar list with no ->
;    is always a <Fields>.
; 2. A type identifier is read as a <Type> wherever a type is expected,
;    even though it is also an Identifier.
; 3. A <Type Header>, a <Trait Header> and a <Bound> are written alike. What
;    one is follows from where it stands: after struct or enum, after trait,
;    or after with and at the head of an impl.

{fn |x : u32 -> u32| x}   ; |x : u32 -> u32| is a <Signature>                           [2.5.2.1]
{fn |x : u32| x}          ; error: fn needs a <Signature>, and |x : u32| has no output  [2.5.2.1]
(struct Point |x : i32 y : i32|)  ; |x : i32 y : i32| is a <Fields>                     [2.5.2.1]
(struct Pair |x : i32 -> i32|)  ; error: struct takes a <Fields>, which has no output   [2.5.2.1]
(define E : f64 = 2.718)  ; f64 is read as a <Type>                                     [2.5.2.2]
(trait [Shape T] area : [T -> f64])  ; [Shape T] is a <Trait Header>                    [2.5.2.3]
{fn [forall T with [Shape T]] |s : T -> f64| (area s)}  ; [Shape T] is a <Bound>        [2.5.2.3]

;; 2.5.3 arms and patterns
<Pattern T>      ; a pattern that matches values of type T (6)
<Field Pattern>  ; <Identifier> = <Pattern>: a field, and the pattern its value must match
<Box Pattern>    ; (box <Pattern T>): matches a [Box T] whose contents match (6.6)
<Pin>            ; ^<Identifier>, or ^ and a qualified name: a name that compares (6.8)
<Guard>          ; if <Value bool>
<Arm T U>        ; <Pattern T> <Guard>? => <Value U>, or <Pattern T> <Guard>? => next

;;; 2.6 worked breakdowns
; Each diagram takes one expression apart. Every line under the first repeats
; some of its tokens in the same columns.

;; 2.6.1 a constant definition
(define PI : f64 = 3.14)  ; the whole form: a <Declaration>, so an <Expression Unit>
 define                   ; Keyword
        PI                ; Identifier; after this define, also a <Value f64>
           :              ; Punctuation
             f64          ; a <Type>; also an Identifier, but <Type> wins (2.5.2.2)
        PI : f64          ; <Annotated f64>
                 =        ; Punctuation
                   3.14   ; Literal, and a <Value f64>

;; 2.6.2 an anonymous function
{fn |x : u32 y : u32 -> u32| (+ x y)}  ; <Expression [u32 u32 -> u32]>
 fn                                    ; Keyword
    |x : u32 y : u32 -> u32|           ; <Signature>; in the body, x and y are <Value u32>s
     x       y                         ; Identifiers
       :       :     ->                ; Punctuation
         u32     u32    u32            ; <Type>s
     x : u32                           ; <Annotated u32>
             y : u32                   ; <Annotated u32>
                             (+ x y)   ; <Expression u32>, so a <Value u32>
                              +        ; Identifier; here a function of type [u32 u32 -> u32]
                                x y    ; Identifiers, and <Value u32>s

;; 2.6.3 a function-valued default
; The default for f is itself a fn, so the signature holds a second pair of
; bars. The { } around the inner fn keep the two pairs apart (2.4.1.2).
{fn |x : u32  f : [u32 -> u32] = {fn |y : u32 -> u32| (+ y 1_u32)} -> u32| (f x)}
;   ^ open (depth 1)                 ^ open (depth 2)                    ^ close (depth 1)
;                                                   ^ close (depth 2)

;; 2.6.4 a match arm
(Weapon.Bow damage = d) if (> d 10.0) => true  ; <Arm Weapon bool>
(Weapon.Bow damage = d)                        ; <Pattern Weapon>: a constructor pattern (6.4)
 Weapon.Bow                                    ; Identifier: the variant, qualified (6.4.2)
            damage = d                         ; <Field Pattern>: the field damage, bound to d
                        if (> d 10.0)          ; <Guard>; d is bound here (5.7.1.2)
                                      =>       ; Punctuation
                                         true  ; <Value bool>: the arm's value

;; 2.6.5 a loop
(loop |n : u32 = 1 -> u32| (if (< n 9_u32) (recur n = (* n 2_u32)) n))  ; <Expression u32>; returns 16
 loop                                                                   ; Keyword
      |n : u32 = 1 -> u32|                                              ; <Signature>
       n : u32 = 1                                                      ; <Parameter u32>; n starts at 1
                   -> u32                                               ; the loop's type (5.8.2)
                           (if (< n 9_u32) (recur n = (* n 2_u32)) n)   ; the body
                               (< n 9_u32)                              ; go on while n < 9
                                           (recur n = (* n 2_u32))      ; next pass, n doubled
                                                  n = (* n 2_u32)       ; <Binding>
                                                                   n    ; the value of the last pass
; 9_u32 and 2_u32 need their suffixes: the parameters of < and * are type
; parameters, so a literal there keeps its fixed type (5.1.1.3)

;; 2.6.6 a generic function
{fn [forall T U] |x : T f : [T -> U] -> U| (f x)}  ; type: [[forall T U] T [T -> U] -> U]
 fn                                                ; Keyword
    [forall T U]                                   ; <Forall>
     forall                                        ; Keyword
            T U                                    ; Identifiers, usable as types from here on
                 |x : T f : [T -> U] -> U|         ; <Signature>
                            [T -> U]               ; <Type>: a function type (3.3)
                                           (f x)   ; <Application U>, in ( ) (2.4.2.2)

;; 2.6.7 a call with type arguments
(first_or [i32] numbers 0)  ; <Application i32>, with numbers : [List i32]
 first_or                   ; Identifier: a generic function (7.3)
          [i32]             ; <Type Argument>: T is i32 (7.4)
 first_or [i32]             ; <Callee i32>: first_or with T given
                numbers     ; <Value [List i32]>
                        0   ; Literal; an i32, the type written for fallback (5.1.1.2)

;;;; 3 types

;;; 3.1 primitive types
u8      ; unsigned 8-bit integer
u16     ; unsigned 16-bit integer
u32     ; unsigned 32-bit integer
u64     ; unsigned 64-bit integer
u128    ; unsigned 128-bit integer, a quad
u256    ; unsigned 256-bit integer, an oct
ubig    ; unsigned integer of any size
usize   ; unsigned integer the width of an address on the target
i8      ; signed 8-bit integer
i16     ; signed 16-bit integer
i32     ; signed 32-bit integer
i64     ; signed 64-bit integer
i128    ; signed 128-bit integer, a quad
i256    ; signed 256-bit integer, an oct
ibig    ; signed integer of any size
isize   ; signed integer the width of an address on the target
f32     ; 32-bit float
f64     ; 64-bit float
f128    ; 128-bit float, a quad
f256    ; 256-bit float, an oct
bool    ; true or false
char    ; a character, written 'c' (1.3.3)
string  ; text, written "like this" (1.3.4)
; 1. Primitive type names are lowercase.
; 2. The integer and float types are the numerical types. bool and char are
;    not (1.3.3.4).
; 3. There are no implicit conversions. A value changes type only through an
;    explicit cast (8.6). The casts are methods of traits over a target and
;    a source type, such as [Widen To From] (7.5.2, 8.6.1).
;    why: every change of type is visible in the code
; 4. u8 to u256 and i8 to i256 are the fixed-size integer types. A u type
;    of N bits holds 0 to 2^N - 1. An i type of N bits holds -2^(N-1) to
;    2^(N-1) - 1, in two's complement.
; 5. f32 to f256 are the fixed-size float types: the IEEE 754 binary32,
;    binary64, binary128 and binary256 formats. Their significands hold 24,
;    53, 113 and 237 bits.
; 6. A fixed-size type gives the same results on every target. Where the
;    hardware lacks a type, the compiler emulates it in software, which is
;    slower. Most hardware lacks f128, and none has f256.
;    why?: a program's results do not depend on the machine it runs on
; 7. ubig and ibig are the big types. A ubig holds any integer from 0 up,
;    and an ibig any integer. Each grows to fit its value, so an ibig never
;    overflows, and a ubig overflows only below 0, like any unsigned type
;    (11.5.3).
; 8. A big type value's size is not known at compile time. Its digits sit
;    on the heap in a buffer with a length and a capacity, as a List keeps
;    its elements (3.4). When a result outgrows the capacity, the capacity
;    doubles. When an operand is not used again (9.2), the result is written
;    into that operand's buffer. Running out of memory panics (8.9). When a
;    buffer shrinks is open (11.3.1).
;    why?: a loop that grows one value, such as a factorial, takes linear
;    time overall, instead of copying the value at every step
; 9. usize and isize are the size types. Their width W is the width of a
;    memory address on the target: 32 or 64 bits. A usize holds 0 to
;    2^W - 1, and an isize holds -2^(W-1) to 2^(W-1) - 1, in two's
;    complement. They are meant for sizes, counts and offsets in memory.
;    why?: they match what the machine uses to address memory, so they
;    need no conversion there
; 10. The size types are the only numerical types whose range depends on
;     the target. The compiler checks them against the narrowest width, 32
;     bits: a literal of a size type must fit in 32 bits (1.3.1.9), and as
;     accepts a conversion only when it widens on every target (8.6.2).
;     Arithmetic, overflow and wrap (8.6.4) use the target's own width, so
;     a result past 32 bits can differ between targets.
;     why?: a program that compiles for one target compiles for every
;     target; only values past 32 bits are not portable
; Floats of any precision are not built in. They will likely come from
; the standard library (11.8.5) or a specific custom math library.

(let count : u32 = 3)                 ; u32 is primitive, so lowercase               [3.1.1]
(+ 2.5 1.5)                           ; f64 is numerical; returns 4.0                [3.1.2]
(+ true false)                        ; error: bool is not numerical; + has no impl for it  [3.1.2]
(let wide : u64 = count)              ; error: count is a u32; write (as [u64] count)  [3.1.3]
(let wider : u64 = (as [u64] count))  ; the explicit cast                              [3.1.3]
340282366920938463463374607431768211455_u128  ; the largest u128, 2^128 - 1          [3.1.4]
340282366920938463463374607431768211456_u128  ; error: 2^128 does not fit in a u128  [3.1.4]
-1_i256                               ; type: i256                                   [3.1.4]
1e400_f128                            ; type: f128                                   [3.1.5]
1e400_f64                             ; error: outside the range of an f64           [3.1.5]
(pow [ubig] 2 200)                    ; returns 2^200, a ubig; it grows to fit       [3.1.7]
(* (pow [ibig] 10 40) -3_ibig)        ; returns -3 * 10^40, an ibig                  [3.1.7]
(let size : usize = 4096)             ; usize is a size type                         [3.1.9]
-1_isize                              ; type: isize                                  [3.1.9]
4294967295_usize                      ; the largest usize literal, 2^32 - 1          [3.1.10]
4294967296_usize                      ; error: 2^32 does not fit a 32-bit usize      [3.1.10]
(* [usize] 65536 65536)               ; 2^32 on a 64-bit target; overflows on 32 bits  [3.1.10]

;;; 3.2 Unit and Never
Unit   ; a type with exactly one value, written ()
Never  ; a type with no values
; 1. () is the value of an empty expression, such as (do) (5.3).
; 2. Discarding a Unit or Never value never warns. Discarding a value of
;    any other type warns (5.3.4; lint discarded_value, 4.7.3).
;    why: a discarded non-Unit value was computed for nothing, which is
;    usually a mistake
;    why: a Never expression produces no value, so nothing is discarded
; 3. No expression of type Never produces a value. panic returns Never
;    (8.9).
; 4. A Never value fits wherever a value of any other type is expected:
;    as an if branch, a match arm, a let's value, an argument or a
;    function's result.
;    why?: no Never value is ever produced, so it can never be a value of
;    the wrong type
; 5. Code that can only run after a Never expression is unreachable, and
;    warns (lint unreachable, 4.7.3). In a do (5.3), every item after an
;    item of type Never is unreachable.
;    why?: unreachable code never runs, so it is usually a mistake, such as
;    a panic left in by accident

()                  ; type: Unit                                  [3.2.1]
(do)                ; returns ()                                  [3.2.1]
(do (print "a") 1)  ; print's () is discarded without a warning   [3.2.2]
(do (+ 1 2) 1)      ; warning: the i32 from (+ 1 2) is discarded  [3.2.2]
(panic "stop")      ; type: Never; panics: stop                   [3.2.3]
(if true 5 (panic))                ; returns 5; the branches fit as i32s  [3.2.4]
(let size : u32 = (panic "no size"))  ; panics: no size                   [3.2.4]
(do
    (panic "stop")  ; the Never is discarded without a warning  [3.2.2]
    (print "after")
    1)  ; panics: stop; warning: (print "after") and 1 are unreachable  [3.2.5]

;;; 3.3 function types
[T... -> U]  ; a function from parameters of types T... to a U
; 1. A bracketed type whose outermost level contains -> is a function type.
; 2. A function has exactly one output type.
; 3. A value of a function type is produced in { } (2.4.2).
; 4. [-> Unit] takes nothing and returns Unit. It is the emptiest function
;    type.
; 5. The type of a generic function starts with its forall list:
;    [[forall T] T -> T] (7.3).
; 6. Function values cannot be compared: no function type has ==, and none
;    ever will (8.3.2).
; 7. A function value is a fixed size, whatever its type: it refers to its
;    body and the values it keeps alive (9.2.2). So a function type counts
;    as indirection (3.7.4).
; 8. A function type does not record which parameters have defaults. Only a
;    required method's type in a trait may mark a parameter optional
;    (7.5.2.8). Whether a function value should keep its defaults in its
;    type is open (11.3.13).

[u32 u32 -> u32]                ; two u32 parameters, a u32 output                           [3.3.1]
[u32 -> [u32 -> u32]]           ; returns a function                                         [3.3.1]
[[u32 -> u32] u32 -> u32]       ; takes a function as its first parameter                    [3.3.1]
[List [u32 -> u32]]             ; not a function type: the -> is not at the outermost level  [3.3.1]
[u32 -> u32 bool]               ; error: a function has one output type                      [3.3.2]
(if true {fn |-> u32| 1} {fn |-> u32| 2})  ; error: the value is a [-> u32]; write {if ...}  [3.3.3]
{fn |-> Unit| ()}               ; type: [-> Unit]                                            [3.3.4]
{fn [forall T] |x : T -> T| x}  ; type: [[forall T] T -> T]                                  [3.3.5]
(== {fn |-> u32| 1} {fn |-> u32| 1})  ; error: functions cannot be compared                  [3.3.6]
{fn |x : u32 n : u32 = 1 -> u32| x}   ; type: [u32 u32 -> u32]; the default is not in the type  [3.3.8]

;;; 3.4 List

;; 3.4.1 the type
[List T]  ; an ordered list of values of type T
; 1. All elements of a list have the same type T.
; 2. Indices are u32 and start at 0.
; 3. list builds a list (8.7.1), and get reads an element safely (8.7.2).

(let numbers : [List i32] = (list 1 2 3))  ; three i32s                         [3.4.1.1]
(list 1 "two")   ; error: an i32 and a string in one list                       [3.4.1.1]
(get numbers 0)  ; returns (Option.Some [i32] value = 1); index 0 is the first  [3.4.1.2]
(let j : i32 = 1)
(get numbers j)  ; error: j is an i32, and an index is a u32                    [3.4.1.2]
(get numbers 2)  ; returns (Option.Some [i32] value = 3)                        [3.4.1.3]

;; 3.4.2 literal indexing
; 1. name.N, where name is an identifier or a qualified name (2.3.1) bound
;    to a list and N is an integer literal, returns element N.
; 2. If N is out of range, name.N panics.
; 3. N must be a literal. For any other index, use get (8.7.2).

numbers.0        ; returns 1                                                    [3.4.2.1]
numbers.2        ; returns 3                                                    [3.4.2.1]
numbers.3        ; panics: index out of range                                   [3.4.2.2]
(let i : u32 = 1)
numbers.i        ; error: numbers.i is field access, and a list has no field i  [3.4.2.3]
(get numbers i)  ; returns (Option.Some [i32] value = 2)                        [3.4.2.3]
(list 7 8 9).0   ; error: . must follow an identifier (2.3.4)                   [3.4.2.1]

;;; 3.5 Map (todo)
[Map K V]  ; a map from keys of type K to values of type V
; Maps cannot be built or read yet (11.3.6).
; 1. A Map keeps its contents on the heap in a buffer, as a List keeps its
;    elements (3.1.8). So a [Map K V] is a fixed size whatever K and V are,
;    and counts as indirection (3.7.4).

;;; 3.6 Option and Result
[Option T]    ; either a T or nothing
[Result T E]  ; either a success T or an error E
; Both are built-in generic enums (4.5, 7.2), defined as if by:
(enum [Option T] Some |value : T| None)
(enum [Result T E] Ok |value : T| Error |error : E|)
; 1. Their variants are qualified like any other: Option.Some, Option.None,
;    Result.Ok and Result.Error (4.5.2).
; 2. Option.None has no fields, so it is a value, not a function.
; 3. Like any generic enum, they are built with their type arguments (7.4):
;    (Option.Some [i32] value = 5), Option.None [i32]. A Result.Ok or
;    Result.Error takes both T and E, even though it holds only one of them.
; 4. Like any enum, they hold their fields inline: an Option.Some is as big
;    as its value. They are not indirection, so a type that contains itself
;    through one of them needs a Box (3.7.5).

(Option.Some [i32] value = 5)                     ; type: [Option i32]                         [3.6.3]
(let nothing : [Option i32] = Option.None [i32])  ; no parentheses                             [3.6.2]
(let failed = (Result.Error [u32] [string] error = "bad input"))
                                                  ; failed : [Result u32 string]               [3.6.3]
(Some [i32] value = 5)                            ; error: write Option.Some                   [3.6.1]
(Option.None [i32])                               ; error: Option.None is not a function       [3.6.2]
(Option.Some value = 5)                           ; error: Option.Some needs its type argument  [3.6.3]

;;; 3.7 Box
[Box T]  ; a T stored on the heap, behind a pointer
; 1. Box is a built-in generic type. A [Box T] is built with box and read
;    with unbox (8.8), or with a box pattern (6.6). It is one pointer in
;    size, whatever T is (9.2.5).
;    why: no new grammar; a Box is a type, so it goes wherever a type can,
;    and the allocation is visible where it happens
; 2. Every type has a fixed size, so a type may not contain itself inline.
;    A struct or enum that reaches itself by walking its fields, directly
;    or through other types, before passing through indirection (rule 4)
;    is an error.
;    why: the cost of indirection is written where it is paid; nothing
;    boxes a field automatically, which would hide an allocation
; 3. A reading never sees through a box by itself. A [Box T] is not a T:
;    unbox it, or match it with a box pattern.
;    why?: seeing through would be an implicit conversion (3.1.3)
; 4. Box, List, Map and function types are indirection. Each is a fixed
;    size whatever its parameters are (3.4, 3.5.1, 3.3.7), so a type may
;    contain itself through any of them, and the walk of rule 2 stops
;    there. This list is written out for now. It will be expressed more
;    generally later, so that a program's own containers can count too
;    (11.7.17).
;    - Cost: List and Map are committed to keeping their contents off the
;      inline value.
; 5. An enum such as Option is inline (3.6.4), so [Option [Tree T]] inside
;    Tree is an error. The error suggests [Option [Box [Tree T]]]. A
;    program's own [Maybe T] behaves exactly like Option.
; 6. A type with no finite value is a warning (lint uninhabited, 4.7.3).
;    The check, repeated until nothing changes: a struct can have a value
;    if all its fields can, and an enum if some variant's fields all can.
;    Type parameters are assumed to have values. Unit, the numerical types,
;    bool, char and string always do, and Never never does. [List X] and
;    [Map K V] always do (the empty one), as do [Option X] (None) and
;    function types (a body can panic). [Box X] has one only if X does.
;    why: values are acyclic (9.2.3), so a type whose every value needs
;    another value of the same type can never be built; that is a mistake,
;    cheap to find
;    A type meant to be empty is written Never (3.2), and the check does
;    not apply to it.
; 7. Which types may refer to themselves at which type arguments is set by
;    7.2.5.

(enum [Tree T]
    Leaf
    Node |left : [Box [Tree T]] value : T right : [Box [Tree T]]|)  ; ok: through Box  [3.7.2]
(struct Loop |next : Loop|)  ; error: Loop contains itself without a Box               [3.7.2]
(struct Ping |pong : Pong|)
(struct Pong |ping : Ping|)  ; error: Ping contains itself through Pong without a Box  [3.7.2]
(struct [Rose T] |value : T kids : [List [Rose T]]|)  ; ok: List is indirection         [3.7.4]
(struct Stream |head : u8 next : [-> Stream]|)        ; ok: a lazy stream               [3.7.4]
(struct [Forest T] |trees : [List [Box [Tree T]]]|)   ; ok, but the Box is not needed   [3.7.4]
(struct [Link T] |value : T next : [Option [Link T]]|)
    ; error: Link contains itself through Option without a Box;
    ; write [Option [Box [Link T]]]  [3.7.5]
(struct [Chain T] |value : T next : [Option [Box [Chain T]]]|)  ; ok                   [3.7.5]
(struct Spiral |next : [Box Spiral]|)  ; warning: Spiral has no finite value           [3.7.6]
(struct Bush |kids : [List Bush]|)     ; ok: the empty list ends it                    [3.7.6]
(let leaf = (box Tree.Leaf [u8]))      ; leaf : [Box [Tree u8]]                        [3.7.1]
(let t = (Tree.Node [u8] left = leaf value = 1_u8 right = leaf))  ; t : [Tree u8]      [3.7.1]
(let l : [Tree u8] = leaf)             ; error: leaf is a [Box [Tree u8]], not a [Tree u8]  [3.7.3]
(unbox leaf)                           ; returns Tree.Leaf [u8]                        [3.7.1]

;;;; 4 declarations

;;; 4.1 bindings and scope
; 1. A binding gives a name to a value. Bindings are immutable: nothing in
;    the language changes a binding once it is made. Mutable references
;    will never be added. If anything like mutation ever arrives, it will
;    only drop or assign, in place, a value nothing else refers to; it will
;    never be a reference that is shared and written through (9.2.3).
; 2. define binds in the global scope. Global definitions are
;    order-independent: each may refer to any other, wherever it appears.
; 3. let binds inside the form that directly contains it. Its name is
;    visible from the next item of that form to the form's end. Only a do
;    has later items, so a let anywhere else binds a name nothing can read
;    (4.1.10, 2.5.1.3).
; 4. When a block ends, the names it bound go out of scope, and their values
;    are dropped unless something still refers to them (9.2.2).
; 5. A local binding may reuse the name of any local binding in scope, in
;    the same block or an outer one. This is shadowing. The new binding may
;    have a different type. Local bindings are made by let, by parameters
;    (5.4.1, 5.8, 5.10) and by patterns (6).
;    why?: bindings are immutable, so shadowing is how a value is refined
;    step by step without inventing a new name for each step
; 6. Shadowing changes no binding. The shadowed one is unreachable by name
;    until the shadowing one goes out of scope. A fn that captured it
;    (5.4.1.4) keeps it.
; 7. Two top-level names may not be the same. This covers define, struct,
;    enum, trait, the methods of every trait (7.5.2.1), mod aliases (9.4)
;    and the built-in names of sections 3 and 8, which include the names
;    of the prelude (11.8.5).
; 8. A local binding that hides a top-level or built-in name is a warning
;    (lint shadowing, 4.7.3). This includes a trait's method. A pattern
;    never hides one: a top-level name in a pattern compares (6.2.2). A
;    trait's own name cannot be hidden at all: a local binding or a type
;    parameter with a trait's name is an error (7.5.1.7).
;    why?: a new global or built-in can then never break local code, only
;    warn
; 9. No binding, top-level or local, may take the name of a keyword (2.2)
;    or a special form (8.1.2). This covers let, parameters and patterns,
;    and it is an error, not the warning of 8.
;    why?: keywords and special forms are not values (2.2.1, 2.2.2), so a
;    binding by one of their names could never be read the way the name
;    reads elsewhere; a fixed, short list costs local code little
; 10. A local binding that is never read warns (lint unused_binding,
;     4.7.3). This covers let, parameters (5.4.1, 5.8, 5.10), patterns (6)
;     and the name of a named fn (5.4.3.7); 5.7.1.6 is this rule for arms.
;     Write _ instead of a name: _ binds nothing, so it never warns, and it
;     may appear any number of times. For a named fn, drop the name.
;     - A parameter named _ still takes an argument by position, and may
;       have a default. It cannot be named in recur (5.9.3), so in a loop it
;       keeps its default on every pass.

(define is_even = {fn |n : u32 -> bool| (if (== n 0_u32) true (is_odd (- n 1_u32)))})
(define is_odd = {fn |n : u32 -> bool| (if (== n 0_u32) false (is_even (- n 1_u32)))})
(is_even 4)  ; returns true; is_even uses is_odd before its define  [4.1.2]
(do
    (let a : u32 = 1)
    (let b : u32 = (+ a 1_u32))  ; a is visible here  [4.1.3]
    b)                           ; returns 2
(do
    (let b : u32 = (+ a 1_u32))  ; error: a is not bound yet  [4.1.3]
    (let a : u32 = 1)
    b)
(do
    (if true (let y : u32 = 1) ())
    y)  ; error: y is bound only inside the if's branch  [4.1.3]
(do
    (do (let inner : u32 = 1))
    inner)  ; error: inner went out of scope with its block  [4.1.4]
(do
    (let total : u32 = 1)
    (= total 2)  ; error: there is no assignment; = only appears inside binding forms  [4.1.1]
    total)
(do
    (let n : u32 = 5)
    (let n = (+ n 1_u32))    ; the right side still sees the old n; n : u32  [4.1.5, 4.1.3]
    (let n = (to_string n))  ; n : string; a shadow may change the type      [4.1.5]
    n)                       ; returns "6"
(do
    (let x : u32 = 1)
    (do (let x : u32 = 2) x)  ; returns 2; the inner x shadows the outer one  [4.1.5]
    x)                        ; returns 1; the outer x is back                [4.1.6]
(do
    (let x : u32 = 1)
    (let get_x = {fn |-> u32| x})
    (let x : u32 = 2)
    (get_x))  ; returns 1; get_x kept the x it captured  [4.1.6]
(define MAX_SIZE : u32 = 10)
(define MAX_SIZE : u32 = 20)  ; error: MAX_SIZE is already bound      [4.1.7]
(struct MAX_SIZE |n : u32|)   ; error: MAX_SIZE is already bound      [4.1.7]
(define list = 1)             ; error: list is a built-in name (8.7)  [4.1.7]
(define greeting = "hi")
(do (let greeting = "hey") greeting)
    ; returns "hey"; warning: greeting hides a top-level name  [4.1.8]
(do (let list = (list 1 2)) list)
    ; returns the list; warning: list hides a built-in name   [4.1.8]
(define debug = 1)             ; error: debug is a special form                [4.1.9]
(do (let print = "hi") print)  ; error: print is a special form, not a warning [4.1.9]
({fn |if : u32 -> u32| if} 1)  ; error: a parameter may not be named if        [4.1.9]
(do (let unused : u32 = 1) 2)        ; returns 2; warning: unused is never used  [4.1.10]
(do (let _ = (to_string 5)) ())      ; returns (); no warning                    [4.1.10]
{fn |_ : u32 x : u32 -> u32| x}      ; no warning: _ is never read               [4.1.10]
{fn |n : u32 -> u32| 0_u32}          ; warning: n is never used                  [4.1.10]
{fn helper |n : u32 -> u32| (+ n 1_u32)}
    ; warning: helper is never used; drop the name  [4.1.10]
(trait [Shape T] area : [T -> f64])
(define area = {fn |r : f64 -> f64| r})  ; error: area is already bound, as Shape's method  [4.1.7]
(do (let area = 2.0) area)  ; returns 2.0; warning: area hides Shape's method   [4.1.8]
(do (let Shape = 2.0) Shape)  ; error: a trait's name cannot be hidden          [4.1.8]

;;; 4.2 define
(define <Annotated T> = <Value T>) -> Unit
(define <Identifier> = <Value T>) -> Unit
; 1. define binds a name in the global scope. It may only appear at the top
;    level.
; 2. The annotation may be left out. The name then has its value's type,
;    which is worked out from the value itself.
;    why?: a value's type never depends on where the value is used: a
;    literal's type is written or fixed (5.1.1), and a call's types come
;    from its type arguments and its arguments alone (7.4.7). So every
;    value's type is known from the value alone
; 3. Defines without annotations may not need each other's types in a cycle,
;    since then none of the types can be worked out. The error names every
;    define in the cycle. Annotating one of them breaks it. A fn literal's
;    type comes from its signature, so fns may still call each other.

(define SOME_NUMBER : u32 = (+ [u32] 2 5))  ; returns (); SOME_NUMBER is global            [4.2.1]
(do (define X : u32 = 1) X)                 ; error: define only appears at the top level  [4.2.1]
(define E = 2.718)                          ; E : f64, from the literal                    [4.2.2]
(define add = {fn |x : u32 y : u32 -> u32| (+ x y)})  ; add : [u32 u32 -> u32]             [4.2.2]
; an annotation is allowed even where it could be left out  [4.2.2]
(define add_2 : [u32 u32 -> u32] = {fn |x : u32 y : u32 -> u32| (+ x y)})
(define LIMIT = (+ 2 5))        ; LIMIT : i32, the type of (+ 2 5)  [4.2.2]
(define primes = (list 2 3 5))  ; primes : [List i32]               [4.2.2]
(define A = B)
(define B = A)                  ; error: A and B need each other's types; annotate one  [4.2.3]
(define C = (twice 2))
(define twice = {fn |n : i32 -> i32| (* n 2)})  ; fine: twice's type is its signature  [4.2.3]

;;; 4.3 let
(let <Annotated T> = <Value T>) -> Unit
(let <Identifier> = <Value T>) -> Unit
(let _ = <Value T>) -> Unit  ; evaluates the value and binds nothing (4.1.10)
; 1. let binds a name for the rest of the form that directly contains it
;    (4.1.3).
; 2. As with define, the annotation may be left out (4.2.2).
; 3. let may not appear at the top level: use define.
;    why: a top-level define can be exported and used before the line it is
;    on, so a top-level let would only add an ordering constraint
; 4. let may shadow an earlier binding (4.1.5).

(do
    (let some_other_number : u32 = (+ [u32] 2 5))  ; annotated           [4.3.1]
    (let greeting = "hi")                          ; greeting : string   [4.3.2]
    (let inc = {fn |x : u32 -> u32| (+ x 1_u32)})  ; inc : [u32 -> u32]  [4.3.2]
    (inc some_other_number))                       ; returns 8
(do (let doubled = (* 2 3)) doubled)  ; returns 6; doubled : i32                 [4.3.2]
(do (let s = "hi") (let s = (+ s "!")) s)  ; returns "hi!"                       [4.3.4]
(let top_level_number : u32 = 7)      ; error: let may not appear at the top level; use define  [4.3.3]

;;; 4.4 struct

;; 4.4.1 definition
(struct <Definition>) -> Unit
(struct <Type Header> <Fields>) -> Unit  ; a generic struct (7.2)
; 1. A struct is a product type: each value holds all of its fields.
; 2. The definition's <Identifier> becomes a type, and the name of that
;    type's constructor.
; 3. Every field is a named <Parameter>, and may have a default value.
; 4. struct may only appear at the top level.

(struct Employee |
    name   : string
    age    : u32
    dept   : string = "unknown"  ; a field with a default                    [4.4.1.3]
    salary : f64|)               ; Employee is now a type and a constructor  [4.4.1.2]
(struct Hacker |
    skill  : string
    salary : f64|)
(struct Sword |
    damage    : f64
    name      : string
    legendary : bool|)
(struct Point |x : i32 y : i32|)  ; every Point holds both an x and a y  [4.4.1.1]
(struct Bad |i32 string|)         ; error: every field needs a name      [4.4.1.3]
(do (struct Local |x : i32|) ())  ; error: struct only appears at the top level  [4.4.1.4]

;; 4.4.2 construction
(<Identifier> <Binding>...) -> T
; 1. A struct value is built by calling the struct's name with one binding
;    per field.
; 2. Bindings are by name, so their order does not matter.
; 3. A field with a default may be left out, and then takes its default.
; 4. It is a compile error to leave out a field that has no default, to bind
;    a field twice, or to bind a name that is not a field.

(let john : Employee = (Employee
    name = "john"
    age = 24
    dept = "finance"
    salary = 2.0))  ; one binding per field  [4.4.2.1]
(let jane : Employee = (Employee
    name = "jane"
    age = 22
    salary = 2.5))  ; dept is left out, so it is "unknown"  [4.4.2.3]
(define excalibur : Sword = (Sword
    damage = 12.0
    legendary = true
    name = "Excalibur"))  ; not in definition order, which is fine  [4.4.2.2]
(Point 1 2)                       ; error: fields are bound by name                     [4.4.2.1]
(Employee name = "max" age = 30)  ; error: salary has no default                        [4.4.2.4]
(Employee name = "max" name = "m" age = 30 salary = 1.0)  ; error: name is bound twice  [4.4.2.4]
(Point x = 1 y = 2 z = 3)         ; error: Point has no field z                         [4.4.2.4]

;; 4.4.3 field access
; 1. Every field F of a struct S has an accessor function S.F of type
;    [S -> T], where T is the field's type. The struct definition creates
;    it, with the same scope as the struct.
; 2. name.F reads field F of the struct bound to name, an identifier or a
;    qualified name (2.3.1).
; 3. . only works after an identifier or a qualified name (2.3.4). For any
;    other value, call the accessor function.
;    why: . then stays part of names and is never an operator on
;    expressions

jane.dept             ; returns "unknown"                                          [4.4.3.2]
excalibur.legendary   ; returns true                                               [4.4.3.2]
jane.rank             ; error: Employee has no field rank                          [4.4.3.2]
(Employee.dept jane)  ; returns "unknown"                                          [4.4.3.1]
Employee.dept         ; type: [Employee -> string]                                 [4.4.3.1]
(let get_name : [Employee -> string] = Employee.name)
(get_name john)       ; returns "john"; an accessor is an ordinary function value  [4.4.3.1]
(Employee
    name = "jane"
    age = 22
    salary = 2.5).dept  ; error: . must follow an identifier  [4.4.3.3]
(Employee.dept (Employee
    name = "jane"
    age = 22
    salary = 2.5))  ; returns "unknown"  [4.4.3.3]

;; 4.4.4 copying fields
(<Identifier> ..<Identifier> <Binding>...) -> T
; 1. ..source copies every field of source, which is a value of the same
;    struct type.
; 2. A binding after ..source replaces the copied value of that field.

(let bob : Employee = (Employee
    ..jane
    name = "bob"
    age = 23))  ; name and age replaced  [4.4.4.2]
bob.salary                                   ; returns 2.5, copied from jane             [4.4.4.1]
(let jane_2 : Employee = (Employee ..jane))  ; a field-for-field copy                    [4.4.4.1]
(Hacker ..jane skill = "none")               ; error: jane is an Employee, not a Hacker  [4.4.4.1]

;;; 4.5 enum

;; 4.5.1 definition
(enum <Identifier> <Definition>...) -> Unit
(enum <Type Header> <Definition>...) -> Unit  ; a generic enum (7.2)
; 1. An enum is a sum type: each value is exactly one of its variants.
; 2. The <Identifier> becomes a type. Each <Definition> becomes a variant,
;    named Enum.Variant.
; 3. A variant's fields follow the struct rules (4.4.1.3): every field is
;    named, and may have a default.
; 4. A variant with no fields is a value, not a function.
; 5. enum may only appear at the top level.

(enum Weapon
    Sword |sword : Sword|
    Bow   |damage : f64|
    Fist)  ; variants Weapon.Sword, Weapon.Bow and Weapon.Fist  [4.5.1.2]
(enum Person
    Employee |person : Employee|
    Hacker   |person : Hacker|)  ; a Person is an Employee or a Hacker, never both  [4.5.1.1]
(enum Signal
    Beep |pitch : u32 = 440|
    Silence)  ; a variant field with a default  [4.5.1.3]
(enum Broken
    Wrapped |f64|)  ; error: the field needs a name  [4.5.1.3]
(do (enum Local A B) ())  ; error: enum only appears at the top level  [4.5.1.5]

;; 4.5.2 construction
(<Identifier>.<Identifier> <Binding>...) -> T
<Identifier>.<Identifier>  ; a variant with no fields
; 1. A variant with fields is built like a struct (4.4.2), with the
;    qualified name Enum.Variant as the callee.
; 2. A variant with no fields is written Enum.Variant, without parentheses.
; 3. A variant's name is always qualified with its enum's name.

(define switchbow : Weapon = (Weapon.Bow damage = 5.0))  ; built like a struct      [4.5.2.1]
(define legend : Weapon = (Weapon.Sword sword = excalibur))
    ; the variant Weapon.Sword holds the struct Sword  [4.5.2.3]
(define bare_hands : Weapon = Weapon.Fist)  ; a value  [4.5.1.4, 4.5.2.2]
(Signal.Beep)                               ; a Beep with pitch 440                 [4.5.2.1]
(Weapon.Fist)                               ; error: Weapon.Fist is not a function  [4.5.2.2]
(Bow damage = 5.0)                          ; error: write Weapon.Bow               [4.5.2.3]
(do
    (let person_1 : Person = (Person.Employee person = john))
    (let person_2 : Person = (Person.Hacker person = (Hacker
        skill = "backdoors"
        salary = 1.0)))
    (print (to_string person_2)))  ; prints person_2 as text (11.7.4)  [4.5.2.1]

;; 4.5.3 field access
; 1. A field F that every variant of an enum E has, with the same type T,
;    is a shared field of E. The enum definition creates an accessor
;    function E.F of type [E -> T], with the same scope as the enum.
; 2. name.F reads the shared field F of the enum value bound to name, an
;    identifier or a qualified name (2.3.1), whichever variant it is.
; 3. A field that is missing from some variant, or has different types in
;    different variants, is not shared. Read it by matching (6.8).
; 4. The rest of the field access rules, including that . only works after
;    an identifier or a qualified name, are those of 4.4.3.

(enum Shape
    Circle |name : string radius : f64|
    Square |name : string side : f64|)
; with ring : Shape, built as (Shape.Circle name = "ring" radius = 1.0)
ring.name         ; returns "ring"; name is in every variant                      [4.5.3.2]
Shape.name        ; type: [Shape -> string]                                       [4.5.3.1]
ring.radius       ; error: Shape.Square has no field radius, so match instead     [4.5.3.3]
Shape.Circle.name ; error: Shape.Circle is a variant, not a value                 [4.5.3.4]

;;; 4.6 doc comments
; A doc comment (1.1.3) documents a define, struct, enum, trait or impl, and
; every part of it. The compiler reads docs, checks that they are complete,
; and checks their examples.

;; 4.6.1 attachment
; 1. A doc is one or more ;: lines with nothing else between them.
; 2. A doc on the lines directly above a top-level define, struct, enum,
;    trait or impl documents it. Nothing may come between them, not even a
;    blank line or an ordinary comment, except the item's lint commands,
;    which come after the doc (4.7.1.3).
; 3. A field or variant on a line of its own may be documented in place:
;    by a doc on the lines directly above it, or by a doc that trails it on
;    its line and continues on the lines below. An entry in the body of a
;    trait or an impl (7.5) is documented in place too, by a doc on the
;    lines directly above it.
; 4. In one struct, or in one enum, the in-place docs are either all above
;    or all trailing. A trailing doc runs until the next field or variant.
;    why?: every ;: line between two fields then belongs to exactly one of
;    them, without a rule about columns
; 5. A field that shares its line with other fields is documented by a
;    field entry (4.6.2.10) in the doc of the struct or variant that holds
;    it. A variant that shares its line is documented by a variant entry
;    (4.6.2.11) in the enum's doc.
; 6. Any field or variant may be documented by an entry instead of in
;    place. Either way, it is documented exactly once.
; 7. A doc that documents nothing is an error: one above a let, an export,
;    an expression or a blank line, one that trails anything other than a
;    field or variant, or one at the end of a file. A ;: line after a lint
;    command of the same item is an error too (4.7.1.3).

;: The ratio of a circle's circumference to its diameter.
(define PI : f64 = 3.14)  ; the doc directly above documents PI  [4.6.1.2]
;: Someone on the payroll.
(struct Staff |
    ;: Full legal name.
    name : string
    ;: Age in whole years.
    age : u32
    ;: The department they work in.
    ;: default: not assigned to one yet
    dept : string = "unknown"
    ;: Yearly pay, in millions.
    salary : f64|)  ; every field documented above it  [4.6.1.3]
;: Something a player can attack with.
(enum Arm
    Blade |sword : Sword|  ;: A bladed weapon.
                           ;: field sword: the blade's stats
    Bow   |damage : f64|   ;: A ranged weapon.
                           ;: field damage: damage per arrow
    Fist)                  ;: No weapon at all.
    ; every variant documented by a trailing doc; the variant's fields share
    ; its line, so they are field entries  [4.6.1.3, 4.6.1.5]
;: A point on the grid.
;: field x: the column, counted from the left
;: field y: the row, counted from the top
(struct Cell |x : i32 y : i32|)  ; x and y share a line, so they are entries  [4.6.1.5]
;: A size on the screen.
(struct Extent |
    ;: Pixels across.
    width : u32
    height : u32|)  ;: Pixels down.
    ; error: Extent mixes docs above fields with trailing docs  [4.6.1.4]
;: A size on the screen.
;: field width: pixels across
(struct Span |
    ;: Pixels across.
    width : u32
    ;: Pixels down.
    height : u32|)  ; error: width is documented twice  [4.6.1.6]
;: How many so far.
(let count : u32 = 0)       ; error: a doc may not document a let  [4.6.1.7]
(define LIMIT : u32 = 10)   ;: The most allowed.
    ; error: a doc may only trail a field or a variant  [4.6.1.7]

;; 4.6.2 entries
; 1. A doc starts with its summary, the text before its first entry. The
;    summary is required.
; 2. An entry is a ;: line that starts with an entry word in lowercase,
;    then a name for the words that take one, then a :, then the entry's
;    text. The text continues on the lines below, up to the next entry or
;    the end of the doc. Any other line is text: in a summary, "Returns the
;    first item" is not an entry.
; 3. type T: describes the type parameter T, from a forall list (7.3), a
;    type header (7.2) or a trait header (7.5.1).
; 4. param x: describes the parameter x. param 1: describes the first
;    parameter of a function define whose value is not a fn literal
;    (4.6.3.2) or of a required method (4.6.7.3), param 2: the second, and
;    so on.
; 5. default x: describes what leaving out the parameter or field x means.
;    In the in-place doc of a field, it is written default:, with no name.
;    Where parameters go by position, it is written default 1:, default 2:,
;    matching param 1:.
; 6. returns: describes the value a function returns.
; 7. errors: describes when a function returns a Result.Error, and what
;    the error holds.
; 8. panics: describes when a function panics (8.9). A function doc with
;    no panics: says the function never panics. panics: never says the
;    same, and is optional.
; 9. example: is followed by an example on the lines below (4.6.9).
; 10. field x: describes the field x. In an enum's doc, the field x of the
;     variant V is written field V.x:.
; 11. variant V: describes the variant V.
; 12. law: states a property that every impl of a trait must keep, such as
;     "area is never negative". It appears only in a trait's doc (4.6.7).
;     The compiler does not check laws: an impl that breaks one is a bug in
;     the impl, as with panics: (4.6.3.6).
;     why: laws are the contract that makes generic code over a trait
;     correct, so they get an entry that tools and readers can find
; 13. No summary or entry may be empty. An example: needs at least one line
;     of code.
; 14. Each name has at most one entry of each word. returns:, errors: and
;     panics: appear at most once. law: and example: may appear any number
;     of times.
; 15. Entries come in this order: type, law:, param and default, variant
;     and field, returns:, errors:, panics:, then example:. Entries of one
;     word follow the order of the declaration. A default entry comes
;     directly after the entry of its parameter or field. In the doc of a
;     method inside an impl, which has no param entries (4.6.8.3), default
;     entries come after the summary, in parameter order.
; 16. An entry that names something that does not exist, or a word that
;     does not apply to what the doc documents (4.6.3 to 4.6.8), is an
;     error.

;: Adds y to x.
;: param x: the starting value
;: param y: how much to add
;: default y: adds 5
;: returns: the sum
;: panics: never
;: example:
;:   (add_some 2 3)  ; returns 5
(define add_some = {fn |x : u32 y : u32 = 5 -> u32| (+ x y)})  ; a complete doc  [4.6.2.4, 4.6.2.5, 4.6.2.8]
;: Adds one to a number.
;: param x: the number
;: returns: one more than x
;: example:
;:   (bump 1)  ; returns 2
(define bump = {fn |x : u32 -> u32| (+ x 1_u32)})  ; no panics:, so bump never panics  [4.6.2.8]
;: param n: the number
;: returns: n doubled
;: example:
;:   (twice_of 2)  ; returns 4
(define twice_of = {fn |n : u32 -> u32| (* n 2_u32)})  ; error: the doc has no summary  [4.6.2.1]
;: Doubles a number.
;: returns: n doubled
;: param n: the number
;: example:
;:   (doubled 2)  ; returns 4
(define doubled = {fn |n : u32 -> u32| (* n 2_u32)})  ; error: param n comes before returns:  [4.6.2.15]
;: Adds one to a number.
;: param n: the number
;: returns: one more than n
;: example:
;:   (plus_one 1)  ; returns 2
(define plus_one = {fn |x : u32 -> u32| (+ x 1_u32)})
    ; error: plus_one has no parameter n, and x is not documented  [4.6.2.16]
;: Adds one to a number.
;: param x:
;: returns: one more than x
;: example:
;:   (succ 1)  ; returns 2
(define succ = {fn |x : u32 -> u32| (+ x 1_u32)})  ; error: param x is empty  [4.6.2.13]

;; 4.6.3 function defines
; 1. A define whose type is a function type is a function define. Its doc
;    needs a summary, a type entry for each type parameter in its forall
;    list, a param entry for each parameter, a default entry for each
;    parameter with a default, returns:, errors: when its output type is a
;    Result, panics: when it can panic (4.6.2.8), and at least one
;    example:.
; 2. When the define's value is not a fn literal, its parameters have no
;    names, and their entries are written param 1:, param 2:, and so on,
;    in the order of the function type's inputs.
; 3. The name of a named fn (5.4.3), and the bindings inside the body, take
;    no entries.
; 4. main is a function define like any other, with no exceptions.
; 5. returns: is required even when the output type is Unit. Write
;    returns: ().
; 6. The compiler does not check panics: against the body. It is the
;    author's word, written by the style of 4.6.11.
;    why?: whether a function can panic depends on every function it calls,
;    and the compiler cannot always tell

;: Returns the first item of a list, or a fallback when the list is empty.
;: type T: the type of the items
;: param xs: the list to read
;: param fallback: what to return when xs is empty
;: returns: the item at index 0, or fallback
;: example:
;:   (head_or [i32] (list 4 5 6) 0)  ; returns 4
;: example:
;:   (head_or [i32] (list [i32]) 0)  ; returns 0
(define head_or = {fn [forall T] |xs : [List T] fallback : T -> T|
    (match (get xs 0)
        (Option.Some value = first) => first
        ^Option.None                => fallback)})  ; every part is documented  [4.6.3.1]
;: Adds two u32s.
;: param 1: the first number
;: param 2: the second number
;: returns: their sum
;: example:
;:   (sum_u32 2 3)  ; returns 5
(define sum_u32 = + [u32])  ; not a fn literal, so parameters go by position  [4.6.3.2]
;: Returns n factorial.
;: param n: the number to take the factorial of
;: returns: the product of 1 to n, or 1 when n is 0
;: example:
;:   (factorial 5)  ; returns 120
(define factorial = {fn fact |n : u64 -> u64|
    (if (== n 0_u64) 1 (* n (fact (- n 1_u64))))})  ; fact takes no entry  [4.6.3.3]
;: Prints a greeting.
;: returns: ()
;: example:
;:   (main)  ; prints: hello
(define main = {fn |-> Unit| (print "hello")})  ; main is documented like any function  [4.6.3.4, 4.6.3.5]
;: Wraps n in an Ok.
;: param n: the number to wrap
;: returns: n, as an Ok
;: example:
;:   (always_ok 3)  ; returns (Result.Ok [u32] [string] value = 3)
(define always_ok = {fn |n : u32 -> [Result u32 string]| (Result.Ok [u32] [string] value = n)})
    ; error: the output is a Result, so errors: is required  [4.6.3.1]
;: Returns its input.
;: param n: any number
;: returns: n
(define same = {fn |n : u32 -> u32| n})  ; error: the doc needs an example  [4.6.3.1]

;; 4.6.4 value defines
; 1. A define whose type is not a function type is a value define. Its doc
;    needs a summary.
; 2. It may have examples. Any other entry is an error.

;: The most items a basket holds.
(define BASKET_SIZE : u32 = 12)  ; a summary is all a value needs  [4.6.4.1]
;: The first few primes.
;: example:
;:   (get FIRST_PRIMES 0)  ; returns (Option.Some [i32] value = 2)
(define FIRST_PRIMES = (list 2 3 5))  ; an example is allowed  [4.6.4.2]
;: How fast light goes, in meters per second.
;: returns: 299792458
(define LIGHT_SPEED : u64 = 299792458)  ; error: a value define takes no returns:  [4.6.4.2]

;; 4.6.5 structs
; 1. A struct's doc needs a summary and a type entry for each parameter in
;    its type header. Each of its fields needs a doc, in place or by a field
;    entry (4.6.1).
; 2. A field with a default also needs a default entry: default: in its
;    in-place doc, or default x: after its field entry.
; 3. The struct's doc may have examples. Any other entry is an error.

;: Two values of any types.
;: type A: the type of the first value
;: type B: the type of the second value
;: field first: the first value
;: field second: the second value
(struct [Duo A B] |first : A second : B|)  ; [4.6.5.1]
;: A sound to play.
;: field pitch: how high, in hertz
;: default pitch: concert A
;: field length: how long, in milliseconds
(struct Tone |pitch : u32 = 440 length : u32|)  ; a default, documented by an entry  [4.6.5.2]
;: A sound to play.
;: field pitch: how high, in hertz
;: field length: how long, in milliseconds
(struct Note |pitch : u32 = 440 length : u32|)  ; error: pitch has a default; add default pitch:  [4.6.5.2]
;: A rectangle's size.
;: field width: pixels across
(struct Area |width : u32 height : u32|)  ; error: height is not documented  [4.6.5.1]

;; 4.6.6 enums
; 1. An enum's doc needs a summary and a type entry for each parameter in
;    its type header. Each of its variants, and each field of each variant,
;    needs a doc, in place or by an entry (4.6.1).
; 2. A variant's in-place doc is a summary, then entries for its fields
;    that are not documented in place.
; 3. A variant field with a default needs a default entry, as a struct
;    field does (4.6.5.2).
; 4. The enum's doc may have examples. Any other entry is an error.

;: A traffic light's color.
;: variant Red: stop
;: variant Amber: get ready to stop
;: variant Green: go
(enum Light Red Amber Green)  ; variants on one line are documented by entries  [4.6.6.1]
;: A mark drawn on the grid.
(enum Mark
    Dot   |at : Cell|              ;: A single point.
                                   ;: field at: where it is
    Line  |from : Cell to : Cell|  ;: A straight line.
                                   ;: field from: one end
                                   ;: field to: the other end
    Blank)                         ;: Nothing drawn.
    ; [4.6.6.1, 4.6.6.2]
;: A sound or its absence.
;: variant Beep: a tone
;: field Beep.pitch: how high, in hertz
;: variant Hush: no tone
(enum Chime
    Beep |pitch : u32 = 440|
    Hush)  ; error: Beep.pitch has a default; add default Beep.pitch:  [4.6.6.3]
;: A choice of one of two values.
;: variant Left: the first kind
;: variant Right: the second kind
(enum [Fork L R]
    Left  |value : L|
    Right |value : R|)  ; error: L, R, Left.value and Right.value are not documented  [4.6.6.1]

;; 4.6.7 traits
; 1. A trait's doc needs a summary and a type entry for each parameter in
;    its header. It may have law: entries (4.6.2.12) and examples. Any
;    other entry is an error. Supertraits (7.5.5) take no entry, since the
;    header already names them.
; 2. Every method in the trait's body needs a doc, in place (4.6.1.3). A
;    method's doc follows the rules for a function define (4.6.3), with the
;    differences of rules 3 to 5. The trait's parameters are documented by
;    the trait's doc, so a method's doc has type entries only for type
;    parameters of its own (7.5.2.4).
; 3. A required method has no fn, so its parameters go by position:
;    param 1:, param 2: (4.6.2.4). An auto-implemented method names its
;    parameters from its fn.
; 4. Examples are optional on a required method, since the trait's module
;    may have no impl to run them against. They are required on an
;    auto-implemented method, as on any function.
; 5. A parameter that the trait marks optional (7.5.2.8) needs a default
;    entry, default x: or default 1:, that says what leaving it out means.
;    Each impl keeps to that meaning.
; 6. An example in a trait's doc runs in the trait's module (4.6.9.2), so
;    it can only use impls that module can find (7.5.6.4).
; 7. Bounds (7.5.4) take no entry anywhere: a generic function's type entry
;    covers its parameter, and its bounds are in the signature.

; with (struct Square |side : f64|) in the same module
;: Things with an area.
;: type T: the type of the shape
;: law: (area s) is never negative
(trait [Shape T]
    ;: Returns a shape's area.
    ;: param 1: the shape
    ;: returns: its area, in square units
    area : [T -> f64]

    ;: Returns the area of two copies of a shape.
    ;: param s: the shape
    ;: returns: twice its area
    ;: example:
    ;:   (double_area (Square side = 2.0))  ; returns 8.0
    double_area = {fn |s : T -> f64| (* (area s) 2.0)})
    ; a law, a required method by position, and an auto-implemented method
    ; with its example  [4.6.7.1, 4.6.7.3, 4.6.7.4]
;: Things that can grow.
;: type T: the type that grows
(trait [Grow T]
    ;: Returns a value grown by some amount.
    ;: param 1: the value to grow
    ;: param 2: how much to grow it by
    ;: default 2: grows by one unit
    ;: returns: the grown value
    grow : [T f64 = _ -> T])  ; an optional parameter needs its default entry  [4.6.7.5]
;: Things with a name.
(trait [Named T]
    ;: Returns the name.
    ;: param 1: the thing to name
    ;: returns: its name
    name_of : [T -> string])  ; error: the doc needs type T:  [4.6.7.1]
;: Things with a weight.
;: type T: the type that is weighed
(trait [Weigh T]
    weigh : [T -> f64])  ; error: the method weigh is not documented  [4.6.7.2]

;; 4.6.8 impls
; 1. An impl's doc is optional, and an impl with no doc gets no warning
;    (4.6.10.1). If it has one, the doc needs a summary and may have
;    examples, like a value define (4.6.4). Any other entry is an error.
;    why: the trait already says what each method does; an impl's doc is
;    the natural place for examples that run, since the impl's module has
;    the impl
; 2. A doc on a method inside an impl is optional too. If present, it has
;    a summary, a default x: entry for each default the impl gives (the
;    value belongs to the impl, 7.5.3.7), and may have examples.
; 3. param and returns: entries inside an impl would repeat the trait, so
;    they are errors.

;: A square's area is its side squared.
;: example:
;:   (area (Square side = 3.0))  ; returns 9.0
(impl [Shape Square]
    area = {fn |s : Square -> f64| (* s.side s.side)})  ; an impl's doc, with an example  [4.6.8.1]
(struct Disc |r : f64|)
(impl [Shape Disc]
    area = {fn |d : Disc -> f64| (* 3.14 (* d.r d.r))})  ; no doc, and no warning  [4.6.8.1]
(impl [Grow Square]
    ;: Grows a square's side.
    ;: default by: one unit longer
    grow = {fn |s : Square by : f64 = 1.0 -> Square| (Square side = (+ s.side by))})
    ; a method doc inside an impl: a summary and the default  [4.6.8.2]
(impl [Grow Disc]
    ;: Grows a disc's radius.
    ;: param d: the disc
    ;: default by: one unit wider
    grow = {fn |d : Disc by : f64 = 1.0 -> Disc| (Disc r = (+ d.r by))})
    ; error: param repeats the trait's doc  [4.6.8.3]

;; 4.6.9 examples
; 1. An example is the code on the lines after its example: entry, up to the
;    next entry or the end of the doc. Its ;: is followed by two spaces
;    more than the entry's (1.5.10).
; 2. Each example is its own test: its lines are the items of one do, with
;    every top-level name of the module in scope, private ones included.
; 3. An example's trailing comments state outcomes as in 0.3.2. An example
;    may show a mistake: an error: outcome means the code must not compile.
; 4. Every example is compiled with its module. One that does not compile
;    when no error: says so, or whose type:, error: or warning: outcome is
;    wrong, is an error. A wrong returns, prints: or panics: outcome is
;    found when the examples are run, and is an error then (11.1.2).

;: Returns the larger of two numbers.
;: param a: one number
;: param b: the other number
;: returns: whichever of a and b is larger, or a when they are equal
;: example:
;:   (larger 2 7)  ; returns 7
;: example:
;:   (larger 2 "7")  ; error: b is an i32, not a string
(define larger = {fn |a : i32 b : i32 -> i32| (if (>= a b) a b)})  ; one example shows a mistake  [4.6.9.3]
;: Doubles a number.
;: param n: the number to double
;: returns: n times 2
;: example:
;:   (double 4)  ; returns 9
(define double = {fn |n : u32 -> u32| (* n 2_u32)})  ; error: the example returns 8, not 9  [4.6.9.4]

;; 4.6.10 missing and incomplete docs
; 1. A define, struct, enum or trait with no doc at all, on it or on any of
;    its fields, variants or methods, is a warning (lint missing_doc,
;    4.7.3). An impl with no doc is not (4.6.8.1).
; 2. Once any of its docs is there, the declaration must be documented in
;    full. Breaking any rule of 4.6.1 to 4.6.9 is an error.
;    why?: a missing doc marks code that is not documented yet, which is
;    normal in a draft; a doc that is there but partial or wrong misleads
;    whoever reads it
; 3. Private declarations follow the same rules as exported ones (9.6).

(define triple = {fn |n : u32 -> u32| (* n 3_u32)})  ; warning: triple has no doc  [4.6.10.1]
(struct Gap |
    ;: Pixels across.
    width : u32|)  ; error: Gap has a field doc, so it needs a summary too  [4.6.10.2]
;: Returns n tripled.
;: param n: the number to triple
(define treble = {fn |n : u32 -> u32| (* n 3_u32)})
    ; error: the doc needs returns: and an example  [4.6.10.2]
(trait [Shape T] area : [T -> f64])  ; warning: Shape has no doc  [4.6.10.1]

;; 4.6.11 style
; The recommended way to write a doc. None of it is checked.
; 1. A summary is one sentence, with a capital and a period. A function's
;    starts with what it does: "Returns ...", "Prints ...". A type's or a
;    value's says what it is: "A sound to play."
; 2. Entry text starts in lowercase and ends without a period, unless it
;    holds more than one sentence.
; 3. returns: says what the value means, not its type, which the signature
;    already shows.
; 4. default says what leaving the parameter or field out means, not only
;    the value, which the code already shows.
; 5. panics: lists each cause as a "when ..." clause, separated by ;. A
;    function that never panics leaves panics: out. It counts panics in
;    the functions it calls, as far as the author knows.
; 6. errors: lists each cause as a "when ..." clause, and says what the
;    error holds.
; 7. Bad input is a Result.Error, documented under errors:. A panic is for
;    a mistake in the program itself, such as a broken assumption.
;    why?: a caller can handle an error, but not a panic (8.9)
; 8. An example shows typical use first. Edge cases and mistakes come in
;    later examples.

;: Returns the item at index i.
;: type T: the type of the items
;: param xs: the list to read
;: param i: the index, counted from 0
;: returns: the item at i
;: panics: when i is past the end of xs
;: example:
;:   (item_at [i32] (list 4 5 6) 1)  ; returns 5
(define item_at = {fn [forall T] |xs : [List T] i : u32 -> T|
    (match (get xs i)
        (Option.Some value = x) => x
        ^Option.None            => (panic "index past the end"))})
    ; follows 4.6.11.5, but by 4.6.11.7 it would be better to return an [Option T]  [4.6.11.5, 4.6.11.7]

;;; 4.7 lint commands
; Every warning has a name, called its lint. A lint command (1.1.4) changes
; how a lint is reported: for one item, or for a whole module.

;; 4.7.1 form and attachment
;! <Level> <Lint> <Argument>?   ; for the item below
;!! <Level> <Lint> <Argument>?  ; for the whole module
<Level>  ; allow, warn or deny
<Lint>   ; the name of a lint (4.7.3)
; 1. A ;! comment is a lint command for one item. It attaches by the rules
;    for a doc (4.6.1): on the lines directly above the item, with nothing
;    between, or trailing a field or variant.
; 2. An item is anything a doc can attach to: a define, struct, enum, trait
;    or impl, an entry in a trait or impl body, or a field or variant. A ;!
;    that attaches to nothing is an error, like a doc (4.6.1.7).
; 3. When an item has a doc and lint commands, the doc comes first. The ;!
;    lines come directly after the doc's last ;: line and directly above
;    the item. A ;: line after a ;! line is an error.
;    why: the summary stays the first line a reader sees, and a doc is
;    still ;: lines with nothing else between them (4.6.1.1)
; 4. A ;! applies to every warning inside its item: in a define's body, a
;    struct's fields, every entry of a trait or impl. A warning inside an
;    expression is silenced by a ;! on the item that holds it.
;    why: lets and expressions cannot take a doc (4.6.1.7), so they cannot
;    take a ;! either; covering the whole item reaches every warning
;    without new attachment rules
;    - Cost: a ;! on a large item silences more than the one site it was
;      written for. Keep items small, or use the lint's argument where it
;      has one.
; 5. A ;!! comment is a lint command for the whole module. It never
;    attaches to anything. Every ;!! line comes before the module's first
;    form. Blank lines and ordinary comments may come before it or between
;    ;!! lines. A ;!! after the first form is an error. A doc for the
;    first form still goes directly above that form, after the ;!! lines.
;    why: a module's lint settings are all in one place, before any code
;    they affect, without forcing them above a file's header comment
; 6. The levels are allow, which silences the lint, warn, which is the
;    default for every lint, and deny, which makes it an error.
; 7. Some lints take an argument after their name. The latent-overlap
;    lints take a goal (7.5.8.5).
; 8. An unknown lint is an error, and so is an argument that does not
;    resolve.
;    why: a mistyped allow must not silently do nothing

; as a whole module:
; geometry helpers
;!! allow layout
;!! deny shadowing
;: The ratio of a circle's circumference to its diameter.
(define PI : f64 = 3.14)    ; two module-wide commands, then the first form's doc  [4.7.1.5]
;: Returns n plus one.
;: param n: the number
;: returns: n plus 1
;: example:
;:   (bump 1)  ; returns 2
;! allow unused_binding
(define bump = {fn |n : u32 -> u32| (do
    (let scratch = 5_u32)   ; no warning: silenced by the ;! on bump  [4.7.1.4]
    (+ n 1_u32))})          ; the doc first, then the ;!              [4.7.1.3]
;!! allow naming            ; error: a ;!! comes before the first form  [4.7.1.5]
;! allow unused_binding
;: Returns n minus one.
(define dip = {fn |n : u32 -> u32| (- n 1_u32)})  ; error: a ;: line after a ;! line  [4.7.1.3]
;! allow unused_bindings
(define dop = {fn |n : u32 -> u32| n})  ; error: unused_bindings is not a lint  [4.7.1.8]
;! allow naming

(define dup = {fn |n : u32 -> u32| n})  ; error: the ;! attaches to nothing  [4.7.1.2]

;; 4.7.2 which command applies
; 1. The closest command wins. A ;! on an item beats a ;!! in the module,
;    which beats the default. When items nest, the innermost wins: a ;! on
;    a trait entry, field or variant beats a ;! on the trait, struct or
;    enum around it.
; 2. Two commands for one lint at the same place are an error.
; 3. An unused lint command is a warning (lint unused_lint). A ;!! is
;    unused when it silences or changes nothing in its module. A ;! is
;    unused when it silences or changes nothing inside its item. Both are
;    checked in the module itself.
; 4. The one exception to rule 3: a ;! for a latent-overlap lint on an impl
;    (7.5.8.5) is unused when it silences nothing anywhere in the program.
;    It is the one warning reported after every module is checked.
;    why: the two impls may sit in modules that do not import each other,
;    so the pair first meets in a module downstream; checking only the
;    impl's own module would flag a ;! that is doing its job
;    - Cost: deleting a downstream module can make a ;! upstream start
;      warning.
; 5. Every warning names its lint in its message, as in
;    warning[unused_binding]: scratch is never used, so the ;! to write is
;    always shown. The wording of the messages is open (11.8.8).

;!! allow unused_binding
;! warn unused_binding
(define near = {fn |n : u32 -> u32| (do
    (let spare = 1_u32)  ; warning: spare is never used; the ;! beats the ;!!  [4.7.2.1]
    n)})
;! allow naming
;! deny naming
(define Far = {fn |n : u32 -> u32| n})  ; error: two commands for naming  [4.7.2.2]
;! allow unreachable
(define calm = {fn |n : u32 -> u32| n})
    ; warning: the ;! silences nothing (unused_lint)  [4.7.2.3]

;; 4.7.3 the lints
; Each warning in this file belongs to exactly one lint.
;   spacing                 whitespace around ::, or after .. before a name
;                           (1.2.10)
;   leading_zero            a number with a leading zero, such as 007
;                           (1.3.1.10)
;   naming                  naming conventions, for names (1.4.4) and path
;                           segments (9.3.4)
;   layout                  the recommended layout (1.5)
;   declaration_value       a declaration's () used as a value, including
;                           as the last item of a do (2.5.1.3, 5.3.4)
;   discarded_value         a discarded value that is not Unit or Never
;                           (3.2.2)
;   unreachable             code after a Never expression (3.2.5)
;   uninhabited             a type with no finite value (3.7.6)
;   shadowing               a local that hides a top-level name, a built-in
;                           or prelude name, an alias or a trait method
;                           (4.1.8, 5.4.1.6, 7.5.2.7, 9.4.9, 11.8.5)
;   unused_binding          a binding that is never read, including a named
;                           fn's name (4.1.10, 5.4.3.7, 5.7.1.6)
;   missing_doc             a declaration with no doc (4.6.10.1)
;   unused_lint             a lint command that changes nothing (4.7.2.3)
;   recur_not_tail          recur not in tail position (5.9.6)
;   unpinned_name           a name in a pattern that compares without a pin
;                           (6.2.2, 6.4.5)
;   latent_overlap          two impls kept apart only by a goal with no
;                           type parameter (7.5.8.1)
;   latent_overlap_generic  two impls kept apart only by goals over type
;                           parameters (7.5.8.1)
;   explicit_default_alias  a mod alias written out when it is the default
;                           (9.4.5)
; 1. Every warning added to the language gets a lint of its own.
; 2. 2.5.1.3 and 5.3.4 describe one warning seen from two rules, so they
;    share declaration_value.
; 3. A local named like a prelude name (11.8.5), one that hides an alias
;    (9.4.9), and a parameter that hides a top-level name (5.4.1.6) all
;    warn by 4.1.8, so they are shadowing, not lints of their own.
;    why: the list is short and fixed, and since a ;! covers its whole
;    item, ;! allow works for every warning
;    why: a lint is named by a word, not a rule number, so renumbering
;    this file breaks no program

;;;; 5 expressions

;;; 5.1 literals

;; 5.1.1 number literals
; 1. A literal with a type suffix has that type: 200_u8 is a u8 (1.3.1.6).
; 2. A literal without a suffix takes a type written where it appears:
;    - the annotation of its define or let
;    - the declared type of the parameter or field it is bound to, in a call,
;      a constructor, a recur, or a loop's default
;    - the output type of the fn or loop whose value it is
;    - a type argument (7.4), which makes the parameter types it fills
;      written
;    A written type also reaches into the value positions of do, if and
;    match: the last item, both branches, and every arm.
; 3. Anywhere else, an integer literal is an i32, or an i64 if it is too
;    large for an i32, and a float literal is an f64. An integer literal
;    too large for an i64 is an error there; it needs a suffix or a written
;    type, such as _i128 or _ibig. This includes an argument whose
;    parameter type is a type parameter that the call does not give
;    (7.4.10): a literal never takes its type from the other arguments. In
;    (+ x 1) with x : u8, the 1 is an i32, so the call is an error. A
;    parameter whose type is written in the signature, such as get's u32
;    index, is covered by rule 2.
;    why?: a literal's type is always written in the code or fixed, never
;    worked out from its neighbors
; 4. A float literal never takes an integer type. An integer literal takes a
;    float type from a float suffix (1.3.1.7). Whether an unsuffixed integer
;    literal can take a float type from where it is used is open (11.5.2).
; 5. A literal is never a value of a type parameter. Inside a generic body,
;    a literal where a T is expected keeps its fixed type, which is an
;    error. Generic code gets 0 and 1 from zero and one (8.2.6), and any
;    other constant by casting a suffixed literal under a bound (8.6.7).
;    why: a generic body is checked once, before T is known (7.5.4.4), and
;    whether 300 fits a T is known only for each T
; Refining these rules is open (11.5.12). Sugar that relaxes them may come
; later (11.7.15).

(let small : u8 = 200)             ; 200 is a u8, from the annotation              [5.1.1.2]
(+ small 1)                        ; error: T is u8 from small but i32 from 1; write 1_u8  [5.1.1.3]
(+ small 1_u8)                     ; returns 201, a u8                             [5.1.1.1]
(+ [u8] small 1)                   ; returns 201; [u8] makes + take u8s (7.4)      [5.1.1.2]
(get (list 4 5) 1)                 ; the 1 is a u32: get's index is written as u32  [5.1.1.2]
(let ratio : f32 = 0.5)            ; 0.5 is an f32                                 [5.1.1.2]
(let chosen : u8 = (if true 1 2))  ; 1 and 2 are u8s, through the if               [5.1.1.2]
(define wide = {fn |-> u64| 7})    ; 7 is a u64, the fn's output type              [5.1.1.2]
42                                 ; type: i32                                     [5.1.1.3]
3000000000                         ; type: i64, too large for an i32               [5.1.1.3]
100000000000000000000              ; error: too large for an i64; write a suffix   [5.1.1.3]
100000000000000000000_i128         ; type: i128                                    [5.1.1.1]
(let huge : ubig = 100000000000000000000)  ; 10^20 is a ubig, from the annotation  [5.1.1.2]
2.5                                ; type: f64                                     [5.1.1.3]
42_u8                              ; type: u8                                      [5.1.1.1]
(let tiny : u8 = 2.5)              ; error: a float literal is never a u8          [5.1.1.4]
{fn [forall T] |x : T -> T| 0}     ; error: the body is an i32, not a T            [5.1.1.5]

;; 5.1.2 fancy strings
; 1. A fancy string is a function value. Each distinct ${name} is a
;    parameter, in order of first appearance. A call returns the text with
;    each ${name} replaced by its argument.
; 2. When the same name appears more than once, it is one parameter.
; 3. Every parameter is a string. Convert other values with to_string (8.6).
; 4. ${name} names a parameter. It never reads a binding called name from
;    the surrounding scope.
; 5. A fancy string with no ${} has type [-> string].
;    why: the fn it stands for is "extremely messy", which is why the
;    sugar hides it

`name: ${name}, number: ${num}`  ; type: [string string -> string]  [5.1.2.1]
; is equivalent to
{fn |name : string num : string -> string| (+ (+ "name: " name) (+ ", number: " num))}
(`name: ${name}, number: ${num}` "jane" "22")  ; returns "name: jane, number: 22"       [5.1.2.1]
(`${b} before ${a}` "1" "2")                   ; returns "1 before 2"; b appears first  [5.1.2.1]
`${a} and ${a} again`                          ; type: [string -> string]               [5.1.2.2]
(`${a} and ${a} again` "x")                    ; returns "x and x again"                [5.1.2.2]
(`count: ${n}` 5)                              ; error: parameters are strings          [5.1.2.3]
(`count: ${n}` (to_string 5))                  ; returns "count: 5"                     [5.1.2.3]
(do
    (let name : string = "jane")
    (`hi ${name}`))  ; error: missing argument; ${name} is a parameter  [5.1.2.4]
`hello`    ; type: [-> string]  [5.1.2.5]
(`hello`)  ; returns "hello"    [5.1.2.5]

;;; 5.2 calls
(<Callee T> <Value>...) -> T
{<Callee T> <Value>...} -> T  ; when T is a function type
; 1. A call evaluates its callee and its arguments, then applies the callee
;    to the arguments. The order of evaluation is open (11.5.1).
; 2. Arguments bind to parameters from left to right. A function has a
;    fixed number of parameters, and passing more is a compile error.
; 3. Trailing parameters that have defaults may be left out, and take their
;    defaults. Leaving out a parameter with no default is a compile error.
; 4. No name has more than one function behind it: there are no overloads.
;    An operator that works on several types, such as +, is one generic
;    function, the method of a trait (7.5.2). A call settles its type
;    parameters from its arguments (7.4), then uses the impl for those
;    types (7.5.3).

; with x, y, z : i32
(+ x y)        ; x + y                                      [5.2.1]
(+ (+ x y) z)  ; x + y + z                                  [5.2.1]
(+ x y z)      ; error: + takes two arguments               [5.2.2]
(shl 5)        ; returns 10; n takes its default 1          [5.2.3]
(shl 5 2)      ; returns 20                                 [5.2.3]
(- 5)          ; error: - takes two arguments; write (neg 5) (8.2.2)  [5.2.3]
(+ 1 2)        ; + at i32, using i32's impl; returns 3      [5.2.4]
(+ "a" "b")    ; + at string, using string's impl; returns "ab"  [5.2.4]
(define pick = {fn |x : u32 = 1 y : u32 -> u32| (+ x y)})
(pick 5 6)     ; returns 11                                 [5.2.2]
(pick 5)       ; error: 5 binds to x, and y has no default  [5.2.3]
(define always_one = {fn |-> u32| 1})
(always_one)    ; returns 1; a call with no arguments       [5.2.1]
(always_one 1)  ; error: always_one takes no arguments      [5.2.2]

;;; 5.3 do
(do <Value>... <Value T>) -> T
{do <Value>... <Value F>} -> F
(do) -> Unit
; 1. A do block evaluates its items in order.
; 2. The block's value is the value of its last item.
; 3. A let inside the block binds for the items after it, and the binding
;    is dropped when the block ends (4.1).
; 4. Every item but the last is discarded. Discarding a value that is not
;    Unit or Never warns (3.2.2; lint discarded_value). A declaration as
;    the last item warns instead, since its () is used (2.5.1.3; lint
;    declaration_value). Items after an item of type Never are unreachable,
;    and warn (3.2.5; lint unreachable).
; 5. An empty block returns ().

(do
    (let x : u32 = 2)  ; x is in scope from here on           [5.3.3]
    (let y : u32 = 3)  ; x and y are in scope                 [5.3.3]
    (+ x y))           ; returns 5, then x and y are dropped  [5.3.2]
(do
    (print "one")
    (print "two"))  ; prints: one  [5.3.1]
                    ;         two
(do
    (+ 1 2)         ; warning: the i32 is discarded  [5.3.4]
    (print "done")  ; a discarded (), no warning     [5.3.4]
    3)              ; returns 3
(do (let x : u32 = 1))
    ; returns (), the value of the let; warning: the let's () is used;
    ; warning: x is never used  [5.3.2, 2.5.1.3, 4.1.10]
(do)                    ; returns ()                        [5.3.5]
{do
    (let n : u32 = 10)
    {fn |x : u32 -> u32| (+ x n)}}  ; type: [u32 -> u32]; a function-valued block  [2.4.2.1]

;;; 5.4 fn

;; 5.4.1 anonymous functions
{fn <Identifier>? <Forall>? <Signature> <Value T>} -> F
; The <Identifier> names the function (5.4.3). The <Forall> makes it generic
; (7.3).
; 1. fn creates a function value, so it is always written in { } (2.4.2.5).
; 2. The <Signature> lists the parameters and, after ->, the single output
;    type. The output type is required (2.5.2.1).
; 3. The parameters are bound in the body. The body can be any value,
;    function or not, and its type must be the output type.
; 4. The body may also use any binding in scope where the fn is written. The
;    function keeps those values alive (9.2.2). A top-level name is not
;    kept alive this way: the function refers to it directly, since
;    top-level values live for the whole program (9.2.4).
; 5. The type F of the fn is built from its parameter and output types:
;    |x : u32 y : u32 -> u32| gives [u32 u32 -> u32].
; 6. A parameter may shadow any local in scope where the fn is written
;    (4.1.5). When fns nest, the innermost parameter wins. A parameter that
;    hides a top-level or built-in name warns (4.1.8; lint shadowing).
; 7. Two parameters of one signature may not have the same name.

{fn |x : u32 y : u32 -> u32| (+ x y)}  ; a simple adder, like + on u32s
{                                   }  ; a fn is a function value, so it is wrapped in { }
 fn                                   ; it starts with the keyword fn
    |                      |          ; its parameters and output are wrapped in | |
     x : u32 y : u32                  ; each parameter is a name, a : and a type, with no separators
                     -> u32           ; -> and the one output type
                             (+ x y)  ; the body: what the function computes and returns

({fn |x : u32 y : u32 -> u32| (+ x y)} 2 3)  ; returns 5; a fn called directly         [5.4.1.1]
{fn |-> Unit| ()}                            ; type: [-> Unit]                         [5.4.1.5]
{fn |x : u32 -> u32| (+ x 1_u32)}            ; type: [u32 -> u32]                      [5.4.1.5]
{fn |x : u32 -> string| x}                   ; error: the body is a u32, not a string  [5.4.1.3]
{fn |x : u32| x}                             ; error: the output type is required      [5.4.1.2]
{fn |x : u32 -> [u32 -> u32]| {fn |y : u32 -> u32| y}}  ; a body that is a function    [5.4.1.3]
(define make_adder = {fn |x : u32 -> [u32 -> u32]|
    {fn |y : u32 -> u32| (+ x y)}})  ; the inner fn uses the outer x  [5.4.1.4]
({make_adder 10} 5)  ; returns 15; x = 10 lives on in the inner fn  [5.4.1.4]
(define inner_wins = {fn |x : u32 -> [u32 -> u32]|
    {fn |x : u32 -> u32| x}})  ; the inner x shadows the outer x  [5.4.1.6]
({inner_wins 10} 5)  ; returns 5; the innermost x wins            [5.4.1.6]
(do
    (let n : u32 = 1)
    ({fn |n : u32 -> u32| (* n 2_u32)} 4))  ; returns 8; the parameter n shadows the let  [5.4.1.6]
{fn |greeting : string -> string| greeting}
    ; warning: greeting hides a top-level name (4.1.8)  [5.4.1.6]
{fn |x : u32 x : u32 -> u32| x}  ; error: two parameters are named x  [5.4.1.7]

;; 5.4.2 default parameters
; 1. A parameter written name : Type = value has a default, used when a call
;    leaves it out (5.2.3).
; 2. A default may be a function value. Its fn brings a second pair of bars
;    into the signature, kept apart by its { } (2.6.3).
;    why: "bracketing allows this to be possible"
; 3. A default may use the parameters before it. As with let (4.1.3), each
;    parameter is visible from the next parameter on, so a default never
;    sees its own parameter or a later one.

(define adder_default = {fn |x : u32 y : u32 = 5 -> u32| (+ x y)})
(adder_default 2)    ; returns 7; y defaults to 5  [5.4.2.1]
(adder_default 2 3)  ; returns 5                   [5.4.2.1]
(adder_default)      ; error: x has no default     [5.2.3]
(define apply_or_inc = {fn |x : u32  f : [u32 -> u32] = {fn |y : u32 -> u32| (+ y 1_u32)} -> u32|
    (f x)})
(apply_or_inc 1)                  ; returns 2, using the default f  [5.4.2.2]
(apply_or_inc 1 {make_adder 10})  ; returns 11                      [5.4.2.2]
(define span = {fn |start : u32 stop : u32 = (+ start 10_u32) -> u32| (- stop start)})
(span 5)    ; returns 10; stop defaults to 15, using start  [5.4.2.3]
(span 5 7)  ; returns 2                                     [5.4.2.3]
{fn |x : u32 = y y : u32 = 1 -> u32| x}  ; error: y is not visible yet  [5.4.2.3]
{fn |x : u32 = x -> u32| x}              ; error: x is not visible in its own default  [5.4.2.3]

;; 5.4.3 named functions
; 1. In {fn name <Signature> <Value T>}, name is bound to the function
;    itself, and is visible only inside the body.
; 2. The name lets the body call the function itself.
;    why: factorial in the example below is not bound until after the fn is
;    evaluated, so without fact the body could not refer to it
; 3. The name is not bound outside the fn.
; 4. The name may not be the same as one of the fn's parameters.
;    why?: the parameter would hide the name, so the fn could not call
;    itself, and the name would be pointless
; 5. The name may shadow a binding outside the fn (4.1.5).
; 6. The name refers to the function being called. It is supplied to the
;    body on each call, not stored in the function value, so a named fn
;    does not refer to itself. An inner fn that captures the name only
;    refers outward, and returning the name just shares the function.
;    why: values stay acyclic (9.2.3), so reference counting never leaks;
;    nothing a program can observe changes
; 7. The name is a binding like any other: a named fn whose name is never
;    used warns (4.1.10; lint unused_binding). The fix is to drop the name.

(do
    (let factorial = {fn fact |n : u64 -> u64|
        (if (== n 0_u64)
            1
            (* n (fact (- n 1_u64))))})  ; fact is the fn itself  [5.4.3.1]
    (factorial 5))                       ; returns 120            [5.4.3.2]
(do
    (let broken = {fn |n : u64 -> u64|
        (if (== n 0_u64) 1 (* n (broken (- n 1_u64))))})
    (broken 5))  ; error: broken is not bound inside its own value  [5.4.3.2]
(do
    (let factorial = {fn fact |n : u64 -> u64| (if (== n 0_u64) 1 (* n (fact (- n 1_u64))))})
    (fact 5))  ; error: fact is only bound inside the fn  [5.4.3.3]
{fn n |n : u64 -> u64| n}  ; error: the fn's name n is also a parameter  [5.4.3.4]
(do
    (let fact = {fn fact |n : u64 -> u64| (if (== n 0_u64) 1 (* n (fact (- n 1_u64))))})
    (fact 5))  ; returns 120; inside the body, fact is the fn itself  [5.4.3.5]
(let make = {fn self |n : u64 -> [-> u64]|
    {fn |-> u64| ({self n})}})
    ; the inner fn captures self: it refers outward, so there is no cycle  [5.4.3.6]
{fn helper |n : u32 -> u32| (+ n 1_u32)}
    ; warning: helper is never used; drop the name  [5.4.3.7]

;;; 5.5 if
(if <Value bool> <Value T> <Value T>) -> T
{if <Value bool> <Value F> <Value F>} -> F
; 1. The condition is a <Value bool>.
; 2. When the condition is true, the if evaluates the first branch and
;    returns its value. Otherwise it evaluates and returns the second. The
;    other branch is never evaluated.
;    why: this avoids runtime panics 
; 3. Both branches have the same type T. A mismatch is a compile error, and
;    no choice of brackets fixes it. A Never branch fits any T (3.2.4).
; 4. The braced form is used exactly when T is a function type (2.4.2).

(if (== 5 2) 4 3)                        ; returns 3                                  [5.5.2]
(if true "yes" "no")                     ; returns "yes"                              [5.5.2]
(if (> 3 2) (print "big") (print "small"))  ; prints: big, and only that              [5.5.2]
(if 1 2 3)                               ; error: the condition must be a bool        [5.5.1]
(if true 5 2.5)                          ; error: the branches are an i32 and an f64  [5.5.3]
{if false `hello ${world_name}` "hello world!"}
    ; error: a [string -> string] and a string; neither ( ) nor { } fixes this        [5.5.3]
{if true {make_adder 1} {make_adder 2}}  ; type: [u32 -> u32]                         [5.5.4]
(if true {make_adder 1} {make_adder 2})
    ; error: the branches are functions; write {if ...}  [5.5.4]

;;; 5.6 when and unless
(when <Value bool> <Value Unit>) -> Unit
(unless <Value bool> <Value Unit>) -> Unit
; 1. when evaluates its body only if the condition is true.
; 2. unless evaluates its body only if the condition is false.
; 3. The body is a single <Value Unit>, and the form returns (). There is no
;    else branch.
; 4. (when c v) is the same as (if c v ()), and (unless c v) is the same as
;    (if c () v).

(let x : u32 = 5)
(when (> x 3_u32) (print "x is big"))       ; prints: x is big                                  [5.6.1]
(unless (> x 3_u32) (print "x is small"))   ; returns (), and prints nothing                    [5.6.2]
(when (> x 3_u32) 7)                        ; error: the body must be Unit, not an i32          [5.6.3]
(when (> x 3_u32) (print "a") (print "b"))  ; error: when takes one body; wrap several in a do  [5.6.3]
(when (> x 3_u32) (do
    (print "a")
    (print "b")))  ; prints: a  [5.6.3]
                   ;         b
(if (> x 3_u32) (print "x is big") ())  ; the same as the first when  [5.6.4]

;;; 5.7 match
(match <Value T> <Arm T U>...) -> U
{match <Value T> <Arm T F>...} -> F
; A match compares a value against patterns (6) and evaluates the arm that
; applies.

;; 5.7.1 arms and guards
<Pattern T> <Guard>? => <Value U>
<Pattern T> <Guard>? => next
; 1. Each arm has a pattern, an optional guard, and then a value or next
;    (5.7.3).
; 2. An arm applies when its pattern matches and its guard, if it has one,
;    is true.
; 3. The bindings a pattern makes are visible in that arm's guard and value
;    only.
; 4. The if of a guard is part of the arm, not an if form (5.5).
; 5. Every arm's value has the same type U.
; 6. A binding that the arm never uses warns (4.1.10; lint unused_binding).
;    Use _ instead (6.1).

(let weapon : Weapon = Weapon.Fist)
(match weapon
    (Weapon.Sword sword = sword)          => sword.legendary  ; binds sword      [5.7.1.3]
    (Weapon.Bow damage = d) if (> d 10.0) => true             ; a guard          [5.7.1.2]
    (Weapon.Bow damage = _)               => false            ; _ binds nothing  [5.7.1.6]
    ^Weapon.Fist                          => false)           ; returns false    [5.7.1.1]
(match 3
    n if (> n 2) => "big"
    n            => "small")  ; returns "big"; each n is a separate binding  [5.7.1.3]
(match weapon
    (Weapon.Bow damage = d) => d
    _                       => "none")  ; error: an f64 arm and a string arm  [5.7.1.5]
(match weapon
    (Weapon.Bow damage = d) => true
    _                       => false)  ; warning: d is never used  [5.7.1.6]
(match weapon
    w if (== w Weapon.Fist) => "fist"
    _                       => "armed")  ; returns "fist"; a guard is any <Value bool>  [5.7.1.4]

;; 5.7.2 order and exhaustiveness
; 1. Arms are tried in order. The first arm that applies is chosen, and only
;    its value is evaluated.
; 2. A match must be exhaustive: for every possible value of type T, some
;    arm applies. A match that is not exhaustive is a compile error.
;    why?: a match always produces a U, so no value may be left without one
; 3. An arm with a guard does not count toward exhaustiveness.
;    why?: its guard may be false, and the compiler cannot know in general

(match 3
    n if (> n 1) => "more than one"
    3            => "three"
    _            => "other")  ; returns "more than one": the first arm applies first  [5.7.2.1]
(match 5
    1 => "one"
    2 => "two")  ; error: no arm for the other i32 values  [5.7.2.2]
(match true
    true  => "yes"
    false => "no")  ; exhaustive without _: bool has two values  [5.7.2.2]
(match weapon
    (Weapon.Sword sword = _)               => false
    (Weapon.Bow damage = d) if (> d 10.0)  => true
    ^Weapon.Fist                           => false)
    ; error: not exhaustive; the guarded arm does not cover every Bow  [5.7.2.3]

;; 5.7.3 next
; 1. An arm whose value is next shares the value of the arm after it. When
;    it applies, the match evaluates the next arm's value instead, without
;    checking that arm's pattern or guard. Chains of next arms share the
;    value at the end of the chain.
; 2. All arms that share one value must bind the same names, with the same
;    types.
; 3. The last arm cannot be next. That is a compile error.
;    why: the match would have no value for it, so it could not be
;    exhaustive

(let gunbow : Weapon = (Weapon.Bow damage = 5.0))
{match gunbow
    (Weapon.Sword sword = _) => {fn |-> string| "sword"}
    (Weapon.Bow damage = _)  => next
    ^Weapon.Fist             => {fn |-> string| "other"}}
    ; type: [-> string]; gunbow gets the Fist arm's value         [5.7.3.1]
    ; the Bow and Fist arms both bind nothing, so they may share  [5.7.3.2]
(match 2
    1 => next
    2 => next
    3 => "small"
    _ => "large")  ; returns "small": 2 shares the 3 arm's value  [5.7.3.1]
(match gunbow
    (Weapon.Bow damage = d) if (> d 10.0) => next
    (Weapon.Bow damage = d)               => (* d 2.0)
    _                                     => 0.0)
    ; returns 10.0; both shared arms bind d : f64  [5.7.3.2]
(match gunbow
    (Weapon.Bow damage = d) => next
    _                       => "other")
    ; error: the Bow arm binds d, and the arm it shares with binds nothing  [5.7.3.2]
(match 2
    1 => "one"
    _ => next)  ; error: the last arm cannot be next  [5.7.3.3]
(match gunbow
    _ => {fn |-> string| "any"})
    ; error: the arms are functions; write {match ...}  [2.4.2.3]

;;; 5.8 loop
(loop <Signature> <Value T>) -> T
; 1. Every parameter needs a default, which is its value on the first pass.
;    why?: a loop is never called, so nothing else could supply the values
; 2. The output type in the signature is the type of the whole loop, and is
;    required (2.5.2.1).
; 3. Each pass evaluates the body with the parameters bound. A recur in the
;    body starts the next pass (5.9).
; 4. The loop's value is the value of its first pass, where each recur
;    evaluates to the value of the pass it starts. When recur is only used
;    in tail position, that is the value of the last pass: the first one
;    that ends without calling recur.
; 5. When the output type is a function type, the loop is written in { },
;    and so are the forms inside that produce its value (2.4.2.1).
; 6. Loop parameters follow the rules for fn parameters: they may shadow
;    (5.4.1.6), may not repeat a name (5.4.1.7), and their defaults see the
;    parameters before them (5.4.2.3).

(loop |number : u32 = 1 -> Unit| (do
    (print (`hello ${number}` (to_string number)))
    (if (< number 3_u32)
        (recur number = (+ number 1_u32))
        (print "loop terminated!"))))  ; prints: hello 1  [5.8.3]
                                       ;         hello 2
                                       ;         hello 3
                                       ;         loop terminated!
(loop |n : u32 = 1 -> u32| (if (< n 9_u32) (recur n = (* n 2_u32)) n))
    ; returns 16, the value of the pass where n is 16  [5.8.4]
(loop |i : u32 = 0 total : u32 = 0 -> u32|
    (if (< i 4_u32)
        (recur i = (+ i 1_u32) total = (+ total i))
        total))  ; returns 6, which is 0 + 1 + 2 + 3  [5.8.3]
(loop |n : u32 -> u32| n)  ; error: n has no starting value      [5.8.1]
(loop |n : u32 = 0| n)     ; error: the output type is required  [5.8.2]
{loop |n : u32 = 0 -> [u32 -> u32]|
    {if (< n 3_u32)
        {recur n = (+ n 1_u32)}
        {make_adder n}}}  ; type: [u32 -> u32]; adds 3  [5.8.5]
(loop |i : u32 = 0 i : u32 = 1 -> u32| i)  ; error: two parameters are named i  [5.8.6]
(loop |lo : u32 = 2 hi : u32 = (* lo 3_u32) -> u32| hi)  ; returns 6; hi's default uses lo  [5.8.6]

;;; 5.9 recur
(recur <Binding>...) -> T
; Its bindings are written like a constructor's.
; 1. recur starts a new pass of the innermost loop or fn that contains it.
; 2. Each <Binding> gives a parameter its value for the new pass. Parameters
;    left out keep their current values.
; 3. A binding's name must be a parameter of that loop or fn.
; 4. recur evaluates to the value of the pass it starts, so its type T is
;    the output type of that loop or fn.
; 5. A recur is in tail position when its value becomes the value of the
;    whole pass: it is the body itself, or the last item of a do, a branch
;    of an if, the body of a when or unless, or an arm value of a match,
;    where that do, if, when, unless or match is itself in tail position.
; 6. recur may appear anywhere, but one that is not in tail position warns
;    (lint recur_not_tail, 4.7.3).
;    why: it helps learners avoid stack overflows
; 7. A recur in tail position runs in constant stack space: it is a jump,
;    not a call.

(loop |i : u32 = 0 acc : u32 = 1 -> u32|
    (if (< i 5_u32)
        (recur i = (+ i 1_u32) acc = (* acc 2_u32))
        acc))  ; returns 32  [5.9.2]
(loop |i : u32 = 0 limit : u32 = 3 -> u32|
    (if (< i limit)
        (recur i = (+ i 1_u32))  ; limit is left out, so it stays 3  [5.9.2]
        i))                      ; returns 3
(define factorial_2 = {fn |n : u64 acc : u64 = 1 -> u64|
    (if (== n 0_u64)
        acc
        (recur n = (- n 1_u64) acc = (* acc n)))})  ; recur in a fn needs no name  [5.9.1]
(factorial_2 5)  ; returns 120  [5.9.7]
(loop |i : u32 = 0 -> u32|
    (if (< i 2_u32)
        (recur i = (+ i 1_u32))         ; a new pass of the outer loop
        (loop |j : u32 = 10 -> u32|
            (if (< j 12_u32)
                (recur j = (+ j 1_u32))  ; a new pass of the inner loop  [5.9.1]
                (+ i j)))))              ; returns 14
(loop |n : u32 = 3 -> u32|
    (if (== n 0_u32)
        0
        (+ n (recur n = (- n 1_u32)))))
    ; warning: recur is not in tail position; returns 6  [5.9.4, 5.9.6]
(loop |i : u32 = 0 -> u32|
    (match (> i 2_u32)
        true  => i
        false => (recur i = (+ i 1_u32))))  ; returns 3; a match arm is a tail position  [5.9.5]
(loop |i : u32 = 0 -> u32| (if (< i 3_u32) (recur j = 1) i))  ; error: j is not a parameter  [5.9.3]
(recur n = 1)  ; error: no loop or fn contains this recur                                [5.9.1]

;;; 5.10 for
(for |<Parameter>...| <Value bool> (recur <Binding>...) <Expression Unit>) -> Unit
; The step (recur ...) is written before the body, but runs after it.
; 1. for is sugar for a loop that returns Unit. Its parameters are written
;    with no ->, because the output is always Unit.
; 2. A for expands to:
(loop |<Parameter>... -> Unit|
    (if <Value bool>
        (do
            <Expression Unit>
            (recur <Binding>...))
        ()))
; 3. The condition is checked before every pass. If it holds, the body runs,
;    then the step starts the next pass. Otherwise the for returns ().
; 4. As in loop, every parameter needs a default (5.8.1).
; 5. As in loop, the parameters follow the rules for fn parameters (5.8.6).

(for |i : u32 = 0| (< i 3_u32) (recur i = (+ i 1_u32))
    (print (to_string i)))  ; prints: 0  [5.10.3]
                            ;         1
                            ;         2
(for |i : u32 = 10| (< i 3_u32) (recur i = (+ i 1_u32))
    (print "never"))  ; prints nothing: the condition fails on the first pass  [5.10.3]
(for |i : u32 = 0 j : u32 = 10| (< i j) (recur i = (+ i 1_u32) j = (- j 1_u32))
    (print (`${a}-${b}` (to_string i) (to_string j))))
    ; prints: 0-10, 1-9, 2-8, 3-7 and 4-6, one per line  [5.10.2]
(for |i : u32 = 0 -> Unit| (< i 3_u32) (recur i = (+ i 1_u32)) (print "x"))
    ; error: for takes no ->          [5.10.1]
(for |i : u32| (< i 3_u32) (recur i = (+ i 1_u32)) (print "x"))
    ; error: i has no starting value  [5.10.4]
(for |i : u32 = 0 i : u32 = 5| (< i 3_u32) (recur i = (+ i 1_u32)) (print "x"))
    ; error: two parameters are named i (5.4.1.7)  [5.10.5]

;;;; 6 patterns
; A pattern either matches a value or does not. A pattern that matches may
; bind names, which are visible in its arm's guard and value (5.7.1.3).
; Examples use jane, the Employee from 4.4.2.

;;; 6.1 wildcard
_
; 1. _ matches any one value and binds nothing.

(match 42
    _ => "anything")  ; returns "anything"  [6.1.1]
(match (list 1 2)
    (list _ _) => "two items"
    _          => "other")  ; returns "two items"; each _ matches one element  [6.1.1]

;;; 6.2 identifiers
<Identifier>
; 1. An identifier matches any value, and binds it to that name. If a local
;    binding has the same name, the new binding shadows it (4.1.5).
; 2. An identifier that names a top-level or built-in name does not bind.
;    It matches only a value equal to that name's value (8.3), and warns
;    unless it is pinned (6.8; lint unpinned_name, 4.7.3).
;    why?: a pattern never hides a top-level name (4.1.8), and the warning
;    makes every comparison visible as ^
; 3. One pattern may not bind the same name twice. To require two parts to
;    be equal, bind them to different names and compare them in a guard.

(match 4
    n => (* n 2))  ; returns 8; n binds 4  [6.2.1]
(do
    (let target : i32 = 3)
    (match 5
        target => "hit"
        _      => "miss"))
    ; returns "hit": target binds 5, shadowing the let;
    ; warning: target is never used (5.7.1.6)  [6.2.1]
(do
    (let target : i32 = 3)
    (match 5
        ^target => "hit"
        _       => "miss"))  ; returns "miss": ^target compares with 3  [6.8.1]
(do
    (let target : i32 = 3)
    (match 5
        n => (+ n target)))  ; returns 8: n binds, and target is the let  [6.2.1]
(define LUCKY : i32 = 7)
(match 7
    ^LUCKY => "lucky"
    _      => "plain")  ; returns "lucky"; LUCKY is pinned, so it compares  [6.2.2]
(match 7
    LUCKY => "lucky"
    _     => "plain")
    ; returns "lucky"; warning: LUCKY is top-level, so it compares; write ^LUCKY  [6.2.2]
(match (list 1 1)
    (list x x) => "same"
    _          => "different")  ; error: x is bound twice in one pattern  [6.2.3]
(match (list 1 1)
    (list x y) if (== x y) => "same"
    _                      => "different")  ; returns "same"  [6.2.3]

;;; 6.3 literals
<Literal>
; 1. A literal matches a value equal to it.

(match 'c'
    'a' => 1
    'c' => 3
    _   => 0)  ; returns 3  [6.3.1]
(match "hi"
    "hi" => true
    _    => false)  ; returns true  [6.3.1]
(match (Option.Some [i32] value = 0)
    (Option.Some value = 0) => "zero"
    (Option.Some value = _) => "other"
    ^Option.None            => "none")  ; returns "zero"; a literal inside a pattern  [6.3.1]

;;; 6.4 constructor patterns
(<Identifier> <Field Pattern>...)
(<Identifier> <Field Pattern>... ..)
; 1. A constructor pattern matches a struct or variant value built by that
;    constructor when each listed field's value matches its pattern.
; 2. The constructor's name is written in full, as when building:
;    (Option.Some value = v), not (Some value = v).
; 3. Each field is written name = pattern. (Option.Some value), with no
;    pattern, is an error.
; 4. Every field must be listed, unless the pattern ends with .., which
;    skips the remaining fields. .. may only come last.
; 5. A variant with no fields is matched by its name alone, without
;    parentheses. Like a top-level name, it compares, and warns unless it
;    is pinned: ^Weapon.Fist (6.2.2, 6.8; lint unpinned_name).
; 6. A constructor pattern never sees through a box: a field of type
;    [Box T] is matched by a box pattern (6.6), not by a pattern for T.

(match (Option.Some [i32] value = 3)
    (Option.Some value = v) => v
    ^Option.None            => 0)  ; returns 3  [6.4.1]
(match (Option.Some [Option i32] value = (Option.Some [i32] value = 3))
    (Option.Some value = (Option.Some value = x)) => x
    (Option.Some value = ^Option.None)            => 0
    ^Option.None                                  => -1)
    ; returns 3; patterns nest  [6.4.1]
(match (Person.Hacker person = (Hacker skill = "backdoors" salary = 1.0))
    (Person.Hacker person = (Hacker skill = s ..))     => s
    (Person.Employee person = (Employee name = n ..))  => n)
    ; returns "backdoors"  [6.4.1]
(match (Option.Some [i32] value = 3)
    (Some value = v) => v
    _                => 0)  ; error: write Option.Some  [6.4.2]
(match (Option.Some [i32] value = 3)
    (Option.Some value) => 1
    _                   => 0)  ; error: the field value has no pattern  [6.4.3]
(match jane
    (Employee name = n age = _ dept = _ salary = _) => n)
    ; returns "jane"; every field is listed  [6.4.4]
(match jane
    (Employee name = n ..) => n)  ; returns "jane"; .. skips the other fields  [6.4.4]
(match jane
    (Employee dept = "unknown" ..) => "unassigned"
    _                              => "assigned")  ; returns "unassigned"  [6.4.1]
(match jane
    (Employee name = n) => n)  ; error: age, dept and salary are not listed  [6.4.4]
(match jane
    (Employee .. name = n) => n)  ; error: .. must come last  [6.4.4]
(do
    (let nothing : [Option i32] = Option.None [i32])
    (match nothing
        ^Option.None            => "none"
        (Option.Some value = _) => "some"))  ; returns "none"  [6.4.5]

;;; 6.5 list patterns
(list <Pattern>...)
; 1. (list p...) matches a list whose elements match the patterns, in order.
;    Without .., the list must have exactly as many elements as there are
;    patterns.
; 2. .. matches zero or more elements and binds nothing.
; 3. .. may appear more than once, as long as no binding sits between two
;    of them.
;    why: otherwise which element the binding refers to is ambiguous

(let numbers : [List i32] = (list 1 2 3))
(match numbers
    (list 1 2 3)  => true    ; exactly 1, 2, 3                               [6.5.1]
    (list 1 2 ..) => true    ; starts with 1, 2                              [6.5.2]
    (list 1 .. 3) => false   ; starts with 1, ends with 3                    [6.5.2]
    _             => false)  ; returns true; _ is needed for exhaustiveness  [5.7.2.2]
(match (list 1 2 3 4 5)
    (list 1 .. 3 .. 5)     => true                 ; 1, any, 3, any, 5; 3 binds nothing    [6.5.3]
    (list a b .. e f)      => (> (+ a b) (+ e f))  ; binds the first two and the last two  [6.5.2]
    l @ (list 1 .. 3 .. 2) => (== l.2 5)           ; binds the whole list to l (6.7)
    _                      => false)               ; returns true, from the first arm      [5.7.2.1]
(match numbers
    (list)        => "empty"
    (list _)      => "one"
    (list _ _ ..) => "two or more")  ; returns "two or more"; exhaustive with no final _  [6.5.1]
(match numbers
    (list 1 3) => "one, three"
    _          => "other")  ; returns "other": without .., the length must match  [6.5.1]
(match numbers
    (list a .. b .. c) => b
    _                  => 0)
    ; error: b sits between two .., so the element it binds is ambiguous  [6.5.3]

;;; 6.6 box patterns
(box <Pattern T>)
; 1. (box p) matches a [Box T] (3.7) when the value inside the box matches
;    p. It mirrors (box x), which builds a box (8.8), as a constructor
;    pattern mirrors building (6.4.2).
; 2. It takes exactly one pattern, in a fixed place, not name = pattern.
; 3. Box patterns nest: inside a constructor pattern, a list pattern or
;    another box pattern, and around any pattern.
; 4. A pattern (box ...) is always a box pattern, never a constructor
;    pattern (6.4). A constructor pattern names a struct or a qualified
;    variant, which are capitalized (1.4.1), and box is a built-in name
;    that no constructor can take (4.1.7).
; 5. (box p) matches only a [Box T]. No other pattern sees through a box:
;    a pattern for a T against a [Box T] is a type error (6.4.6).
;    why?: seeing through would be an implicit conversion, and it would be
;    unclear whether left = l binds the box or its contents
; 6. A boxed value can also be read with unbox and then matched, like any
;    function result (8.8).
;    why: unbox needs no rule of its own; the pattern is what lets
;    patterns nest through boxes, which every recursive type needs

; with Tree from 3.7
(let leaf = (box Tree.Leaf [u8]))
(let t = (Tree.Node [u8] left = leaf value = 1_u8 right = leaf))
(match t
    (Tree.Node left = (box ^Tree.Leaf) right = (box ^Tree.Leaf) ..) => "two leaves"
    _                                                               => "other")
    ; returns "two leaves"; a box pattern inside a constructor pattern  [6.6.3]
(match leaf
    (box (Tree.Node value = v ..)) => v
    (box ^Tree.Leaf)               => 0_u8)  ; returns 0; matching a [Box [Tree u8]]  [6.6.1]
(match (unbox leaf)
    ^Tree.Leaf     => "leaf"
    (Tree.Node ..) => "node")  ; returns "leaf"; unbox first, then match  [6.6.6]
(match t
    (Tree.Node left = ^Tree.Leaf ..) => "leaf on the left"
    _                                => "other")
    ; error: left is a [Box [Tree u8]]; write (box ^Tree.Leaf)  [6.6.5]
(match leaf
    (box value = v) => v
    _               => 0_u8)  ; error: a box pattern takes one pattern, not name = pattern  [6.6.2]

;;; 6.7 whole-value binding
<Identifier> @ <Pattern>
; 1. name @ pattern matches when pattern does, and also binds the whole
;    value to name.

(match (Option.Some [i32] value = 4)
    whole @ (Option.Some value = v) if (> v 3) => whole
    _                                          => Option.None [i32])
    ; returns (Option.Some [i32] value = 4)  [6.7.1]
(match (list 1 2 3)
    all @ (list 1 ..) => all.1
    _                 => 0)  ; returns 2; all is the whole list  [6.7.1]
(match jane
    e @ (Employee age = a ..) if (> a 18_u32) => e.name
    _                                         => "minor")  ; returns "jane"  [6.7.1]

;;; 6.8 pins
<Pin>
; 1. ^name matches only a value equal to the value bound to name (8.3). A
;    pin binds nothing.
; 2. The pinned name must already be bound outside the pattern: a local, a
;    top-level or built-in name, or a variant with no fields, written in
;    full. Pinning anything else is an error.
; 3. Since a pin binds nothing, it may sit between two .. in a list pattern
;    (6.5.3).
; 4. The name before @ always binds (6.7), so it cannot be pinned.
;    why?: with ^, a pattern's meaning is visible from the pattern alone.
;    Adding a define or a let elsewhere never turns a binding into a
;    comparison

(match Weapon.Fist
    ^Weapon.Fist => "fist"
    _            => "armed")  ; returns "fist"  [6.8.2]
(match Weapon.Fist
    Weapon.Fist => "fist"
    _           => "armed")  ; returns "fist"; warning: write ^Weapon.Fist  [6.4.5]
(do
    (let limit : i32 = 3)
    (match (Option.Some [i32] value = 3)
        (Option.Some value = ^limit) => "at the limit"
        _                            => "elsewhere"))
    ; returns "at the limit"; a pin inside a constructor pattern  [6.8.1]
(match 5
    ^missing => 1
    _        => 0)  ; error: missing is not bound  [6.8.2]
(do
    (let middle : i32 = 3)
    (match (list 1 2 3 4 5)
        (list 1 .. ^middle .. 5) => "has it"
        _                        => "lacks it"))
    ; returns "has it"; a pin may sit between two ..  [6.8.3]
(match jane
    ^e @ (Employee ..) => e.name)  ; error: the name before @ always binds  [6.8.4]

;;;; 7 generics

;;; 7.1 type parameters
; 1. Every type parameter must be declared: in a type header for a struct or
;    enum (7.2), or in a forall list for a function (7.3).
; 2. A type parameter may not reuse the name of a type parameter that is
;    already in scope, such as one from an enclosing generic fn.
;    why: it promotes messy code for no benefit
; 3. A type parameter may not have the name of an existing type, built-in or
;    defined. The error suggests the type's first letter, or its name with
;    the suffix Type: E or EmployeeType for Employee.
; 4. A type parameter may not have the name of a trait either (7.5.1.7).
; A forall list may bound its type parameters by traits. That is how a
; generic function requires an operation such as + (7.5.4).

(struct Crate |item : T|)  ; error: T is not declared; write (struct [Crate T] |item : T|)  [7.1.1]
{fn |x : T -> T| x}      ; error: T is not declared; write {fn [forall T] |x : T -> T| x}  [7.1.1]
{fn [forall T] |x : T -> T| (do
    (let keep_x = {fn [forall U] |y : U -> T| x})
    (keep_x [i32] 1))}  ; fine: U is a new name  [7.1.2]
{fn [forall T] |x : T -> T| (do
    (let keep_x = {fn [forall T] |y : T -> T| y})
    (keep_x [i32] 1))}  ; error: T is already a type parameter here  [7.1.2]
(struct [Holder Employee] |item : Employee|)
    ; error: Employee is an existing type; write E or EmployeeType  [7.1.3]
{fn [forall string] |x : string -> string| x}
    ; error: string is a built-in type; write S or StringType     [7.1.3]

;;; 7.2 generic structs and enums
(struct <Type Header> <Fields>) -> Unit
(enum <Type Header> <Definition>...) -> Unit
; 1. In the header [Name T U...], Name is the new generic type and T U...
;    are its type parameters. They can be used as types in every field.
; 2. A generic type is used by giving it type arguments: [Pair i32 string].
; 3. When a generic value is built, its type arguments are written after
;    the constructor's name (7.4): (Pair [i32] [string] first = 1 ...). A
;    variant with no fields takes them too: Option.None [string].
; 4. A generic struct's accessors are generic functions: Pair.first has type
;    [[forall A B] [Pair A B] -> A], so a call gives its type arguments.
;    name.field needs none, since the type of name is already known.
;    Constructors and accessors need all of their type arguments, even
;    where a generic function could leave them out (7.4.4). Whether they
;    should follow the same rule is open (11.7.16).
; 5. A type may refer to itself, directly or through other types, as long
;    as it never does so at a bigger type. A recursive group is a set of
;    types that mention each other. Build a graph with one node for each
;    type parameter of the group's members. For each mention of a member
;    inside the group, and each of its type arguments i:
;    - a parameter X passed exactly as argument i adds a plain edge from X
;      to the member's parameter i;
;    - a parameter X nested inside argument i, as in [List X], adds an
;      expanding edge from X to the member's parameter i.
;    A cycle that contains an expanding edge is an error. An argument with
;    no type parameter in it adds no edge. The same check applies to
;    generic functions that call themselves (7.3.8).
;    why: it rejects exactly the definitions that need infinitely many
;    types. A type, and everything it contains, then involves only finitely
;    many distinct types, so walking its structure always finishes
; 6. Whether a type may contain itself without a Box is a separate rule
;    (3.7.2). No bounds may be written in a type header (7.5.4.3).

(struct [Pair A B] |
    first  : A
    second : B|)  ; A and B are used as field types  [7.2.1]
(let p : [Pair i32 string] = (Pair [i32] [string] first = 1 second = "one"))
                                         ; the type [Pair i32 string], built with [i32] [string]  [7.2.2, 7.2.3]
(Pair [u8] [string] first = 1 second = "one")  ; type: [Pair u8 string]; 1 is a u8 (5.1.1.2)  [7.2.3]
(Pair first = 1 second = "one")          ; error: Pair needs its type arguments A and B      [7.2.3]
(Pair [i32] first = 1 second = "one")    ; error: Pair takes two type arguments              [7.2.3]
p.second                                 ; returns "one"                                     [7.2.1]
(let q : [Pair i32] = p)                 ; error: Pair takes two type arguments              [7.2.2]
(let none_yet = Option.None [string])    ; none_yet : [Option string]                        [7.2.3]
(let not_yet : [Option string] = Option.None)  ; error: Option.None needs its type argument  [7.2.3]
(Pair.first [i32] [string] p)            ; returns 1                                         [7.2.4]
Pair.first                               ; type: [[forall A B] [Pair A B] -> A]              [7.2.4]
(Pair.first p)                           ; error: Pair.first needs its type arguments        [7.2.4]
(enum [Either L R]
    Left  |value : L|
    Right |value : R|)  ; a generic enum  [7.2.1]
(let e = (Either.Right [i32] [string] value = "r"))  ; e : [Either i32 string]  [7.2.3]
(enum [Tree T]
    Leaf
    Node |left : [Box [Tree T]] value : T right : [Box [Tree T]]|)
    ; ok: each mention passes T as T, an edge that does not expand  [7.2.5]
(let tree = (Tree.Node [i32]
    left = (box Tree.Leaf [i32])
    value = 5
    right = (box Tree.Leaf [i32])))  ; the children are boxed (3.7)  [7.2.3]
(struct [Flip A B] |swap : [Option [Box [Flip B A]]]|)
    ; ok: A -> B -> A, and no edge expands  [7.2.5]
(struct [Mixed A B] |kid : [Option [Box [Mixed [List u8] A]]]|)
    ; ok: [List u8] holds no type parameter, so it adds no edge  [7.2.5]
(enum [Nest T]
    Flat |value : T|
    Deep |inner : [Box [Nest [List T]]]|)
    ; error: T becomes [List T] here, so the edge T -> T expands  [7.2.5]

;;; 7.3 generic functions
{fn <Identifier>? <Forall> <Signature> <Value T>} -> [<Forall> <Type>... -> <Type>]
; 1. [forall T U...] declares the function's type parameters, which can be
;    used as types in its signature and body.
; 2. The function's type starts with the same forall list.
; 3. Each call fills the type parameters: from the type arguments it
;    writes, and from its arguments for the rest (7.4). Each parameter has
;    one type throughout the call.
; 4. Inside the body, a value whose type is a type parameter is not known to
;    be a function, so it is produced in ( ) (2.4.2.2).
;    why: T is not known to be a function there
; 5. A forall list may end with the keyword with and one or more bounds:
;    [forall T U with [Shape T]]. A bound says which traits the type
;    parameters must implement (7.5.4). The bounds are part of the
;    function's type.
; 6. The body is checked once, where the function is written, against its
;    bounds. It is never checked again for each call (7.5.4.4).
; 7. Generics are monomorphized: each distinct set of type arguments a
;    function is used with gets its own compiled copy, and every trait
;    method the body calls resolves to an impl at compile time (7.5.3).
;    There are no runtime dictionaries.
; 8. A generic function may not call itself, directly or through other
;    generic functions, at ever bigger types: that would need infinitely
;    many copies. The check is the graph of 7.2.5, built from a group of
;    generic functions that call each other, with each call in place of a
;    mention, using the call's settled type arguments (7.4). A call in a
;    branch that never runs still counts.

(define first_or = {fn [forall T] |xs : [List T] fallback : T -> T|
    (match (get xs 0)  ; ( ) even though T could be a function  [7.3.4]
        (Option.Some value = first) => first
        ^Option.None                => fallback)})
first_or                                     ; type: [[forall T] [List T] T -> T]           [7.3.2]
(first_or [i32] (list 4 5 6) 0)              ; returns 4; T is i32                          [7.3.3]
(first_or [string] (list [string]) "none")   ; returns "none"; T is string                  [7.3.3]
(first_or [i32] (list 1 2) "none")           ; error: fallback is an i32 here, not a string  [7.3.3]
(first_or (list 4 5 6) 0)                    ; returns 4; T is i32, settled by the arguments  [7.3.3]
{first_or [u32 -> u32] (list {make_adder 1}) {make_adder 2}}
    ; type: [u32 -> u32]; at this call T is a function, so { }  [2.4.2.2]
(define apply = {fn [forall T U] |x : T f : [T -> U] -> U| (f x)})
(apply [i32] [bool] 3 {fn |n : i32 -> bool| (> n 2)})  ; returns true; T is i32, U is bool  [7.3.3]
(apply [Employee] [string] jane Employee.name)
    ; returns "jane"; an accessor is a function (4.4.3)  [7.3.1]
(define pair_up = {fn [forall A B] |a : A b : B -> [Pair A B]| (Pair [A] [B] first = a second = b)})
(pair_up [i32] [string] 1 "one")             ; type: [Pair i32 string]                      [7.3.1]
(trait [Shape T] area : [T -> f64])
(define total_area = {fn [forall T with [Shape T]] |a : T b : T -> f64| (+ (area a) (area b))})
total_area                                   ; type: [[forall T with [Shape T]] T T -> f64]  [7.3.5]
(define depth = {fn [forall T] |x : T n : u32 -> u32|
    (if (== n 0_u32) 0_u32 (depth (list x) (- n 1_u32)))})
    ; error: depth calls itself with T as [List T], so T -> T expands  [7.3.8]
(define trade = {fn [forall A B] |a : A b : B n : u32 -> u32|
    (if (== n 0_u32) 0_u32 (trade b a (- n 1_u32)))})
    ; ok: A and B only trade places, and no edge expands  [7.3.8]

;;; 7.4 type arguments
<Identifier> <Type Argument> <Type Argument>...
; 1. A name followed by type arguments is that name with its type parameters
;    filled in, in the order of its forall list (7.3) or type header (7.2),
;    starting from the first. first_or [i32] is a [[List i32] i32 -> i32].
; 2. In the callee position, this is a call: (first_or [i32] xs 0) calls
;    first_or [i32]. A [ ] directly after a name belongs to that name, so in
;    (combine [i32] 2 3 + [i32]) the second [i32] belongs to +.
; 3. Each [ ] holds exactly one type: [u32], [List i32], [u32 -> u32]. A
;    bracketed type with no arguments is that type, so [u32] is u32.
; 4. A call of a generic function may leave type arguments out. The ones it
;    writes fill the forall list from the left, and the call's arguments
;    settle the rest. To give a later parameter, a call gives every
;    parameter before it. Giving too many is a compile error.
;    why: it is the order rule of 7.5.2.3: an author puts the parameters
;    that callers write first, and which parameter a type argument fills
;    never changes silently
;    A generic constructor or accessor still needs all of its type
;    arguments wherever it is used (7.2.3, 7.2.4). Whether they should
;    follow this rule too is open (11.7.16).
; 5. Type arguments may only follow an identifier or a qualified name, as .
;    may (2.3.4). Any other value must be bound to a name first.
; 6. Patterns take no type arguments. A pattern gets its types from the
;    value it is matched against.
; 7. A type parameter that is not given is settled by the call's arguments
;    and by nothing else. The type expected where the call appears never
;    settles it, even when that type is written: an annotation, a declared
;    parameter type, a fn's output type.
;    why: a call's types always follow from what is written in the call,
;    with no rule for working a type out from its surroundings
;    A written type still reaches a literal (5.1.1.2), so
;    (let n : u8 = 200) compiles, while (let e : [List u8] = (list)) does
;    not. Closing that gap is sugar for later (11.7.15).
; 8. A type parameter that the arguments cannot settle must be given. That
;    covers a parameter that appears only in the output type, as in parse,
;    zero and the casts (8.6.1), list with no arguments (8.7.1.3), and a
;    trait parameter that a method does not mention (7.5.2.5). A generic
;    function used as a value, not called, has no arguments, so it needs
;    all of its type arguments: + [u32], first_or [i32].
; 9. A given type argument and an argument that disagree are an error. Two
;    arguments that settle one type parameter differently are an error
;    that names both. Nothing is widened to make them agree (3.1.3).
; 10. An unsuffixed literal argument takes its parameter's type only when
;     that type is written: in the signature, or by a type argument at the
;     call (5.1.1.2). When the parameter's type is a type parameter that
;     the call does not give, the literal keeps its fixed type (5.1.1.3)
;     and helps settle the parameter like any other argument. A literal
;     never takes its type from the other arguments.
; 11. A type parameter cannot be settled as a type that contains it, such
;     as T = [List T]. The error points at the argument that settled it.
;     While types come from arguments only (rule 7), every argument's type
;     is already known, so this cannot come up yet. The rule is stated so
;     that it is in place if rule 7 is ever relaxed (11.7.15).
;    why?: every type in a call follows from the call itself: its type
;    arguments and the types of its arguments. A reader never has to look
;    at where the call is used
;    why?: [ ] never wrap an expression (2.4.1.1), so a type argument is
;    never mistaken for a value, even for a name such as Employee that is
;    both a type and a constructor

(let numbers : [List i32] = (list 1 2 3))
(first_or [i32] numbers 0)         ; returns 1                                [7.4.2]
(first_or numbers 0)               ; returns 1; T is i32, settled by numbers  [7.4.4]
first_or [i32]                     ; type: [[List i32] i32 -> i32]            [7.4.1]
(let first_num = first_or [i32])   ; a generic function, passed as a value    [7.4.1]
(let first_any = first_or)         ; error: nothing settles T; write first_or [T]  [7.4.8]
(pair_up [i32] [string] 1 "one")   ; two type arguments, in forall order      [7.4.1]
(pair_up 1 "one")                  ; type: [Pair i32 string]; A and B settled by the arguments  [7.4.4]
(pair_up [i64] 1 "one")            ; type: [Pair i64 string]; A is given, so the 1 is an i64  [7.4.4, 7.4.10]
(let n : i32 = 1)
(pair_up [i64] n "one")            ; error: A is i64, but the argument n is an i32  [7.4.9]
(pair_up [i32] [string] [bool] 1 "one")  ; error: pair_up takes two type arguments  [7.4.4]
(pair_up [i32 string] 1 "one")
    ; error: [i32 string] reads as i32 applied to string; one type per [ ]  [7.4.3]
(identity [Employee] jane)         ; returns jane; [Employee] is the type     [7.4.2]
(identity Employee jane)
    ; error: identity takes one argument; here Employee is the constructor, a value  [7.4.2]
{fn [forall T] |x : T -> T| x} [i32]
    ; error: type arguments follow a name; bind the fn first  [7.4.5]
(match (Option.Some [i32] value = 3)
    (Option.Some value = v) => v
    ^Option.None            => 0)  ; returns 3; the pattern takes no type arguments  [7.4.6]
(let x : u32 = 7)
(+ x x)                      ; returns 14; T is u32, settled by the arguments              [7.4.4]
(+ x "b")                    ; error: T is u32 from x but string from "b"                  [7.4.9]
(+ x 1)                      ; error: T is u32 from x but i32 from 1; write 1_u32          [7.4.10]
(+ x 1_u32)                  ; returns 8                                                   [7.4.10]
(+ [u32] x 1)                ; returns 8; [u32] makes the 1 a u32                          [7.4.10]
(get (list 4 5) 1)           ; returns (Option.Some [i32] value = 5); the index is written as u32  [7.4.10]
(let empty = (list [u8]))    ; empty : [List u8]                                           [7.4.8]
(let none_yet : [List u8] = (list))
    ; error: nothing settles T; the annotation does not count; write (list [u8])  [7.4.7]
(let total : u64 = (+ 2 3))  ; error: (+ 2 3) is an i32; the u64 does not settle T         [7.4.7]
(define add_u32 = + [u32])   ; add_u32 : [u32 u32 -> u32]                                  [7.4.8]
(define plus = +)            ; error: nothing settles T; write + [T]                       [7.4.8]
(define add : [u32 u32 -> u32] = +)
    ; error: the annotation does not settle T; write + [u32]  [7.4.7]
(as [i64] x)                 ; returns 7 as an i64; To is given, From is settled by x      [7.4.4]
(as [i64] [u32] x)           ; the same, with both given                                   [7.4.4]
(as x)                       ; error: nothing settles To                                   [7.4.8]
(parse [u32] "42")           ; T is given: the argument cannot settle it                   [7.4.8]
(parse "42")                 ; error: nothing settles T                                    [7.4.8]

;;; 7.5 traits
; A trait names a set of methods that types can implement. The operators,
; such as + and ==, are trait methods (8.1.3), and a program gives its own
; types an operator by writing an impl, never an overload: no name has more
; than one function behind it (5.2.4). Examples use these types:
(struct Circle |r : f64|)
(struct Square |side : f64|)

;; 7.5.1 trait declarations
(trait <Trait Header> <Trait Entry>...) -> Unit
(trait <Trait Header> with <Bound> <Bound>... <Trait Entry>...) -> Unit  ; with supertraits (7.5.5)
; 1. trait declares a trait and its methods. It may only appear at the top
;    level, like struct and enum, and it can be exported (9.6).
; 2. In the header [Name T U...], Name is the new trait and T U... are its
;    type parameters: the types an impl is written for. Every parameter is
;    named. There is no implicit Self.
;    why: a bound is a full trait application (7.5.4.1), so every parameter
;    needs a name; it also fits 7.1.1
; 3. The body holds only methods, and each method has exactly one entry.
;    There are no constants and no associated types.
; 4. name : <Method Type> is a required method: every impl must supply it
;    (7.5.3.3).
; 5. name = {fn ...} is an auto-implemented method: a method with a body,
;    used by every impl that does not supply its own (7.5.3.3). Its type is
;    read from the fn's signature, as in (define f = {fn ...}).
;    why: : means "impls must supply this" and = means "this has a body",
;    and the signature is written once
; 6. An auto-implemented body is checked once, as if its fn began with a
;    forall list of the trait's parameters, in order, bounded by the trait
;    itself: [forall T with [Shape T]]. So it may call any of the trait's
;    methods, and its supertraits' (7.5.5.4), on those types.
; 7. A trait's name is capitalized, like a type's (1.4.1). It may not be the
;    same as any other top-level name: a type, a function, a value or
;    another trait (4.1.7). It cannot be hidden by a local binding or by a
;    type parameter (4.1.8, 7.1.4).
; 8. A trait is not a type. It appears in a bound (7.5.4), in an impl's
;    header (7.5.3) and in a supertrait list (7.5.5), never where a type is
;    expected. There are no trait objects. A list of mixed values uses an
;    enum.
;    why: generics are monomorphized (7.3.7), so there is nothing a value
;    of a trait's type could dispatch through at run time
;    Since a trait's name never clashes with a type's (rule 7), allowing
;    this later breaks nothing.
; 9. A trait with no methods is allowed. It is useful with supertraits
;    (7.5.5.6), or as a promise stated in its laws (4.6.2.12).

(trait [Shape T]
    area : [T -> f64]                                    ; a required method  [7.5.1.4]
    double_area = {fn |s : T -> f64| (* (area s) 2.0)})  ; auto-implemented, from area  [7.5.1.5, 7.5.1.6]
(trait [Show T]
    show : [T -> string])
(trait [Marker T])                 ; a trait with no methods  [7.5.1.9]
(trait [Limit T]
    limit : u32)                   ; error: a trait holds only methods, and u32 is not a function type  [7.5.1.3]
(trait [Twice T]
    twice : [T -> T]
    twice = {fn |x : T -> T| x})   ; error: twice has two entries  [7.5.1.3]
(trait Plain
    plain : [f64 -> f64])          ; error: a trait needs a header with its type parameters  [7.5.1.2]
(do (trait [Local T]) ())          ; error: trait only appears at the top level  [7.5.1.1]
(struct Shape |n : u32|)           ; error: Shape is already bound, as a trait  [7.5.1.7]
{fn [forall Shape] |x : Shape -> Shape| x}  ; error: Shape is a trait; a type parameter cannot hide it  [7.5.1.7]
(let shapes = (list [Shape]))      ; error: Shape is a trait, not a type  [7.5.1.8]

;; 7.5.2 methods
; 1. A trait's method is a top-level name of the trait's module. It belongs
;    to the module just as a define would, so no other top-level name may
;    be the same (4.1.7), the method of another trait included.
;    why: every name keeps exactly one meaning. A name is resolved by scope
;    alone, and finding the impl (7.5.7) is the only extra step
; 2. A method is a generic function. Its forall list is the trait's
;    parameters, in declared order, bounded by the trait itself: area has
;    the type [[forall T with [Shape T]] T -> f64]. A call of a method is
;    an ordinary generic call (7.4), and its bound is proved like any other
;    (7.5.4.5).
; 3. Declaration order is use order: a trait's parameters are given as
;    type arguments in the order the header declares them (7.4.4). A trait
;    author puts the parameters a caller will write first, such as a
;    cast's target before its source.
; 4. A method may have a forall list of its own. Its type parameters come
;    after the trait's, in the method's declared order.
; 5. A method need not mention every parameter of its trait. The arguments
;    never settle a parameter the method leaves out, so callers give it as
;    a type argument (7.4.8). A trait author puts such parameters first.
; 6. A method may not take a built-in or prelude name (4.1.7). So an
;    unqualified + always means the built-in +, and a type gets + by
;    implementing +'s trait, never by declaring a + of its own.
; 7. A local binding that hides a method warns, like any top-level name
;    (4.1.8; lint shadowing). Outside its module, a method is reached like
;    any exported item, as alias::method (9.4.6). The prelude's methods
;    are unqualified (11.8.5).
;    why: methods are ordinary lowercase words. If hiding one were an
;    error, adding a method to a trait would break every caller's local of
;    that name
; 8. A required method marks a parameter optional by writing = _ after its
;    type: [T f64 = _ -> T]. Only trailing parameters may be marked, since
;    only trailing parameters can be left out (5.2.3). = _ appears only in
;    a required method's type in a trait's body, never in an ordinary
;    function type (3.3.8). An auto-implemented method marks a parameter
;    optional by giving it a default in its fn.
;    why: it mirrors name : Type = value (5.4.2), with _ for "the value is
;    given elsewhere", and needs no new token
; 9. The trait decides which parameters are optional, and each impl decides
;    their values (7.5.3.7). A default's value may differ between impls, as
;    a method's behavior does. The trait's doc says what leaving the
;    parameter out means (4.6.7.5), and each impl keeps to it.
;    why: a call in a generic body that leaves the parameter out is checked
;    once, before T is known (7.5.4.4). Since the trait fixes which
;    arguments may be left out, the call is valid for every T or for none

area                      ; type: [[forall T with [Shape T]] T -> f64]  [7.5.2.2]
(trait [Plot T]
    area : [T -> f64])    ; error: area is already bound, as Shape's method  [7.5.2.1]
(define show = {fn |n : u32 -> string| (to_string n)})
    ; error: show is already bound, as Show's method  [7.5.2.1]
(trait [Convert To From]
    convert : [From -> To])
    ; convert : [[forall To From with [Convert To From]] From -> To]; the
    ; target comes first, so a call can give it alone  [7.5.2.2, 7.5.2.3]
(trait [Tagged T]
    tag : [[forall U] T U -> [Pair T U]])
    ; with Pair from 7.2; tag's forall list is [forall T U with [Tagged T]]  [7.5.2.4]
(trait [Named T]
    type_name : [-> string])  ; type_name does not mention T, so every call gives it (7.5.3)  [7.5.2.5]
(trait [VecAdd T]
    + : [T T -> T])       ; error: + is a built-in name  [7.5.2.6]
(do (let area = 10.0) area)   ; returns 10.0; warning: area hides Shape's method  [7.5.2.7]
(trait [Grow T]
    grow : [T f64 = _ -> T])  ; the second parameter is optional; each impl gives its default  [7.5.2.8]
(trait [Pad T]
    pad : [T u32 = _ char -> T])  ; error: only trailing parameters may be optional  [7.5.2.8]
(define nudge : [Circle f64 = _ -> Circle] = grow [Circle])
    ; error: = _ only appears in a required method's type  [7.5.2.8]

;; 7.5.3 impls
(impl <Bound> <Impl Entry>...) -> Unit
(impl <Forall> <Bound> <Impl Entry>...) -> Unit  ; a generic impl
; 1. impl implements a trait for the types in its header. The header is
;    written like a bound (7.5.4.1): the trait, then one type for each of
;    its parameters. impl may only appear at the top level.
; 2. Each entry is name = {fn ...}, and gives one of the trait's methods
;    for those types. The name is written bare, never qualified, even when
;    the trait comes from another module: the header already names the
;    trait.
; 3. Every required method must have an entry. An auto-implemented method
;    may have one, which then replaces the trait's body for those types:
;    the trait's body cannot be called from the impl, or extended. The
;    trait's other auto-implementations still apply. Replacing one never
;    warns.
;    why: no type ever has two definitions of one method
;    why: how often a method is replaced cannot be counted one module at a
;    time. If most impls should replace a method, the trait says so in the
;    method's doc, or makes the method required
; 4. An impl has no name. It cannot be bound, passed or exported. It is
;    usable wherever its trait and every type in its header are (9.6.7).
; 5. An impl may begin with a forall list, written exactly as a function's
;    (7.3), bounds included. Its type parameters can be used in the header
;    and in the methods.
; 6. Every type parameter of an impl must appear in its header.
;    why: lookup matches the header against a goal (7.5.7), so a parameter
;    outside the header would never be settled, and the impl could never
;    be compiled (7.3.7). Relaxing this later breaks nothing
; 7. Each method's type must equal the trait's declared type, with the
;    header's types in place of the trait's parameters. Nothing is loosened
;    or tightened. Every parameter the trait marks optional (7.5.2.8) must
;    have a default in the impl's fn, and no other parameter may have one.
; 8. A function type may appear in a header, like any built-in type:
;    [Arity [u8 -> u8]], or [Arity [A -> B]] under [forall A B]. One impl
;    covers one number of parameters (11.7.11). Whether a module may write
;    such an impl is set by the orphan rule (7.5.6.2).
; 9. A function type with a forall list of its own, such as
;    [[forall T] T -> T], cannot appear in an impl's header or in a bound.
;    A function type whose parameters come from the enclosing forall list,
;    such as [A -> B], is fine.
;    why: lookup and the overlap check (7.5.6.3) unify types, and unifying
;    under a forall list is not defined. Allowing it later only accepts
;    more programs

(impl [Shape Circle]
    area = {fn |c : Circle -> f64| (* 3.14 (* c.r c.r))})  ; double_area comes from the trait  [7.5.3.3]
(impl [Shape Square]
    area = {fn |s : Square -> f64| (* s.side s.side)}
    double_area = {fn |s : Square -> f64| (* 2.0 (* s.side s.side))})
    ; replaces the trait's double_area, for Square only  [7.5.3.3]
(area (Circle r = 1.0))            ; returns 3.14; T is Circle, so Circle's impl is used  [7.5.3.1]
(double_area (Circle r = 1.0))     ; returns 6.28, from the trait's body                  [7.5.3.3]
(double_area (Square side = 2.0))  ; returns 8.0, from Square's impl                      [7.5.3.3]
(area 5.0)                         ; error: f64 has no Shape impl                         [7.5.4.5]
(impl [Show Circle]
    show = {fn |c : Circle -> string| (`circle of radius ${r}` (to_string c.r))})
(impl [forall T with [Show T]] [Show [List T]]
    show = {fn |xs : [List T] -> string|
        (loop |i : u32 = 0 out : string = "" -> string|
            (match (get xs i)
                (Option.Some value = x) => (recur i = (+ i 1_u32) out = (+ out (show x)))
                ^Option.None            => out))})
    ; a generic impl: a list can be shown when its items can  [7.5.3.5]
(show (list (Circle r = 1.0) (Circle r = 2.0)))
    ; returns the two circles' texts, joined; List's impl, then Circle's  [7.5.3.5]
(struct Dot |x : f64|)
(impl [Shape Dot])                 ; error: the required method area has no entry  [7.5.3.3]
(impl [Show Square]
    show = {fn |s : Square n : u32 -> string| "square"})
    ; error: show must be a [Square -> string]  [7.5.3.7]
(impl [forall T U] [Show [Option T]] ...)  ; error: U does not appear in [Show [Option T]]  [7.5.3.6]
(impl [Grow Circle]
    grow = {fn |c : Circle by : f64 = 1.0 -> Circle| (Circle r = (+ c.r by))})
    ; the default that Grow asks each impl for  [7.5.3.7]
(impl [Grow Square]
    grow = {fn |s : Square by : f64 -> Square| (Square side = (+ s.side by))})
    ; error: Grow marks grow's second parameter optional; give it a default  [7.5.3.7]
(grow (Circle r = 1.0))            ; returns (Circle r = 2.0); Circle's default  [7.5.2.9]
(grow (Circle r = 1.0) 0.5)        ; returns (Circle r = 1.5)                    [7.5.2.9]
(impl [Named Circle]
    type_name = {fn |-> string| "Circle"})
(type_name [Circle])               ; returns "Circle"; T is given, since no argument could settle it  [7.5.2.5]
(type_name)                        ; error: nothing settles T                    [7.5.2.5]
(impl [Convert f64 Circle]
    convert = {fn |c : Circle -> f64| c.r})
(convert [f64] (Circle r = 2.0))           ; returns 2.0; To is given, From is settled by the argument  [7.5.2.3]
(convert [f64] [Circle] (Circle r = 2.0))  ; the same, with both given                                 [7.5.2.3]
(convert (Circle r = 2.0))                 ; error: nothing settles To                                 [7.5.2.3]
(trait [Arity F]
    arity : [F -> u32])
(impl [forall A B] [Arity [A -> B]]
    arity = {fn |_ : [A -> B] -> u32| 1})  ; every function of one parameter  [7.5.3.8]
(impl [forall A B C] [Arity [A B -> C]]
    arity = {fn |_ : [A B -> C] -> u32| 2})  ; two parameters need an impl of their own  [7.5.3.8]
(arity {fn |n : u8 -> u8| n})      ; returns 1                                   [7.5.3.8]
(impl [Arity [[forall T] T -> T]] ...)
    ; error: a generic function type cannot be implemented over  [7.5.3.9]
; with (mod geo), where geo exports a trait Solid with one method,
; volume : [T -> f64]
(impl [geo::Solid Circle]
    volume = {fn |_ : Circle -> f64| 0.0})       ; the method is written bare  [7.5.3.2]
(impl [geo::Solid Square]
    geo::volume = {fn |_ : Square -> f64| 0.0})  ; error: impl entries name methods bare  [7.5.3.2]
(geo::volume (Circle r = 2.0))                   ; returns 0.0; qualified at the call  [7.5.2.7]

;; 7.5.4 bounds
[forall <Identifier> <Identifier>... with <Bound> <Bound>...]
; 1. A bound is a full trait application, [Trait Type...], written like a
;    generic type with its arguments: one type for each of the trait's
;    parameters. A forall list gives its type parameters, then with, then
;    its bounds. A parameter with no bound simply appears in none.
;    why: one form covers traits with one parameter and with several, and
;    no trait parameter is privileged as a subject
;    The one-parameter case is wordy. Sugar for it may come later
;    (11.7.15).
; 2. A bound may name concrete types, and any type parameter in scope: its
;    own forall list's or an enclosing one's (7.1.2). Every bound must name
;    at least one type parameter. A bound with none says nothing about the
;    generic, so it is an error.
; 3. Only functions and impls take bounds. The header of a struct or enum
;    (7.2) takes none, so a type cannot require a trait of its parameters.
;    Every function that needs the trait states the bound itself. Allowing
;    bounds in headers later breaks no program.
; 4. A generic body may call a trait's method on a type parameter only if
;    a bound, with its supertraits added (7.5.5.4), covers that use. The
;    body is checked once, before any call, and never again for each call
;    (7.3.6).
; 5. At each call, the type arguments, given or settled (7.4), are put
;    into the bounds, and each resulting goal must be proved by impl
;    lookup (7.5.7). The error points at the type argument, or at the
;    argument that settled it.
; 6. Inside a generic body, a goal over its type parameters is proved by
;    the bounds in scope. So a generic with [Shape T] may call another
;    generic that requires [Shape T]. An inner generic's bound that names
;    an outer parameter is proved the same way, from the outer forall
;    list's bounds, since the outer parameter is still abstract there.
; 7. Duplicate bounds are allowed: with [Shape T] [Shape T] means the same
;    as with [Shape T]. So is a bound that another bound already implies
;    through a supertrait (7.5.5.4).
; 8. A function type with a forall list of its own cannot appear in a
;    bound (7.5.3.9), and a bounded type parameter cannot be settled as
;    one.

(define total_area = {fn [forall T with [Shape T]] |a : T b : T -> f64|
    (+ (area a) (area b))})                         ; area is covered by [Shape T]  [7.5.4.4]
(total_area (Circle r = 1.0) (Circle r = 2.0))
    ; returns 15.7; T is Circle, and [Shape Circle] is proved by Circle's impl  [7.5.4.5]
(total_area 1.0 2.0)                                ; error: f64 has no Shape impl  [7.5.4.5]
(total_area (Circle r = 1.0) (Square side = 1.0))
    ; error: T is Circle from the first argument but Square from the second  [7.4.9]
{fn [forall T] |a : T -> f64| (area a)}             ; error: no [Shape T] bound     [7.5.4.4]
(define show_area = {fn [forall T U with [Shape T] [Show T]] |x : T _ : U -> string|
    (+ (show x) (to_string (total_area x x)))})
    ; U has no bound; total_area's [Shape T] is proved by this fn's own bound  [7.5.4.1, 7.5.4.6]
{fn [forall T with [Convert f64 T]] |x : T -> f64| (convert [f64] x)}
    ; a bound may name a concrete type: any T that converts to f64  [7.5.4.2]
{fn [forall T with [Convert f64 Circle]] |x : T -> T| x}
    ; error: the bound [Convert f64 Circle] names no type parameter  [7.5.4.2]
{fn [forall T with [Shape T] [Convert T f64]] |x : f64 -> f64| (do
    (let via = {fn [forall U with [Convert T U]] |u : U -> f64| (area (convert [T] u))})
    (via x))}
    ; the inner bound names the outer T; at (via x), [Convert T f64] is
    ; proved by the outer bound  [7.5.4.2, 7.5.4.6]
{fn [forall T with [Shape T] [Shape T]] |x : T -> f64| (area x)}  ; a duplicate bound is fine  [7.5.4.7]
(struct [Sheet T with [Shape T]] |items : [List T]|)  ; error: a type header takes no bounds  [7.5.4.3]
; hypothetical: Add is a prelude trait (8.2)
{fn [forall T with [Add T]] |a : T b : T -> T| (+ a b)}  ; + is covered by [Add T]  [7.5.4.4]
{fn [forall T] |a : T b : T -> T| (+ a b)}               ; error: no [Add T] bound  [7.5.4.4]

;; 7.5.5 supertraits
(trait <Trait Header> with <Bound> <Bound>... <Trait Entry>...) -> Unit
; 1. A trait may list supertraits after its header: with, then bounds
;    (7.5.4.1). The list ends at the first entry. A bound is a [ ] form and
;    an entry starts with a method's name, so the two cannot be confused.
; 2. A supertrait bound names only the trait's own parameters, bare:
;    [Shape T] is allowed. [Shape [List T]], [Shape u8] and parameters that
;    are not the trait's are errors.
;    why: a supertrait goal is then never bigger than the goal that implied
;    it, so the size rule (7.5.7.1) needs no extra case. Relaxing this
;    later breaks nothing
; 3. Supertraits are required at the impl. An impl of a trait is an error
;    unless each of the trait's supertraits, at the impl's types, can be
;    proved from the impl's own bounds (rule 4) and the impls its module
;    can find (7.5.6.4). The error is at the impl and names the missing
;    impl. Only direct supertraits are checked: each of their impls was
;    checked for its own supertraits in turn, so every supertrait further
;    up holds too.
; 4. Supertraits are implied at the bound, transitively. A bound stands
;    for itself, plus every supertrait at the same types, plus their
;    supertraits, and so on. This holds everywhere a bound does: in
;    generic bodies (7.5.4.4), in auto-implementations (7.5.1.6), in a
;    generic impl's bounds when its own supertraits are proved (rule 3),
;    in bounds that pass through (7.5.4.6), and in the overlap check
;    (7.5.6.5).
; 5. A trait that reaches itself by following supertraits is an error at
;    its declaration. The error names every trait in the cycle. So adding
;    supertraits to a bound always finishes.
; 6. A trait with supertraits and no methods (7.5.1.9) works as one name
;    for several bounds.

(trait [Solid T] with [Shape T]
    height : [T -> f64]
    volume = {fn |s : T -> f64| (* (area s) (height s))})
    ; Shape is a supertrait, so volume may call area  [7.5.5.1, 7.5.5.4]
(struct Can |base : Circle tall : f64|)
(impl [Shape Can]
    area = {fn |c : Can -> f64| (area c.base)})
(impl [Solid Can]
    height = {fn |c : Can -> f64| c.tall})   ; ok: Can has a Shape impl  [7.5.5.3]
(struct Rod |long : f64|)
(impl [Solid Rod]
    height = {fn |r : Rod -> f64| r.long})   ; error: Solid needs [Shape Rod], and Rod has no Shape impl  [7.5.5.3]
(define bulk = {fn [forall T with [Solid T]] |x : T -> f64| (+ (area x) (volume x))})
    ; [Solid T] implies [Shape T], so area is covered  [7.5.5.4]
{fn [forall T with [Solid T] [Shape T]] |x : T -> f64| (area x)}
    ; fine: [Shape T] repeats what [Solid T] implies  [7.5.4.7]
(trait [Drawable T] with [Shape T] [Show T])  ; no methods: one name for two bounds  [7.5.5.6]
(trait [Stack T] with [Shape [List T]])
    ; error: a supertrait bound names the trait's parameters bare  [7.5.5.2]
(trait [Above T] with [Below T])
(trait [Below T] with [Above T])   ; error: supertrait cycle Above -> Below -> Above  [7.5.5.5]

;; 7.5.6 coherence
; For each trait and each tuple of types, a whole program holds at most one
; impl. These rules ensure it while checking one module at a time.
; 1. The orphan rule: an impl with the header [Trait A1 ... An] may be
;    written in a module only if
;    a. the module defines Trait; or
;    b. some Ai is a type the module defines, and no Aj before it contains
;       an uncovered type parameter of the impl. A type parameter is
;       uncovered when it appears bare, as T, and covered when it is an
;       argument of another type, as in [List T].
;    For a trait with one parameter, this is "the trait's module or the
;    type's module".
;    why: for any one impl, at most one module besides the trait's can
;    satisfy the rule, so two modules cannot write the same impl without
;    one of them seeing the other
; 2. A type the module defines is a struct or enum declared in it, with any
;    type arguments. A built-in generic type is never local to a module,
;    and neither is a function type, even when it mentions the module's
;    own types: [List Meters] and [Meters -> u8] are no more local to the
;    module of Meters than List is. To implement a trait over such a type
;    outside the trait's module, wrap it in a struct the module defines.
;    why: otherwise [Point -> Meters] would be local to two modules, and
;    both could write the same impl unnoticed
; 3. Two impls of one trait may not overlap. They overlap when their
;    headers unify, with each impl's type parameters renamed apart and all
;    positions unified together, and the bounds of both impls could hold
;    at once for the unified types. There is no specialization: no impl
;    wins over another.
; 4. A module finds impls, and checks overlap, in itself and in every
;    module it can reach by following mod forms (9.4), internal modules
;    included (9.5). This is the module's search scope.
;    why: by rule 1, an impl lives in its trait's module or in the module
;    of a type in its header. A call that needs the impl involves the
;    trait and every one of those types, so the impl's module is always
;    among the caller's transitive imports
; 5. Whether the bounds of two impls could hold at once is judged by the
;    impls in the checking module's search scope, with supertraits added
;    (7.5.5.4).
;    why: it is the same lookup a call in that module would run, so the
;    overlap check and impl lookup never disagree
;    Adding an impl can therefore make two other impls overlap. The error
;    is reported in each module whose search scope holds all three. Impls
;    that only a missing impl keeps apart warn before that (7.5.8).
; 6. Two impls with the same header always overlap. So a program holds at
;    most one impl for each trait and tuple of types, even across modules.

; in a module units, which defines (struct Meters |n : f64|) and imports a
; module conv that defines Convert and Show. Each impl is read alone, as if
; it were the only one in the program:
(impl [conv::Convert Meters u32] ...)                  ; ok: Meters is local                        [7.5.6.1]
(impl [conv::Convert f64 Meters] ...)                  ; ok: f64 holds no type parameter            [7.5.6.1]
(impl [forall T] [conv::Convert Meters T] ...)         ; ok: Meters comes before T                  [7.5.6.1]
(impl [forall T] [conv::Convert [List T] Meters] ...)  ; ok: T is covered by List                   [7.5.6.1]
(impl [forall T] [conv::Convert T Meters] ...)         ; error: T is uncovered before Meters        [7.5.6.1]
(impl [conv::Show [List Meters]] ...)                  ; error: [List Meters] is not a local type   [7.5.6.2]
(impl [conv::Show [Meters -> u8]] ...)                 ; error: [Meters -> u8] is not a local type  [7.5.6.2]
(struct Gauge |read : [Meters -> u8]|)
(impl [conv::Show Gauge] ...)                          ; ok: the function is wrapped in a local struct  [7.5.6.2]
; in one module, with the traits and impls of 7.5.1 to 7.5.5:
(impl [forall T] [Marker [Option T]])
(impl [Marker [Option u8]])        ; error: overlaps the impl above at [Option u8]  [7.5.6.3]
(impl [forall T] [Convert T T] ...)
(impl [Convert u32 u32] ...)       ; error: overlaps the impl above at (u32, u32)   [7.5.6.3]
(struct Handler |run : [u8 -> u8]|)
(impl [forall T with [Shape T]] [Marker [List T]])
(impl [Marker [List Handler]])
    ; ok while Handler has no Shape impl: the bound [Shape Handler] cannot
    ; hold; warning: latent overlap (7.5.8)  [7.5.6.3, 7.5.6.5]
(impl [Shape Handler] ...)         ; adding this makes the two impls above overlap: an error  [7.5.6.5]
(impl [Shape Circle] ...)          ; error: Circle already has a Shape impl         [7.5.6.6]
; conv::widen defines Convert, and holds (impl [Convert f64 meters::Meters] ...)
; geo::area imports conv::widen, and defines
;   (define total = {fn [forall T with [widen::Convert f64 T]] |xs : [List T] -> f64| ...})
; main imports units::meters and geo::area, but not conv::widen. In main,
; with m1 and m2 of type meters::Meters:
(area::total (list m1 m2))         ; ok: conv::widen is reached through geo::area  [7.5.6.4]

;; 7.5.7 impl lookup
; Proving a goal such as [Show [List Circle]] means finding the impl whose
; header matches it, then proving that impl's bounds, which are goals in
; turn. The overlap check (7.5.6.3) runs the same lookups. These rules make
; every lookup finish.
; 1. An impl's bounds must not grow. For each bound in an impl's forall
;    list, compared with the impl's header:
;    a. no type parameter appears more times in the bound than in the
;       header; and
;    b. the bound is no bigger than the header (rule 2).
;    The check is made at the impl, in its own module, and the error is at
;    the impl.
;    why a: otherwise filling T in with a big type makes the bound grow,
;    though it looks small
; 2. Size counts 1 for every type name and for every occurrence of a type
;    parameter. Built-in type names (u8, List, Map, string, Box) count the
;    same as any other. A function type counts 1, plus the sizes of its
;    parameter types and its output type: [u8 -> T] has size 3, and [-> T]
;    size 2. For a trait with several parameters, size is totalled across
;    all positions: [Convert [List T] u8] has size 3. The trait's name is
;    not counted.
;    why: every name counts at least 1, so there are finitely many goals of
;    any given size. If Box or a function type were free, a lookup could go
;    from [C T] to [C [Box T]] to [C [Box [Box T]]] forever, without a goal
;    ever repeating
;    why totalled: it is the loosest measure that still terminates, and an
;    impl may move size between positions
; 3. A lookup keeps a stack of the goals it is proving. A new goal that
;    exactly matches one already on the stack is an error: the goal depends
;    on itself. Type parameters that are still abstract count as distinct
;    named types for this comparison.
;    why: an impl is a proof that needs its bounds proved first, and a
;    cycle never reaches an impl without bounds. An impl that vouches for
;    itself then never holds, instead of holding for every type and looping
;    at run time
;    why: a failed goal can be cached. Each goal has at most one impl
;    (7.5.6.6) and there is no backtracking, so every goal on a cycle fails
;    from any starting point
;    Letting a repeat succeed when the cycle follows the structure of a
;    type is a possible later step (11.7.14). It would only accept more
;    programs.
; 4. With rules 1 and 3, every lookup finishes. No goal in a lookup is
;    bigger than the starting goal, and goals are built only from the type
;    names in the starting goal and the concrete types written in impl
;    bounds. That is finitely many goals, so a chain that never repeats
;    must end. Neither rule is enough alone.
; 5. The repeat error appears where the goal is needed, which may be in
;    another module than the impls, since each impl alone passes rule 1.
;    Impls for types that refer to each other avoid it by bounding the type
;    parameters, not the types: an impl may use itself and the other impls
;    in its body. The error's wording is open (11.7.10).

(struct [Crate T] |item : T|)
(impl [forall T with [Show T]] [Show [Crate T]] ...)
    ; ok: the bound has size 1, the header size 2  [7.5.7.1, 7.5.7.2]
(impl [forall T with [Show [List T]]] [Marker [Crate T]])
    ; ok: the bound and the header both have size 2  [7.5.7.1]
(impl [forall T with [Show [Crate [Crate T]]]] [Shape [Crate T]] ...)
    ; error: the bound (3) is bigger than the header (2)  [7.5.7.1]
(impl [forall T with [Show [Pair T T]]] [Named [Crate T]] ...)
    ; error: T appears twice in the bound, once in the header  [7.5.7.1]
(impl [forall T with [Show [-> T]]] [Arity [Crate T]] ...)
    ; ok: [-> T] has size 2, and so does [Crate T]  [7.5.7.2]
(trait [Ping T] ping : [T -> string])
(trait [Pong T] pong : [T -> string])
(impl [forall T with [Pong [Crate T]]] [Ping [Crate T]] ...)
(impl [forall T with [Ping [Crate T]]] [Pong [Crate T]] ...)  ; each impl passes rule 1  [7.5.7.1]
(ping (Crate [u8] item = 1))
    ; error: [Ping [Crate u8]] needs [Pong [Crate u8]], which needs
    ; [Ping [Crate u8]]: the goal repeats  [7.5.7.3]
(struct [Twig T]  |value : T kids : [Grove T]|)
(struct [Grove T] |twigs : [List [Twig T]]|)
(impl [forall T with [Show T]] [Show [Twig T]] ...)
(impl [forall T with [Show T]] [Show [Grove T]] ...)
    ; both bound only T. Each body may use the other impl: Grove's needs
    ; [Show [List [Twig T]]], which List's impl takes to [Show [Twig T]],
    ; and Twig's impl takes that to [Show T]. Nothing repeats  [7.5.7.5]

;; 7.5.8 latent overlap
; 1. Two impls whose headers unify, and that are kept apart only because
;    some bound does not hold (7.5.6.3), are a warning. There are two
;    lints (4.7.3):
;    - latent_overlap: at least one goal that keeps them apart has no type
;      parameter, so adding one concrete impl would make them overlap.
;    - latent_overlap_generic: every goal that keeps them apart mentions a
;      type parameter. The impls overlap as soon as any one type satisfies
;      all those goals at once, so no single impl is the missing one.
;    why: authors see where a type's impls sit in the chain built from
;    smaller types' impls, instead of finding out when someone adds an impl
;    why two lints: the generic case fires on nearly every pair of blanket
;    impls with different bounds. A module can silence it alone, and keep
;    the concrete case
; 2. The warning names both impls, the types where their headers meet, and
;    every chain that keeps them apart, in full. A chain starts at a bound
;    of one of the impls that fails at the meeting types, and follows each
;    failing bound down to a goal with no impl at all. Each step names the
;    impl whose bound led to it. For each final goal, the warning says
;    where that impl could be written: by the orphan rule (7.5.6.1), the
;    trait's module or the module of a type in the goal. For
;    latent_overlap_generic, the warning lists the goals that would all
;    have to hold for one type, and the chain under each where one exists.
;    The wording is open (11.7.10).
; 3. A module reports the warning for a pair of impls when both are in its
;    search scope (7.5.6.4) and no single module it imports directly
;    already has both in its own scope. That is the module where the two
;    impls first meet.
;    why: it fires once for each place the risk first appears, not in every
;    module downstream, and it is still computed one module at a time
;    - Cost: the module that gets it may have written neither impl. A ;!!
;      covers that (rule 5).
; 4. The warning would ideally go to whoever would add the missing impl,
;    but that module usually cannot see the two impls. The warning names
;    that module instead (rule 2).
; 5. Silencing, with lint commands (4.7):
;    - ;! allow latent_overlap <Bound> on either impl says that the author
;      knows the goal keeps it apart from another impl, and expects the
;      goal never to hold. It silences every warning that involves the impl
;      and has that goal on one of its chains, in every module where it
;      would be reported: it travels with the impl. Naming any one goal is
;      enough.
;    - The goal may name the impl's own type parameters, as written in its
;      forall list: ;! allow latent_overlap_generic [Show T].
;    - With no goal, the command silences every warning of that lint that
;      involves the impl.
;    - ;!! allow, with or without a goal, silences the warnings reported in
;      that module. That is how a module that wrote neither impl
;      acknowledges them.
; 6. A ;! only silences. It is not a promise the compiler holds anyone to,
;    and it never forbids the goal's impl. If that impl is added, the
;    overlap is an error as usual (7.5.6.5).
;    why: an enforced promise would be a negative impl, a whole feature for
;    an edge case

; in a module show, which defines Shape and Marker:
(impl [forall T with [Shape T]] [Marker [List T]])
; in a module handler, which imports show and defines Handler:
(impl [Marker [List Handler]])
    ; warning: the two impls would overlap if [Shape Handler] were added
    ; (latent_overlap); reported here, where they first meet  [7.5.8.1, 7.5.8.3]
; a sketch of that warning:
;   warning[latent_overlap]: these impls would overlap if [Shape Handler] were added
;     impl at show.tsc:4     [forall T with [Shape T]] [Marker [List T]]
;     impl at handler.tsc:9  [Marker [List Handler]]
;     they meet at [Marker [List Handler]]
;     kept apart by:
;       [Shape Handler]      (bound of impl at show.tsc:4)
;         no impl; it could be written in show or in handler
;   hint: ;! allow latent_overlap [Shape Handler]                       [7.5.8.2]
; in a module app, which imports handler: no warning, since handler already
; has both impls  [7.5.8.3]
; in handler again, with the command:
;! allow latent_overlap [Shape Handler]
(impl [Marker [List Handler]])     ; no warning, here or downstream  [7.5.8.5]
; in a program where no type has both a Shape impl and a Show impl:
(impl [forall T with [Shape T]] [Marker [Option T]])
(impl [forall U with [Show U]] [Marker [Option U]])
    ; warning: the impls overlap as soon as one type has both Shape and
    ; Show (latent_overlap_generic)  [7.5.8.1]

;;;; 8 built-ins

;;; 8.1 functions and special forms
; 1. A built-in that only takes input values and returns an output, with no
;    side effects, is a function. Like any function value, it can be bound,
;    passed and returned.
; 2. The other built-ins are special forms: print, panic, assert and debug.
;    Like keywords, they can only be called, never passed, and no binding
;    may take their names (4.1.9). A fn that calls one is still an ordinary
;    function value.
; 3. A built-in function that works on several types, such as +, is the
;    method of a trait (7.5.2), and each type it works on has an impl
;    (7.5.3). The built-in traits will be defined in the prelude (11.8.5),
;    and are not designed yet (todo, 11.7.8). Until then, each entry of
;    this section names its traits by their working names, such as Add for
;    +, and lists the types that implement them. Passing such a function
;    as a value needs type arguments, because it is generic (7.4.8):
;    + [u32].
; 4. list takes any number of arguments, so it has no single function type
;    and cannot be passed as a value. Wrap it in a fn instead. It is a
;    special built-in, not a trait method (8.7.1).
; 5. The casts take their target type as a type argument: (as [i64] x)
;    (7.4.8, 8.6).
; 6. A program cannot take a built-in name for a method of its own
;    (7.5.2.6). It gives its own types an operator by implementing the
;    operator's trait.

(define add_u32 = + [u32])     ; add_u32 : [u32 u32 -> u32]                    [8.1.3]
(define join = + [string])     ; + as string concatenation                     [8.1.3]
(define plus = +)              ; error: nothing settles T; write + [T]         [8.1.3]
(define combine = {fn [forall T] |a : T b : T f : [T T -> T] -> T| (f a b)})
(combine [i32] 2 3 + [i32])    ; returns 5; + [i32] is passed like any function  [8.1.1]
(combine [string] "ab" "cd" + [string])  ; returns "abcd"                        [8.1.3]
(combine [i32] 2 3 max [i32])  ; returns 3                                       [8.1.1]
(define three = {fn |a : i32 b : i32 c : i32 -> [List i32]| (list a b c)})
(three 1 2 3)                  ; returns (list 1 2 3); list wrapped in a fn      [8.1.4]
(define bad_three : [i32 i32 i32 -> [List i32]] = list)
    ; error: list cannot be passed as a value  [8.1.4]
(define say : [string -> Unit] = print)  ; error: print is a special form                     [8.1.2]
(define shout = {fn |s : string -> Unit| (print s)})  ; a fn that calls print is fine         [8.1.2]
(as [i64] 5)                                          ; the target type is a type argument    [8.1.5]
(struct Vec2 |x : f64 y : f64|)
; hypothetical: Add is a prelude trait (8.2)
(impl [Add Vec2]
    + = {fn |a : Vec2 b : Vec2 -> Vec2| (Vec2 x = (+ a.x b.x) y = (+ a.y b.y))})
(+ (Vec2 x = 1.0 y = 2.0) (Vec2 x = 3.0 y = 4.0))  ; returns (Vec2 x = 4.0 y = 6.0)        [8.1.6]

;;; 8.2 arithmetic
(+ a b)        ; addition; numerical types and string
(- a b)        ; subtraction; numerical types
(* a b)        ; multiplication; numerical types
(/ a b)        ; division; numerical types
(% a b)        ; modulo, a mod b; numerical types
(pow a b)      ; a to the power b; numerical types
(abs a)        ; absolute value; numerical types
(min a b)      ; the smaller of a and b; numerical types
(max a b)      ; the larger of a and b; numerical types
(clamp a b c)  ; a, kept between b and c; numerical types
(neg a)        ; negation; signed integer types and float types
(zero [T])     ; the T that is 0; numerical types
(one [T])      ; the T that is 1; numerical types
; 1. All operands have the same type, which is also the type of the result.
; 2. - always takes two arguments. Negation is neg. The signed integer
;    types, isize and ibig included, and the float types implement it. The
;    unsigned types, usize and ubig included, do not. There is no sugar:
;    (- a) is an error.
;    why: - is then an ordinary method of two parameters, with no default
;    that fills a first parameter, and negating an unsigned value is a
;    compile error instead of an overflow
; 3. + on two strings joins them. Whether that stays, or joining gets a
;    name of its own, is open (11.7.8).
; 4. / on integers gives an integer, by rule 1. How it rounds negative
;    results, and what division by zero does, is open (11.5.4).
; 5. (clamp a b c) returns b if a < b, c if a > c, and a otherwise.
; 6. zero and one take no arguments, so their T is always given (7.4.8).
;    They are how a generic body writes 0 and 1, since a literal is never
;    a value of a type parameter (5.1.1.5).
; 7. All of these are methods of prelude traits (8.1.3, todo). The working
;    names are one trait for each operation: Add for +, Subtract, Multiply,
;    Divide, Remainder, Pow, Abs, Neg, Zero and One. Whether they become
;    one Num trait, and whether min, max and clamp move to Ord (8.3.5), is
;    open (11.7.8). What overflow does is set for each impl (11.5.3).

(+ 2 3)          ; returns 5                                            [8.2.1]
(+ 2 "cd")       ; error: an i32 and a string                           [8.2.1]
(let small : u8 = 1)
(+ small 2.0)    ; error: a u8 and an f64; nothing converts implicitly  [8.2.1]
(- 7 2)          ; returns 5                                            [8.2.1]
(neg 5)          ; returns -5                                           [8.2.2]
(- 5)            ; error: - takes two arguments; write (neg 5)          [8.2.2]
(neg 5_u32)      ; error: u32 has no Neg impl                           [8.2.2]
(zero [u32])     ; returns 0, a u32                                     [8.2.6]
(one [f64])      ; returns 1.0                                          [8.2.6]
(zero)           ; error: nothing settles T                             [8.2.6]
(+ "ab" "cd")    ; returns "abcd"                                       [8.2.3]
(/ 7.0 2.0)      ; returns 3.5                                          [8.2.4]
(/ 7 2)          ; returns 3, an i32                                    [8.2.4]
(* 2.5 2.0)      ; returns 5.0                                          [8.2.1]
(% 7 3)          ; returns 1                                            [8.2.1]
(pow 2 10)       ; returns 1024                                         [8.2.1]
(abs -4)         ; returns 4                                            [8.2.1]
(min 3 8)        ; returns 3                                            [8.2.1]
(max 3 8)        ; returns 8                                            [8.2.1]
(clamp 15 0 10)  ; returns 10                                           [8.2.5]
(clamp -5 0 10)  ; returns 0                                            [8.2.5]
(clamp 5 0 10)   ; returns 5                                            [8.2.5]
; hypothetical: Add and Zero are prelude traits (8.2.7)
(define sum_of = {fn [forall T with [Add T] [Zero T]] |xs : [List T] -> T|
    (loop |i : u32 = 0 total : T = (zero [T]) -> T|
        (match (get xs i)
            (Option.Some value = x) => (recur i = (+ i 1_u32) total = (+ total x))
            ^Option.None            => total))})  ; the body's 0 is (zero [T])  [8.2.6]
(sum_of (list 1.5 2.5))  ; returns 4.0; T is f64                        [8.2.6]
; hypothetical: Add is a prelude trait (8.2.7)
{fn [forall T with [Add T]] |x : T -> T| (+ x 1)}
    ; error: T is T from x but i32 from 1; a literal is never a T (5.1.1.5)  [8.2.6]

;;; 8.3 comparison
(== a b)  ; equal
(< a b)   ; a < b
(> a b)   ; a > b
(<= a b)  ; a <= b
(>= a b)  ; a >= b
; 1. Both operands have the same type, and the result is a bool.
; 2. == works on every type except function types. Comparing functions is
;    a compile error.
;    why?: whether two functions are equal cannot be decided in general
; 3. NaN is not equal to NaN, and every ordering comparison that involves
;    a quiet NaN is false. A signaling NaN panics at once in any operation
;    that receives it as an operand: arithmetic, a comparison, a cast or
;    to_string. It never propagates, so only quiet NaNs reach a comparison.
;    When the check runs is open (11.7.8).
;    why?: this follows IEEE 754 floating point
; 4. <, >, <= and >= work on numerical types only. Whether string, char
;    and bool get them is open (11.3.3, 11.7.8).
; 5. These are methods of prelude traits (8.1.3, todo). The working design
;    is four traits, linked by supertraits (7.5.5): == is the method of
;    PartialEq, and < of PartialOrd, whose >, <= and >= are
;    auto-implemented from <. Eq, over PartialEq, and Ord, over Eq and
;    PartialOrd, add no operators, only promises: that == is reflexive,
;    and that every pair is ordered. The float types implement only
;    PartialEq and PartialOrd, since NaN breaks both promises. Every other
;    type with == implements Eq too, and every other ordered type Ord. No
;    function type implements any of them (3.3.6). The names and the split
;    are to be confirmed (11.7.8).
;    - Map keys, sets and sorting will require Eq or Ord, so floats cannot
;      be used there without a wrapper type.

(== 2 2)                            ; returns true                         [8.3.1]
(== 2 "2")                          ; error: an i32 and a string           [8.3.1]
(== "a" "b")                        ; returns false                        [8.3.2]
(== Weapon.Fist Weapon.Fist)        ; returns true                         [8.3.2]
(== {make_adder 1} {make_adder 1})  ; error: functions cannot be compared  [8.3.2]
; hypothetical: assumes (/ 0.0 0.0) is NaN (11.5.4)
(let nan : f64 = (/ 0.0 0.0))
(== nan nan)  ; returns false                      [8.3.3]
(< 2 3)       ; returns true                       [8.3.4]
(>= 3 3)      ; returns true                       [8.3.4]
(<= 4 3)      ; returns false                      [8.3.4]
(> 2.5 2.4)   ; returns true                       [8.3.4]
(< "a" "b")   ; error: < needs numerical operands  [8.3.4]
(< nan 1.0)   ; returns false                      [8.3.3]
(>= nan nan)  ; returns false                      [8.3.3]

;;; 8.4 boolean logic
(not a)    ; logical not
(and a b)  ; logical and
(or a b)   ; logical or
(xor a b)  ; logical exclusive or
; 1. The operands and the result are bools.
; 2. and, or and xor are eager. As functions (8.1.1), they evaluate both
;    operands before the call, even when the first one decides the result.
; 3. These are plain functions on bool, not trait methods (8.1.3), so they
;    are passed as values without type arguments.

(not true)                    ; returns false                                              [8.4.1]
(and true false)              ; returns false                                              [8.4.1]
(or true false)               ; returns true                                               [8.4.1]
(xor true true)               ; returns false                                              [8.4.1]
(and 1 0)                     ; error: the operands must be bools                          [8.4.1]
(define both = and)           ; both : [bool bool -> bool]; no type arguments are needed   [8.4.3]
(or true (do (print "evaluated") false))  ; prints: evaluated; returns true                [8.4.2]
(let numbers : [List i32] = (list 1 2 3))
(and false (== numbers.9 1))  ; panics: index out of range; the second operand still runs  [8.4.2]

;;; 8.5 bitwise
(bit_not a)    ; bitwise not
(bit_and a b)  ; bitwise and
(bit_or a b)   ; bitwise or
(bit_xor a b)  ; bitwise exclusive or
(shl a n)      ; shift a left by n bits
(shr a n)      ; shift a right by n bits
; 1. These work on all stack types, which include the fixed-size integer
;    types (3.1.4) and the size types (3.1.9). What else counts as a stack
;    type, including whether ubig and ibig do, is open (11.3.2).
; 2. n defaults to 1 in shl and shr. n is written as a u32 in their
;    signatures, so an unsuffixed literal there is a u32 (5.1.1.2).
; 3. These are methods of a prelude trait (8.1.3, todo), with the working
;    name Bits. The trait marks n optional, and each impl gives the
;    default 1 (7.5.2.8). Which types implement it is the question of
;    rule 1 (11.3.2).

(let b : u8 = 12)  ; binary 00001100               [8.5.1]
(bit_and b 10_u8)  ; returns 8, binary 00001000    [8.5.1]
(bit_or b 10_u8)   ; returns 14, binary 00001110   [8.5.1]
(bit_xor b 10_u8)  ; returns 6, binary 00000110    [8.5.1]
(bit_not b)        ; returns 243, binary 11110011  [8.5.1]
(shl b 2)          ; returns 48; the 2 is a u32    [8.5.2]
(shr b 2)          ; returns 3                     [8.5.1]
(shl b)            ; returns 24                    [8.5.2]

;;; 8.6 casting and conversion
(as [<Type>] a)      ; widening
(try_as [<Type>] a)  ; checked: returns [Option <Type>]
(wrap [<Type>] a)    ; wrapping: wraps around on overflow
(parse [<Type>] a)   ; parsing a string: returns [Result <Type> ParseError]
(to_string a)        ; text: works on every type
; 1. The casts are the only way to change a value's type (3.1.3). Each cast
;    is the method of a trait over two types, the target first and the
;    source second: as is the method of [Widen To From] (7.5.2.3). No
;    argument settles the target, so a call gives it as its first type
;    argument (7.4.8), and the source type comes from the argument.
; 2. as only compiles when the target type is wider than the value's type,
;    so it never fails. u32 is wider than char: (as [u32] c) gives the code
;    point of c (1.3.3.4). Within each family, a larger type is wider:
;    u8 to u256 then ubig, i8 to i256 then ibig, and f32 to f256. usize
;    sits between u32 and u64, and isize between i32 and i64, since a size
;    type is 32 bits on some targets and 64 on others (3.1.10). ibig is also
;    wider than every u type and ubig. Which other conversions count as
;    wider is open (11.7.3).
; 3. try_as returns Option.None when the value does not fit the target type.
; 4. wrap wraps around on overflow. It is native for numerical types. Its
;    target may not be ubig or ibig: a big type has no size to wrap at. A
;    size type wraps at the target's width (3.1.10).
; 5. parse reads a string as a value of the target type. It returns
;    Result.Error when the string is not a valid value of that type.
; 6. to_string turns a value of any type into text.
; 7. Widen has one impl for each pair that rule 2 calls wider, and an
;    identity impl, [Widen T T], for every numerical type. A generic body
;    writes a constant other than 0 and 1 (8.2.6) by casting a suffixed
;    literal under a bound: (as [T] 10_u8) with [Widen T u8]. The identity
;    impls let T be u8 itself there. The source type limits which types
;    the generic accepts: u8 does not widen to i8.
; 8. The traits are prelude traits (8.1.3, todo). Widen is decided. The
;    working names for the others are TryAs and Wrap, shaped like Widen,
;    Parse for parse, and ToString for to_string. Which pairs each has,
;    and whether a program may add Widen impls for its own types, is open
;    (11.7.3, 11.7.8).

(let n : u32 = 7)
(let m : u64 = n)                 ; error: nothing converts implicitly; write (as [u64] n)       [8.6.1]
(as u64 n)                        ; error: u64 is a type, not a value; write [u64]               [8.6.1]
(as [i64] 5)                      ; returns 5 as an i64; i64 is wider than i32                   [8.6.2]
(as [u8] 5)                       ; error: u8 is not wider than i32                              [8.6.2]
(try_as [u8] 200)                 ; returns (Option.Some [u8] value = 200)                       [8.6.3]
(try_as [u8] 300)                 ; returns Option.None [u8]: 300 does not fit in a u8           [8.6.3]
(as [u32] 'A')                    ; returns 65, the code point of A                              [8.6.2]
(try_as [char] 65_u32)            ; returns (Option.Some [char] value = 'A')                     [8.6.3]
(try_as [char] 0xD800_u32)        ; returns Option.None [char]: a surrogate is not a char        [8.6.3]
(as [i128] 5)                     ; returns 5 as an i128                                         [8.6.2]
(as [ibig] 7_u64)                 ; returns 7 as an ibig; ibig holds every integer               [8.6.2]
(as [f256] 2.5)                   ; returns 2.5 as an f256                                       [8.6.2]
(as [u64] 7_ubig)                 ; error: u64 is not wider than ubig; use try_as                [8.6.2]
(as [usize] 7_u32)                ; returns 7 as a usize; a usize has at least 32 bits           [8.6.2]
(as [u64] 7_usize)                ; returns 7 as a u64; a usize has at most 64 bits              [8.6.2]
(as [usize] 7_u64)                ; error: a usize may have only 32 bits; use try_as             [8.6.2]
(as [isize] 7_usize)              ; error: isize is not in the u family; use try_as (11.7.3)     [8.6.2]
(try_as [u64] (pow [ubig] 2 64))  ; returns Option.None [u64]: 2^64 does not fit          [8.6.3]
(try_as [usize] 5000000000_u64)   ; returns Option.None [usize] on a 32-bit target only   [8.6.3]
(wrap [u8] 300)                   ; returns 44, which is 300 - 256                               [8.6.4]
(wrap [u64] (pow [ubig] 2 64))    ; returns 0                                             [8.6.4]
(wrap [ubig] -5)                  ; error: ubig has no size to wrap at                           [8.6.4]
(wrap [usize] -1)                 ; returns 2^32 - 1 or 2^64 - 1, by the target's width          [8.6.4]
(parse [u32] "42")                ; returns (Result.Ok [u32] [ParseError] value = 42)            [8.6.5]
(parse [u32] "forty-two")         ; returns a Result.Error holding a ParseError                  [8.6.5]
(to_string 42)                    ; returns "42"                                                 [8.6.6]
(to_string excalibur)             ; returns excalibur as text; the format is open (11.7.4)       [8.6.6]
(as [i64] [u32] n)                ; returns 7 as an i64; both type arguments given               [8.6.1]
(as n)                            ; error: nothing settles To                                    [8.6.1]
(as [u32] n)                      ; returns 7; the identity impl [Widen u32 u32]                 [8.6.7]
; hypothetical: Multiply and Widen are prelude traits (8.2.7, 8.6.8)
(define tenfold = {fn [forall T with [Multiply T] [Widen T u8]] |x : T -> T|
    (* x (as [T] 10_u8))})        ; a constant in a generic body                                 [8.6.7]
(tenfold 7_u8)                    ; returns 70; T is u8, through the identity impl               [8.6.7]
(tenfold 7_i8)                    ; error: i8 has no [Widen i8 u8] impl                          [8.6.7]

;;; 8.7 lists

;; 8.7.1 list
(list <Value T>...) -> [List T]
(list [T] <Value T>...) -> [List T]
; 1. list returns a list of its arguments, in order.
; 2. list takes any number of arguments. It cannot be passed as a value
;    (8.1.4).
; 3. (list [T]) is an empty [List T]. With no arguments, list needs its type
;    argument, since nothing else settles T (7.4.8). With arguments, they
;    settle T, or [T] gives it, as for a generic function (7.4.4).
; 4. list is a special built-in: neither a trait method nor a generic
;    function, since it takes any number of arguments. That may change if
;    variadic functions arrive (11.7.7).

(list 1 2 3)                ; type: [List i32]                                  [8.7.1.1]
(list "a" "b")              ; type: [List string]                               [8.7.1.1]
(list (list 1) (list 2 3))  ; type: [List [List i32]]; two arguments            [8.7.1.2]
(list [u8] 1 2)             ; type: [List u8]; [u8] makes 1 and 2 u8s (5.1.1.2)  [8.7.1.2]
(let empty = (list [u8]))   ; empty : [List u8]                                 [8.7.1.3]
(let none_yet : [List u8] = (list))
    ; error: list has no arguments, so it needs [T]; the annotation does not count (7.4.7)  [8.7.1.3]

;; 8.7.2 get
(get <Value [List T]> <Value u32>) -> [Option T]
; 1. get returns (Option.Some [T] value = x) when x is the element at the
;    index, and Option.None [T] when the index is out of range.
; 2. The index can be any u32 value, not just a literal (compare 3.4.2).
;    get is a plain generic function, not a trait method. Its type is
;    [[forall T] [List T] u32 -> [Option T]]. The index is written as u32
;    there, so an unsuffixed literal index is a u32 (5.1.1.2).

(let numbers : [List i32] = (list 1 2 3))
(get numbers 1)              ; returns (Option.Some [i32] value = 2)        [8.7.2.1]
(get numbers 5)              ; returns Option.None [i32]: 5 is out of range  [8.7.2.1]
(get numbers (+ [u32] 1 1))  ; returns (Option.Some [i32] value = 3)        [8.7.2.2]
(get numbers (+ 1 1))        ; error: (+ 1 1) is an i32, not a u32           [8.7.2.2]
get                          ; type: [[forall T] [List T] u32 -> [Option T]]  [8.7.2.2]
; hypothetical: len is not defined yet (11.7.5)
(len numbers)  ; returns 3

;;; 8.8 boxes
(box <Value T>) -> [Box T]
(unbox <Value [Box T]>) -> T
; 1. box puts a value on the heap and returns a [Box T] that refers to it
;    (3.7, 9.2.5). Its type is [[forall T] T -> [Box T]].
; 2. unbox returns the value inside a box. Its type is
;    [[forall T] [Box T] -> T]. Anything in the value that is on the heap,
;    such as a list or another box, is shared, not copied.
; 3. Both are plain generic functions, not trait methods, so their type
;    arguments may be left out when the argument settles T (7.4.4). box and
;    unbox are built-in names, so no program can take them (4.1.7).
;    why: a trait over the box type could not name the type inside the box
;    without associated types (7.5.1.3). With two parameters, the inner
;    type would appear only in the output, so every call would have to
;    give it (7.4.8)
; 4. A pattern can also read through a box (6.6).
; 5. Copying a box, by binding it, passing it or copying a field with
;    ..source (4.4.4), copies the pointer. Both copies share the value
;    inside. This cannot be observed (9.2.3).
; 6. A box's identity cannot be observed: no operation tells whether two
;    boxes are the same allocation. == on boxes compares their contents.
;    A compiler may skip the comparison when both boxes are the same
;    allocation only when T's == is reflexive (8.3.5): a [Box f64] that
;    holds NaN is still not equal to itself.
; What to_string and debug show for a box is open (11.7.13).

(let b = (box 5_u8))         ; b : [Box u8]                                     [8.8.1]
(unbox b)                    ; returns 5                                        [8.8.2]
(box [u64] 5)                ; type: [Box u64]; [u64] makes the 5 a u64         [8.8.3]
(+ b 1_u8)                   ; error: T is [Box u8] from b but u8 from 1_u8     [8.8.2]
(+ (unbox b) 1_u8)           ; returns 6                                        [8.8.2]
(let c = b)                  ; c and b share one allocation, unobservably       [8.8.5]
(== b (box 5_u8))            ; returns true: the contents are equal             [8.8.6]
(let leaf = (box Tree.Leaf [u8]))  ; with Tree from 3.7; leaf : [Box [Tree u8]]  [8.8.1]

;;; 8.9 output and failure
(print <Value string>) -> Unit
(panic <Value string>?) -> Never
(assert <Value bool> <Value string>?) -> Unit
(debug <Value>) -> Unit
; All four are special forms (8.1.2).
; 1. print writes its string to the console, then a newline. It only takes
;    a string; convert other values with to_string (8.6).
; 2. panic stops the program with its message, which defaults to "panic". It
;    never returns.
; 3. assert does nothing when its condition is true. When it is false, it
;    panics with its message, which defaults to "assertion failed".
; 4. debug writes a value of any type to the console. The compiler handles
;    it specially.
; 5. debug is an anti-pattern outside of debugging.
;    why: it can result in nonsense being printed

(print "hello")                 ; prints: hello                         [8.9.1]
(print 42)                      ; error: print takes a string           [8.9.1]
(print (to_string 42))          ; prints: 42                            [8.9.1]
(panic)                         ; panics: panic                         [8.9.2]
(panic "out of fuel")           ; panics: out of fuel                   [8.9.2]
(assert (== 1 1))               ; returns ()                            [8.9.3]
(assert (== 1 2))               ; panics: assertion failed              [8.9.3]
(assert (== 1 2) "math broke")  ; panics: math broke                    [8.9.3]
(debug excalibur)               ; prints excalibur in a debugging form  [8.9.4]
(debug (list 1 2))              ; any type works                        [8.9.4]
; as a whole program:
(define main = {fn |-> Unit| (debug "left in by mistake")})
    ; compiles, but debug is not meant to stay in a program  [8.9.5]

;;;; 9 programs

;;; 9.1 main
(define main = {fn |-> Unit| <Value Unit>})
; 1. Every program defines main, a function of type [-> Unit]. Running the
;    program calls main.
; Most examples in this file leave main out (0.3.1). Exit codes are open
; (11.8.2).

(define main = {fn |-> Unit| ()})  ; the smallest program  [9.1.1]
; a different program:
(define main = {fn |-> Unit| (print "hello world!")})  ; prints: hello world!  [9.1.1]

;;; 9.2 memory
; 1. Memory is managed by reference counting, which programs never see.
;    why: memory management is not a priority
; 2. A value stays alive while anything refers to it. A function value
;    keeps alive the values its body uses (5.4.1.4), even after the block
;    that bound them has ended.
; 3. Values are acyclic. A value can only refer to values that existed
;    before it, since nothing is ever mutated through a shared reference
;    (4.1.1). So reference counting never leaks, and every value is
;    finite. It also means sharing cannot be observed: two copies of a
;    box, a list or a function that share memory behave exactly as two
;    separate values would.
; 4. Two things that look like cycles are not. A named fn's name is
;    supplied at each call, not stored in the function value (5.4.3.6).
;    And a function does not keep a top-level name alive: it refers to it
;    directly, since top-level values live for the whole program
;    (5.4.1.4). So top-level functions that call each other form no
;    counted cycle.
; 5. box (8.8) makes one heap allocation that holds a T, counted like
;    everything else. A [Box T] is one pointer in size, whatever T is.
;    Copies of a box share the allocation, and it is freed when nothing
;    refers to the box any more, with no code to free it. Running out of
;    memory panics (8.9), as for List and the big types (3.1.8).
; 6. When a box is not used again, a new box built from its contents is
;    written into its allocation instead of a new one, as the big types
;    reuse an operand's buffer (3.1.8). So (box (f (unbox b))), with b not
;    used afterwards, allocates nothing.
;    why: updating one node of a tree, the common case for immutable
;    recursive data, then takes no allocation at each step. Nothing can
;    observe it: the only code that refers to the value is the code
;    replacing it

(define is_even = {fn |n : u32 -> bool| (if (== n 0_u32) true (is_odd (- n 1_u32)))})
(define is_odd = {fn |n : u32 -> bool| (if (== n 0_u32) false (is_even (- n 1_u32)))})
    ; each refers to the other directly, so there is no counted cycle  [9.2.4]
; with Tree from 3.7
(define bump_leaf = {fn |b : [Box [Tree u8]] -> [Box [Tree u8]]|
    (match b
        (box (Tree.Node left = l value = v right = r)) =>
            (box (Tree.Node [u8] left = l value = (+ v 1_u8) right = r))
        (box ^Tree.Leaf)                               => b)})
    ; when the caller does not use b again, the new box reuses b's allocation  [9.2.6]
; as a whole program:
(define main = {fn |-> Unit| (do
    (do
        (let big : [List i32] = (list 1 2 3))
        ())                                ; big is freed here, with no code to free it   [9.2.1]
    (let add_ten : [u32 -> u32] = {do
        (let ten : u32 = 10)
        {fn |y : u32 -> u32| (+ y ten)}})  ; the fn keeps ten alive after its block ends  [9.2.2]
    (print (to_string (add_ten 5))))})     ; prints: 15                                   [9.2.2]

;;; 9.3 modules and files
; 1. Each .tsc file is exactly one module.
; 2. A module's path is its file path from the project root, without .tsc,
;    with each / written as ::. src/geometry/shapes.tsc has the path
;    geometry::shapes. Where the project root is, is open (11.8.7).
; 3. A directory is only a namespace. It is not a module, and has no file of
;    its own. Having both geometry.tsc and a geometry/ directory is an error.
; 4. Every segment of a path is snake_case (1.4). Breaking this is a
;    warning (lint naming, 4.7.3).
; 5. The file that defines main (9.1) is the entry module. It sits at the
;    project root. No other module may define main.

; src/geometry/shapes.tsc  has the path geometry::shapes              [9.3.2]
; src/geometry.tsc         error: geometry/ is already a directory    [9.3.3]
; src/Geometry/shapes.tsc  warning: path segments are snake_case      [9.3.4]

;;; 9.4 mod
(mod <Path>) -> Unit
(mod <Suffix> = <Path>) -> Unit
<Path>    ; <Identifier>, or <Path>::<Identifier>: a module's path (9.3.2)
<Suffix>  ; one or more trailing segments of the <Path> on the right of =
; 1. mod may only appear at the top level. Like define, its place in the file
;    does not matter (4.1.2).
; 2. A path always starts at the project root. There are no relative paths.
; 3. (mod a::b::c) binds the alias c, the path's last segment. It is the
;    same as (mod c = a::b::c).
; 4. (mod b::c = a::b::c) binds the alias b::c instead. The left side must
;    be a suffix of the path: its last segments, in order. Any other name is
;    an error. There are no free aliases.
; 5. Writing the default alias out, as in (mod c = a::b::c), is a warning
;    (lint explicit_default_alias, 4.7.3).
; 6. Every item the module exports (9.6) is reachable as alias::item in the
;    whole file. Nothing is brought in unqualified. A trait's methods are
;    reached the same way, as alias::method (7.5.2.7), except inside an
;    impl, whose entries name methods bare (7.5.3.2).
; 7. An alias is not a value. It can only appear on the left of ::.
; 8. Two mod forms that bind the same alias are an error, and so is
;    importing the same path twice.
;    why?: an alias always ends in the module's real name, so searching for
;    c::item finds every use, and searching for a::b::c finds every importer
;    why?: the default is the last segment, not the last two, because
;    collisions are rare, and internal (9.5.1) would make many two-segment
;    aliases read internal::x
; 9. An alias may not have the name of a define, struct, enum, trait or
;    method of the same module, or a built-in name (4.1.7). A local may
;    hide an alias, with a warning (4.1.8; lint shadowing).
; 10. Impls are not imported by name, and need no mod of their own. A
;     module finds impls in itself and in every module it can reach by
;     following mod forms, internal modules included (7.5.6.4).

; as a whole program, in src/main.tsc:
(mod geometry::shapes::circle)                   ; binds the alias circle     [9.4.3]
(mod shapes::square = geometry::shapes::square)  ; binds shapes::square       [9.4.4]
(mod ui::circle = ui::circle)                    ; binds ui::circle, the whole path  [9.4.4]
(define main = {fn |-> Unit| (do
    (let c : circle::Circle = (circle::Circle r = 2.0))
    (print (to_string (circle::area c)))                ; prints: 12.56  [9.4.6]
    (print (to_string (shapes::square::area 3.0)))      ; prints: 9.0    [9.4.6]
    (ui::circle::draw c))})                             ; a different module's draw

(mod ui::circle)                       ; error: circle is already bound           [9.4.8]
(mod round = geometry::shapes::circle)   ; error: round is not a suffix of the path  [9.4.4]
(mod circle = geometry::shapes::circle)  ; warning: circle is already the default    [9.4.5]
(mod shapes)                           ; error: no module has the path shapes     [9.4.2]
(define f = circle)                    ; error: an alias is not a value           [9.4.7]
(circle::area)                         ; error: area needs its argument c         [9.4.6]
(define circle = 1.0)                  ; error: circle is already an alias       [9.4.9]
(do (let circle = 1.0) circle)         ; returns 1.0; warning: circle is also an alias  [9.4.9]

;;; 9.5 visibility of modules
; 1. A module whose path contains the segment internal can only be imported
;    by modules whose path starts with the segments before internal.
;    geometry::internal::common can be imported from geometry::shapes, but
;    not from main or from render::draw.
; 2. If a path contains internal more than once, rule 1 applies to each one.
; 3. A module cannot import a module in its own directory (a sibling). Code
;    that siblings share goes in an internal directory below them, or in
;    another public directory.
; 4. An import cycle, where a module reaches itself by following mod forms,
;    is an error. The error names every module in the cycle.
; 5. Because of 3, the entry module cannot import other files at the project
;    root. Every module except the entry module lives in a directory.
;    why?: dependencies point down or across, never sideways within one
;    directory, and a directory's internal modules stay private to it

; in src/geometry/shapes.tsc:
(mod geometry::internal::common)  ; ok: shapes is inside geometry/  [9.5.1]
(mod geometry::vectors)           ; error: vectors is a sibling     [9.5.3]
; in src/main.tsc:
(mod geometry::internal::common)  ; error: common is internal to geometry/  [9.5.1]
(mod geometry::shapes)            ; ok                                      [9.5.3]
(mod config)                      ; error: config is a sibling at the root  [9.5.5]
; in src/render/draw.tsc:
(mod geometry::shapes)            ; ok: public, and not a sibling  [9.5.3]
(mod geometry::a::internal::b::internal::c)
    ; error: only modules under geometry/a/internal/b/ may import c  [9.5.2]
; if src/geometry/shapes.tsc also held (mod render::draw):
    ; error: cycle render::draw -> geometry::shapes -> render::draw  [9.5.4]

;;; 9.6 export
(export <Identifier> <Identifier>...) -> Unit
; 1. export may only appear at the top level, anywhere in the file, any
;    number of times.
; 2. Each identifier must name a define, struct, enum or trait of this
;    module.
; 3. Exporting a struct exports its type, constructor and fields. Exporting
;    an enum exports its type and all of its variants. Exporting a trait
;    exports all of its methods. A method cannot be named in an export
;    form, and a trait cannot be exported without its methods.
;    why: an impl outside the module must supply every required method, so
;    it has to be able to name all of them
; 4. A name that is not exported is private. Reaching it through :: is an
;    error.
; 5. Exporting a name twice, in one form or across forms, is an error.
; 6. Private in public: an exported item may not mention a private type or
;    trait anywhere in its public surface. That covers an exported
;    define's type, an exported struct's field types, an exported enum's
;    variant fields, the bounds in an exported generic's forall list, and
;    an exported trait's method types and supertraits. Anything private
;    never escapes its module.
; 7. An impl has no visibility of its own, and is never exported. It is
;    usable wherever its trait and every type in its header are usable,
;    and it is found through the search scope (7.5.6.4). So:
;    - an impl of an exported trait for exported types is usable wherever
;      both are;
;    - an impl that mentions a private type or a private trait is usable
;      only in its own module, the only place that type or trait can
;      appear (rule 6);
;    - an impl in the trait's module for built-in types, or for another
;      module's types, is usable wherever the trait is.

; in src/geometry/shapes/circle.tsc:
(struct Circle |r : f64|)
(enum Shape
    Round
    Oval |stretch : f64|)
(define area = {fn |c : Circle -> f64| (* 3.14 (* c.r c.r))})
(define helper = {fn |x : f64 -> f64| x})
(export Circle Shape)  ; two names in one form                   [9.6.1]
(export area)          ; a second export form                    [9.6.1]
(export area)          ; error: area is already exported         [9.6.5]
(export total)         ; error: total is not defined here        [9.6.2]
(let local : u32 = 1)
(export local)         ; error: only a define, struct, enum or trait  [9.6.2]
(trait [Curved T] bend : [T -> f64])
(impl [Curved Circle]
    bend = {fn |c : Circle -> f64| (/ 1.0 c.r)})
(export Curved)        ; exports Curved and its method bend      [9.6.3]
(export bend)          ; error: bend is a method; export its trait, Curved  [9.6.3]
(struct Yards |n : f64|)                               ; not exported
(trait [Secret T] reveal : [T -> f64])                 ; not exported
(define to_yards = {fn |c : Circle -> Yards| (Yards n = c.r)})
(define reveal_all = {fn [forall T with [Secret T]] |x : T -> f64| (reveal x)})
(export to_yards)      ; error: private type Yards in the public signature of to_yards  [9.6.6]
(export reveal_all)    ; error: private trait Secret in a public bound of reveal_all    [9.6.6]
(impl [Secret Circle]
    reveal = {fn |c : Circle -> f64| c.r})
    ; allowed: a private trait for an exported type; the impl is usable only here  [9.6.7]
; in src/main.tsc:
(mod geometry::shapes::circle)
(circle::area (circle::Circle r = 2.0))  ; returns 12.56                [9.6.3]
circle::Shape.Round                      ; type: circle::Shape          [9.6.3]
(circle::helper 1.0)                     ; error: helper is not exported  [9.6.4]
(circle::bend (circle::Circle r = 2.0))  ; returns 0.5; Circle's impl comes with the trait and the type  [9.6.7]
(circle::reveal (circle::Circle r = 2.0))  ; error: reveal is not exported  [9.6.4]

;;; 9.7 sugar
; 1. All syntactic sugar, such as for (5.10) and fancy strings (5.1.2), is
;    built in. Programs cannot define their own yet; macros are planned
;    (11.8.4).

(for |i : u32 = 0| (< i 1_u32) (recur i = (+ i 1_u32)) (print "once"))
    ; built-in sugar for a loop (5.10.2); prints: once  [9.7.1]

;;;; 10 example programs
; Each entry is one complete program. Tags point at the rules each part
; relies on.

;;; 10.1 fizzbuzz
;: Returns the fizzbuzz word for a number.
;: param n: the number to name
;: returns: "FizzBuzz" when n is a multiple of 15, "Fizz" when it is a
;: multiple of 3, "Buzz" when it is a multiple of 5, and n as text otherwise
;: example:
;:   (fizzbuzz 9)  ; returns "Fizz"
;: example:
;:   (fizzbuzz 7)  ; returns "7"
(define fizzbuzz = {fn |n : u32 -> string|
    (match n
        k if (== (% k 15_u32) 0_u32) => "FizzBuzz"        ; k is not bound yet, so it binds n      [6.2.1]
        k if (== (% k 3_u32) 0_u32)  => "Fizz"            ; arms are tried in order                [5.7.2.1]
        k if (== (% k 5_u32) 0_u32)  => "Buzz"            ; k is a u32, so the literals need u32   [5.1.1.3]
        k                            => (to_string k))})  ; the unguarded arm makes it exhaustive  [5.7.2.3]
;: Prints fizzbuzz for 1 to 15.
;: returns: ()
;: example:
;:   (main)  ; prints: 1, 2, Fizz and so on to FizzBuzz, one per line
(define main = {fn |-> Unit|
    (for |i : u32 = 1| (<= i 15_u32) (recur i = (+ i 1_u32))
        (print (fizzbuzz i)))})  ; fizzbuzz is defined above, but could be below  [4.1.2]
                                 ; prints: 1, 2, Fizz, 4, Buzz, Fizz, 7, 8, Fizz, Buzz,
                                 ;         11, Fizz, 13, 14, FizzBuzz, one per line

;;; 10.2 summing a list
;: Returns the sum of a list of numbers.
;: param xs: the numbers to add
;: returns: the total of xs, or 0 when xs is empty
;: example:
;:   (sum (list 3 4 5))  ; returns 12
(define sum = {fn |xs : [List i32] -> i32|
    (loop |i : u32 = 0 total : i32 = 0 -> i32|
        (match (get xs i)
            (Option.Some value = x) => (recur
                i = (+ i 1_u32)
                total = (+ total x))              ; in tail position, so a jump  [5.9.7]
            ^Option.None            => total))})  ; past the end of xs           [8.7.2.1]
;: Prints the sums of two lists.
;: returns: ()
;: example:
;:   (main)  ; prints: 12, then 0, one per line
(define main = {fn |-> Unit| (do
    (print (to_string (sum (list 3 4 5))))     ; prints: 12
    (print (to_string (sum (list [i32])))))})  ; prints: 0; an empty list needs [i32]  [8.7.1.3]

;;; 10.3 shapes
;: A point on the grid.
;: field x: the column, counted from the left
;: field y: the row, counted from the top
(struct Point |x : i32 y : i32|)
;: A shape on the grid.
;: variant Square: a square
;: field Square.side: the length of each side
;: field Square.corner: the top-left corner
;: variant Rect: a rectangle
;: field Rect.width: the length across
;: field Rect.height: the length down
;: field Rect.corner: the top-left corner
;: variant Dot: a single point
;: field Dot.at: where the point is
(enum Shape
    Square |side : i32 corner : Point|
    Rect   |width : i32 height : i32 corner : Point|
    Dot    |at : Point|)
;: Returns the area of a shape.
;: param shape: the shape to measure
;: returns: the number of grid squares the shape covers, or 0 for a dot
;: example:
;:   (area (Shape.Rect width = 2 height = 3 corner = (Point x = 0 y = 0)))  ; returns 6
(define area = {fn |shape : Shape -> i32|
    (match shape
        (Shape.Square side = s ..)           => (* s s)  ; .. skips corner               [6.4.4]
        (Shape.Rect width = w height = h ..) => (* w h)
        (Shape.Dot at = _)                   => 0)})  ; one arm per variant: exhaustive  [5.7.2.2]
;: Returns the name of a shape's variant.
;: param shape: the shape to name
;: returns: "square", "rect" or "dot"
;: example:
;:   (name_of (Shape.Dot at = (Point x = 0 y = 0)))  ; returns "dot"
(define name_of = {fn |shape : Shape -> string|
    (match shape
        (Shape.Square ..) => "square"
        (Shape.Rect ..)   => "rect"
        (Shape.Dot ..)    => "dot")})
;: Returns a phrase that describes a shape.
;: param shape: the shape to describe
;: returns: the shape's name and area, as in "rect with area 6"
;: example:
;:   (describe (Shape.Dot at = (Point x = 0 y = 0)))  ; returns "dot with area 0"
(define describe = {fn |shape : Shape -> string|
    (`${kind} with area ${size}`
        (name_of shape)
        (to_string (area shape)))})  ; kind first, then size  [5.1.2.1]
;: Prints a description of three shapes.
;: returns: ()
;: example:
;:   (main)  ; prints: rect with area 6, square with area 16, dot with area 0, one per line
(define main = {fn |-> Unit| (do
    (let origin : Point = (Point x = 0 y = 0))
    (let corner : Point = (Point ..origin x = 5))  ; y is copied from origin  [4.4.4.2]
    (let rect : Shape = (Shape.Rect width = 2 height = 3 corner = corner))
    (print (describe rect))                        ; prints: rect with area 6
    (print (describe (Shape.Square side = 4 corner = origin)))
                                                   ; prints: square with area 16
    (print (describe (Shape.Dot at = origin))))})  ; prints: dot with area 0

;;; 10.4 parsing with Result
;: Reads an age from text.
;: param text: the text to read
;: returns: the age, as an Ok
;: errors: when text is not a number, the error is "not a number: " and the
;: text; when the number is over 150, it is "too old"
;: example:
;:   (parse_age "42")   ; returns (Result.Ok [u32] [string] value = 42)
;: example:
;:   (parse_age "200")  ; returns (Result.Error [u32] [string] error = "too old")
(define parse_age = {fn |text : string -> [Result u32 string]|
    (match (parse [u32] text)
        (Result.Ok value = n) if (> n 150_u32) => (Result.Error [u32] [string] error = "too old")
        (Result.Ok value = n)                  => (Result.Ok [u32] [string] value = n)
        (Result.Error error = _)               =>
            (Result.Error [u32] [string] error = (`not a number: ${t}` text)))})
    ; patterns take no type arguments; built values do  [7.4.6]
; The second arm rebuilds the Ok. Returning the matched value itself would
; not compile: it is a [Result u32 ParseError], not a [Result u32 string].
;: Returns a line that reports the age read from text.
;: param text: the text to read
;: returns: "age " and the age, or "error: " and why no age was read
;: example:
;:   (report "abc")  ; returns "error: not a number: abc"
(define report = {fn |text : string -> string|
    (match (parse_age text)
        (Result.Ok value = n)    => (`age ${n}` (to_string n))
        (Result.Error error = e) => (`error: ${e}` e))})
;: Prints the reports for three texts.
;: returns: ()
;: example:
;:   (main)  ; prints: age 42, error: too old, error: not a number: abc, one per line
(define main = {fn |-> Unit| (do
    (print (report "42"))      ; prints: age 42
    (print (report "200"))     ; prints: error: too old
    (print (report "abc")))})  ; prints: error: not a number: abc

;;; 10.5 functions as values
;: Returns a function that adds a fixed number.
;: param x: the number the new function adds
;: returns: a function that returns its input plus x
;: example:
;:   ({make_adder 10} 5)  ; returns 15
(define make_adder = {fn |x : u32 -> [u32 -> u32]| {fn |y : u32 -> u32| (+ x y)}})
;: Returns a function that applies one function, then another.
;: type A: the type f takes
;: type B: the type f returns and g takes
;: type C: the type g returns
;: param f: the function applied first
;: param g: the function applied to f's result
;: returns: a function that passes its input to f, then f's result to g
;: panics: when f or g panics
;: example:
;:   ({compose [u32] [u32] [u32] {make_adder 1} {make_adder 2}} 0)  ; returns 3
(define compose = {fn [forall A B C] |f : [A -> B] g : [B -> C] -> [A -> C]|
    {fn |x : A -> C| (g (f x))}})  ; ( ) twice: B and C are type parameters  [7.3.4]
;: Combines the items of a list into one value, from first to last.
;: type T: the type of the items
;: type U: the type of the combined value
;: param xs: the items to combine
;: param init: the value to start from
;: param step: combines the value so far with the next item
;: returns: init, after step has combined it with every item of xs in turn;
;: init itself when xs is empty
;: panics: when step panics
;: example:
;:   (fold [i32] [i32] (list 1 2 3) 0 + [i32])  ; returns 6
(define fold = {fn [forall T U] |xs : [List T] init : U step : [U T -> U] -> U|
    (loop |i : u32 = 0 acc : U = init -> U|
        (match (get xs i)
            (Option.Some value = x) => (recur i = (+ i 1_u32) acc = (step acc x))
            ^Option.None            => acc))})
;: Prints the results of four folds and one composed function.
;: returns: ()
;: example:
;:   (main)  ; prints: 10, 24, 4, abc, 12, one per line
(define main = {fn |-> Unit| (do
    (let numbers : [List i32] = (list 1 2 3 4))
    (print (to_string (fold [i32] [i32] numbers 0 + [i32])))    ; prints: 10               [7.4.2]
    (print (to_string (fold [i32] [i32] numbers 1 * [i32])))    ; prints: 24
    (print (to_string (fold [i32] [i32] numbers 0 max [i32])))  ; prints: 4
    (print (fold [string] [string] (list "a" "b" "c") "" + [string]))
                                                    ; + as string concatenation; prints: abc  [8.1.3]
    (let inc_then_double = {compose [u32] [u32] [u32]
        {make_adder 1}
        {fn |n : u32 -> u32| (* n 2_u32)}})         ; inc_then_double : [u32 -> u32]          [4.3.2]
    (print (to_string (inc_then_double 5))))})      ; prints: 12                              [5.4.1.4]

;;;; 11 open questions
; Each item is something this file does not settle yet. (old todo) marks an
; item from the previous todo list.

;;; 11.1 lexical
; 1. Settled: what a doc comment attaches to and what it must say is in
;    4.6. Still open:
;    - Can a module or file have a doc of its own, and where is it written?
;    - Is the text of a doc plain, or Markdown? Can it link to other names?
;    - Can a let-bound fn, or a fn passed as an argument, have a doc?
;    - Can a doc example be marked compile-only, for code that should not
;      run, such as one that reads input?
;    - Should a field or variant with nothing to add to its name be allowed
;      a shorter doc, or none?
; 2. When do doc examples run (4.6.9.4)? In a test command, on every build,
;    or only when asked? What does a failing example report?

;;; 11.2 grammar
; Nothing open. 11.2.1, where each keyword may appear, is settled by
; 2.5.1.3, 4.1.3, 4.1.10, 4.3.3, 4.4.1.4 and 4.5.1.5.

;;; 11.3 types
; 1. Big number storage. Settled: ubig and ibig grow a
;    doubling buffer (3.1.8). Still open:
;    - When does a ubig or ibig buffer shrink after a large value becomes
;      small: never, when it falls below a quarter full, or only when the
;      value is moved?
;    - Should a value small enough for one word skip the heap? It saves an
;      allocation for most values, at the cost of a second layout.
; 2. Which types implement the bitwise trait, Bits (8.5.3)? Today the rule
;    says "stack types" (8.5.1). Do floats, bool and char count? Do ubig
;    and ibig? The list of impls settles it. If they do, an ibig could act
;    as infinite two's complement, so (bit_not 0_ibig) is -1, but
;    (bit_not 0_ubig) would have infinitely many 1 bits.
; 3. Can chars be compared with <? (char is not numerical: 1.3.3.4.)
; 4. Settled: Never fits wherever another type is expected (3.2.4).
; 5. What is ParseError?
; 6. How is a [Map K V] built, read and matched? (todo)
; 7. Type aliases. (old todo) Settled ahead of them: an alias whose
;    expansion reaches itself, directly or through other aliases, is an
;    error at its declaration, and the error names every alias in the
;    cycle, as an import cycle's does (9.5.4). A recursive structure must
;    be a struct or an enum, never an alias.
; 8. Settled: a type may refer to itself, as long as it never does so at a
;    bigger type (7.2.5) and passes through a Box or other indirection
;    (3.7.2).
; 9. Is a struct with no fields allowed, such as (struct Empty)? Is its value
;    written Empty or (Empty)?
; 10. (speculative) Should types take numbers as well as types, as in a
;     fixed-length [Array T N]? It would allow sizes in user types and
;     integers of any fixed width, such as [u 24]. Nothing needs it yet.
; 11. Should list indices, and len once it exists, be usize instead of u32
;     (3.4.1.2, 8.7.2)? A u32 index caps a list at 2^32 elements; a usize
;     index matches the memory the list can fill.
; 12. Size types (3.1.10):
;     - Should a size type literal be checked against the target's width
;       instead of 32 bits, so 5000000000_usize compiles for 64-bit targets?
;     - Are 16-bit targets supported? That would lower the floor to 16 bits.
;     - Should there be a way to ask for the target's width in code?
; 13. Should a function value keep its defaults in its type when it is
;     passed around? Today a function type does not record them (3.3.8),
;     and = _ is for a trait's required methods only (7.5.2.8).

;;; 11.4 declarations
; 1. What happens with a cycle among defines, such as (define A : u32 = B)
;    with (define B : u32 = A)?
; 2. May a define's value have side effects, such as a print? When do they
;    run?
; 3. In (Employee ..source ...), must ..source come first? May it appear
;    more than once? May the source be any value, or only an identifier?
; 4. Can a constructor such as Employee or Option.Some be passed as a
;    function value, given that it takes its arguments by name?
; 5. Settled: there are no overloads, built in or a program's own. A
;    program gives its types an operator by writing an impl of the
;    operator's trait (7.5.3, 8.1.6).

;;; 11.5 expressions
; 1. In what order are a call's arguments evaluated?
; 2. Can an unsuffixed integer literal take a float type from where it is
;    used, as in (let x : f64 = 5)? A float suffix already allows it, as in
;    5_f64 (1.3.1.7). If so, (let r : f64 = (if true 5 2.5)) would compile
;    while (if true 5 2.5) does not (5.5.3).
; 3. What happens on overflow in +, - and *? wrap exists for wraparound on
;    purpose. ibig never overflows. ubig overflows only below 0, as in
;    (- 2_ubig 3_ubig), and wrap cannot help there (8.6.4).
; 4. Does integer / round toward zero or down? What happens on division by
;    zero, for integers and for floats?
; 5. What sign does % give for negative operands?
; 6. What does integer pow do with a negative exponent?
; 7. What does (clamp a b c) return when b > c?
; 8. May a call bind arguments by name, as in (f x = 1), the way recur and
;    constructors do?
; 9. When is a default value evaluated: once, or at each call? (Which
;    parameters it may use is settled in 5.4.2.3.)
; 10. Does an arm that can never apply, such as one after a _ arm, warn?
; 11. Literal handling and arithmetic in general. (old todo)
; 12. Refine the number literal rules (1.3.1, 5.1.1). (todo)
;     - Settled: no. A literal never takes the type of the other arguments,
;       so (+ i 1) with i : u32 is an error (7.4.10). Sugar that allows it
;       may come later (11.7.15).
;     - Should a written type reach further into an expression than the
;       value positions of do, if and match (5.1.1.2)?
;     - Should an unsuffixed integer literal too large for an i64 become an
;       i128 or an ibig instead of an error (5.1.1.3)?

;;; 11.6 patterns
; 1. May the fields of a constructor pattern appear in any order?
; 2. A pin compares by value (6.8.1). What if the pinned name is bound to a
;    function, which == cannot compare (8.3.2)? Is that a compile error?

;;; 11.7 generics and built-ins
; 1. Settled: a generic function requires an operation on a type parameter
;    with a bound (7.5.4). (old todo: traits for generics)
; 2. Is == structural on structs, enums and lists? What about a struct with
;    a function field? In terms of impls: which types get an impl of =='s
;    trait (8.3.5) without writing one. This is the automatic impls of
;    item 9.
; 3. Which pairs have a Widen impl (8.6.7)? Is i32 to f64 wider? i64 to
;    f64? u32 to i64? What does try_as do with a float that has a fraction,
;    and how are floats turned into integers? (Settled: each family widens
;    by size, usize and isize sit between the 32 and 64-bit types, and ibig
;    is wider than every u type and ubig, 8.6.2.) Facts
;    for the rest: f64 holds every i32 exactly, f128 every i64 and u64, and
;    f256 every i128 and u128, but not every i256.
; 4. What text does to_string produce for floats, bools, structs, enums and
;    lists? What does debug print? In terms of impls: what ToString's
;    impls for the built-in types produce, and what an automatic impl
;    (item 9) produces for a struct or enum.
; 5. Which list operations exist: len, push, map, filter, fold? (len is used
;    hypothetically in 8.7.2.) Does get work on strings or maps? On a
;    string, do len and get count UTF-8 bytes or chars (1.3.4.5)? Counting
;    chars makes both take time proportional to the string's length.
; 6. May a generic function be passed without its type arguments, so that
;    the function receiving it gives them? Its parameter would need a type
;    such as [[forall T] T -> T]. Today every use needs them (7.4.8).
; 7. Are variadic functions allowed? Will the useer be allowed to define their own?
; 8. The prelude's traits. (todo) Section 7.5 defines how traits work. The
;    built-in traits themselves, and their impls for the built-in types,
;    are not designed yet (8.1.3). Open:
;    - Arithmetic (8.2): one Num trait, or one trait for each operator
;      (Add, Subtract, Multiply, Divide, Remainder, Pow, Abs, Neg, Zero,
;      One)? Separate traits let a type support + without /. One trait is
;      simpler to bound. neg gets a trait of its own either way, since
;      unsigned types lack it (8.2.2). If Num is a trait with supertraits
;      and no methods (7.5.5.6), do types opt in, or does one blanket impl
;      cover every type with the operators?
;    - + on strings (8.2.3): string implements Add, so generic numeric code
;      accepts strings and + is not commutative; or joining gets a name of
;      its own. One Num trait forces the second.
;    - Do min, max and clamp move to Ord, as auto-implementations? They
;      work on anything ordered, but are ill-defined with NaN.
;    - Where do zero and one live, and do types other than the numerical
;      ones get them?
;    - pow's exponent: the base's type, as today, or a u32 (11.5.6)? Is abs
;      implemented for unsigned types, as the identity?
;    - Overflow (11.5.3) and integer / (8.2.4) differ by impl. Each trait's
;      doc must say what every impl has to do.
;    - Comparison (8.3.5): confirm the four traits, their names, and which
;      methods sit where. Does != exist, as an auto-implementation?
;      Do string, char and bool get Ord (11.3.3)? Should floats get a
;      total-order comparison, as a function or a wrapper type, so they
;      can be sorted? When is a signaling NaN detected: in every build, and
;      on loading one or only on using it?
;    - Bitwise (8.5.3): one Bits trait, or one for the logic operations and
;      one for the shifts? Which types implement it (11.3.2)?
;    - Casts (8.6.8): are the Widen pairs written out or generated by a
;      rule? Which pairs do TryAs and Wrap have? May a program add Widen
;      impls for its own types, or does as stay limited to primitive types
;      (3.1.3)? The orphan rule (7.5.6.1) already limits them to its own
;      types.
;    - Lists (8.7): do len and the other list operations (item 5) become
;      plain generics, like get? contains and the like would need a bound.
;    - Which traits do Unit, Never, bool, char, string, [List T],
;      [Option T], [Result T E] and [Map K V] implement? Does a Map's key
;      need Eq, or a hash trait?
;    - Should debug require a trait, or keep working on every type (8.9.4)?
; 9. Automatic impls. (todo) == works on every type but functions (8.3.2)
;    and to_string on every type (8.6.6), so something has to give a
;    program's structs and enums those impls. Settled: an automatic impl
;    takes one bound for each type parameter of the type, not one for each
;    field: the automatic impl for [Tree T] is bounded by T alone. (A bound
;    for each field would need [Eq [Box [Tree T]]], which leads back to
;    [Eq [Tree T]] and repeats, 7.5.7.3.) Open:
;    - Are they always there, or does a type opt in with a derive-like
;      form?
;    - What do they do: structural == on fields and variants, and which
;      to_string format (items 2 and 4)? What does a struct with a float
;      field get: PartialEq only?
;    - Can a hand-written impl replace an automatic one? Under the overlap
;      rule (7.5.6.3), only if they are opt-in.
;    - A struct with a function field gets no impl of =='s trait. Is that
;      silent, or a warning?
;    - What if the automatic impl's body does not check against its
;      bounds, as when a field's own impl needs more than the bound gives,
;      or a field is a function? Skip the impl silently, make it an error
;      at the type, or bound the fields that lie outside the type's
;      recursive group (7.2.5)?
;    - A parameter that is only a tag, as in (struct [Id T] |raw : u64|),
;      still gets a bound it does not need.
; 10. The wording of the new errors and warnings:
;     - the latent-overlap warnings (7.5.8.2); the layout there is a sketch
;     - a missing supertrait impl (7.5.5.3): name only the direct
;       supertrait, or the whole chain from the bound that needed it?
;     - the expanding-cycle error (7.2.5, 7.3.8): where it points, and
;       whether it lists the whole cycle of parameters
;     - "contains itself without a Box" (3.7.2): should it list the path of
;       fields that leads back to the type?
;     - a repeated goal (7.5.7.3). Leaning: the full chain, each step
;       naming the impl whose bound led to it, with a hint to bound the
;       type parameters instead (7.5.7.5). At the concrete types of the
;       use, or at an abstract T?
;     - a bound that grows (7.5.7.1): the bound and the header, with their
;       sizes?
; 11. One impl over a function type covers one number of parameters
;     (7.5.3.8), so a trait meant for every function needs an impl for
;     each. Variadic generics (item 7) would be the general fix.
; 12. Output-only trait parameters. Without associated types (7.5.1.3), a
;     parameter that appears only in a method's output is settled only by
;     a type argument (7.4.8). That is fine for the casts. It would make
;     an operator over mixed types, such as [Add L R Out], need [Out] on
;     every call. Leaning: operators stay [T T -> T].
; 13. What do to_string and debug show for a box (8.8): the box, as
;     (box ...), so that the text reads like the code that builds the
;     value, or only its contents?
; 14. (later) Should a repeated goal succeed when the cycle follows the
;     structure of a type (7.5.7.3)? That would let an automatic impl
;     (item 9) take a bound for each field, and let hand-written bounds
;     name types that refer to each other. It only turns errors into valid
;     programs, so it can be done at any time. It needs these rules with
;     it: only a cycle whose every step goes from a type to one of its own
;     components succeeds (a field type, or the parameter of an
;     indirection type, 3.7.4); a supertrait is proved without assuming
;     the impl's own header; a bound equal to its own header is an error;
;     and there are still no associated types. Without them, an impl could
;     vouch for itself, and a supertrait could be implied with no impl
;     behind it. That the first of these rules is enough is an argument,
;     not a proof.
; 15. (later) Sugar. Today everything the arguments cannot settle is
;     written (7.4.7, 7.4.10). Each item below would only shorten that. It
;     should only turn errors into valid programs, and never change what a
;     valid program means:
;     - a written type settles a type parameter, as in
;       (let e : [List u8] = (list)); this needs the rule of 7.4.11 and a
;       rule for when the arguments settle the parameter too
;     - a literal takes the type the other arguments settle, as in (+ x 1)
;       with x : u32 (11.5.12)
;     - a bare 0 or 1 at a type parameter means (zero [T]) or (one [T]),
;       and other bare literals are allowed under a Widen bound (8.6.7)
;     - a shorter way to write a bound on one type parameter (7.5.4.1)
;     - holes in type arguments, as in (as [_] [u32] x), so that a later
;       parameter can be given alone (7.4.4)
;     Not on this list: (- a) as sugar for (neg a), which is ruled out
;     (8.2.2).
; 16. Should a generic constructor or accessor also be allowed to leave out
;     the type arguments that its arguments settle, as a generic function
;     may (7.4.4)? Today (Option.Some value = 5) and (Pair.first p) are
;     errors (3.6.3, 7.2.3, 7.2.4). A variant with no fields, such as
;     Option.None, and a Result.Ok, whose E no argument settles, would
;     still need theirs.
; 17. The indirection list of 3.7.4 is written out. It should become a
;     general rule, such as a marker trait on the types that count, so
;     that a program's own containers can count too. The same definition
;     would serve as the "component" of item 14.
;;; 11.8 programs
; 1. Input and output beyond print, such as reading input or files. (old
;    todo)
; 2. Command-line arguments, and exit codes from main.
; 3. More error management, such as passing a Result.Error along without a
;    match at every step. (old todo)
; 4. Macros, so programs can define their own sugar. (old notes: "macros
;    will come later")
; 5. Is there a standard library, and which of its names are unqualified?
;    (todo) Today every built-in (section 8) and every built-in type
;    (section 3) is unqualified, while 9.4.6 brings nothing from a module in
;    unqualified. Three ways to settle this:
;    a. Everything unqualified. The whole standard library is in scope in
;       every file, as the built-ins are now.
;       pro: nothing to import; short programs stay short
;       pro: matches the current spec, so nothing in sections 3 to 8 changes
;       con: every standard name is taken in every file, and the set grows
;            with each new structure (hash_map, rb_tree, ...)
;       con: a reader cannot tell a standard name from a local one, and
;            searching for a structure's uses does not work as it does
;            for aliases (9.4.8)
;       con: adding a name to the library can break programs that already
;            define it
;    b. Everything qualified. Every standard module is imported with mod,
;       Option included: (mod std::option), then option::Option.Some.
;       pro: one rule for every module, with no exceptions to 9.4.6
;       pro: every dependency, even on the library, is listed at the top
;            of the file
;       con: Option, Result and List are used almost everywhere, so nearly
;            every file starts with the same imports
;       con: core types become long to write: [option::Option T]
;    c. A small unqualified prelude, plus qualified modules. Core names
;       come in automatically. Specialized structures live under std:: and
;       need mod:
;         unqualified:  primitives, Unit, Never, List, Option, Result, the
;                       built-ins of section 8
;         qualified:    (mod std::collections::hash_map), then
;                       hash_map::HashMap; likewise rb_tree, deque, sets,
;                       and I/O beyond print (11.8.1)
;       pro: common code stays short, and specialized structures are
;            visible at the top of each file that uses them
;       pro: the prelude stays fixed and small, so new structures are added
;            under std:: without taking names in every file
;       pro: this is the usual approach (Rust's prelude, Haskell's Prelude)
;       con: two kinds of standard name, and a line to draw between them;
;            the line is a judgment call that has to be defended
;       con: moving a name across the line later breaks programs
;    Settled: c. There is a prelude, and the built-in traits live in it
;    (8.1.3). Their design comes later (11.7.8).
;    Questions that follow from c:
;    - Is [Map K V] (3.5) in the prelude, or does it move to std::?
;    - Settled: a top-level name the prelude already has, such as a
;      program's own Option, is an error, and a local one warns (4.1.7,
;      4.1.8; lint shadowing). The prelude counts as an import. A
;      program's trait cannot take a prelude name for a method (7.5.2.6).
;    - Is std a reserved first segment, so a project cannot have a std/
;      directory? Do 9.5's sibling and internal rules apply inside std?
;    - Is the prelude a real module, std::prelude, that can also be written
;      qualified?
; 6. Can a module re-export items from a module it imports?
; 7. Where is the project root (9.3.2), and how does the compiler find it?
; 8. Linting. Settled: every warning has a lint name, and lint commands
;    silence a lint or raise it to an error, for one item or for one
;    module (4.7). Still open:
;    - The wording of each lint's message. Each should name its lint
;      (4.7.2.5) and, where there is a likely fix, say what it is. (todo)
;    - Which warnings belong to the compiler, and which to a separate
;      linter?
;    - Can a lint be set for a whole project?

;;; 11.9 hypothetical examples
; Examples marked hypothetical:, and what each one assumes.
; 1. 7.5.4: Add is a prelude trait, with + as its method (8.2.7).
; 2. 8.1: Add is a prelude trait that a program's type can implement
;    (8.2.7).
; 3. 8.2: Add and Zero are prelude traits (8.2.7), in two examples.
; 4. 8.3: (/ 0.0 0.0) produces NaN (11.5.4).
; 5. 8.6: Multiply and Widen are prelude traits (8.2.7, 8.6.8).
; 6. 8.7.2: len exists (11.7.5).
; Every example that calls an operator or another built-in function of
; section 8 also assumes the prelude's traits (0.3.5). Those are not
; marked, and not listed here.